./protocol/screening_prompt_templates/scope_reviewer_prompt.txt:51:- A paper that presents a new multimodal model or framework for generating natural-language descriptions from biological data, or generating biological outputs conditioned on natural-language/text-derived evidence, should not be labeled `application_wrapper` solely because it uses a pretrained LLM component.
./protocol/screening_prompt_regression_cases.csv:78: Gregory Koytiger, Alice M. Walsh, Vaishali Marar, Kayla A. Johnson, Max Highsmith, Alexander R. Abbas, Andrew Stirn, Ariel Brumbaugh, Alex David, Darren Hui, Jeffrey Kahn, Sheng-Yong Niu, Liza J. Ray, Candace Savonen, Stein Setvik, Jeffrey T. Leek, Robert K. Bradley. Generative genomics accurately predicts cancer gene expression [abstract]. In: Proceedings of the American Association for Cancer Research Annual Meeting 2026; Part 1 (Regular Abstracts); 2026 Apr 17-22; San Diego, CA. Philadelphia (PA): AACR; Cancer Res 2026;86(7 Suppl):Abstract nr 4213.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,EXCLUDE,,,"GEM-1 is a generative genomics model trained on RNA-seq data and uses pretrained LLM embeddings from experimental metadata to condition predictions, establishing a substantive text-bio bridge. | The abstract explicitly describes GEM-1 as a generative genomics framework using a deep latent variable model trained on large-scale transcriptomic data, with evidence of broad pretraining and transferable representations.","The abstract describes GEM-1, a newly developed generative model for predicting gene expression from experimental metadata, making it a primary model paper. Text component is absent in the model's output, but the text-bio bridge is present via metadata harmonization and pretrained embeddings. Scope reviewer's 'application_wrapper' classification is overridden as the paper presents an original model, not a wrapper."
./protocol/screening_prompt_regression_cases.csv:84:1969,Cell2Text: Multimodal LLM for Generating Single-Cell Descriptions from RNA-Seq Data,"Single-cell RNA sequencing has transformed biology by enabling the measurement of gene expression at cellular resolution, providing information for cell types, states, and disease contexts. Recently, single-cell foundation models have emerged as powerful tools for learning transferable representations directly from expression profiles, improving performance on classification and clustering tasks. However, these models are limited to discrete prediction heads, which collapse cellular complexity into predefined labels that fail to capture the richer, contextual explanations biologists need. We introduce Cell2Text, a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA-seq profiles into structured natural language descriptions. By integrating gene-level embeddings from single-cell foundation models with pretrained large language models, Cell2Text generates coherent summaries that capture cellular identity, tissue origin, disease associations, and pathway activity, generalizing to unseen cells. Empirically, Cell2Text outperforms baselines on classification accuracy, demonstrates strong ontological consistency using PageRank-based similarity metrics, and achieves high semantic fidelity in text generation. These results demonstrate that coupling expression data with natural language offers both stronger predictive performance and inherently interpretable outputs, pointing to a scalable path for label-efficient characterization of unseen cells.",include_decision_unstable,EXCLUDE,INCLUDE,,,"The abstract presents Cell2Text as a framework that integrates existing single-cell foundation models with pretrained LLMs, not as a new foundation model itself, so it is an application wrapper. Bio modality, text component, text-bio bridge, and generative status are clearly present.","Cell2Text is a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA-seq profiles into structured natural language descriptions, integrating gene-level embeddings with a pretrained LLM. This constitutes a primary model paper with a clear text-bio bridge. | The abstract introduces Cell2Text, a generative framework that produces text descriptions from scRNA-seq data, clearly indicating a generative model. However, evidence that Cell2Text itself is a foundation model is unclear as it relies on existing foundation models as components."
./protocol/screening_prompt_regression_cases.csv:111:                This work constitutes an interim report from the initial sprint of our Multi-Modal AI Gym for Science (MMAI), an initiative dedicated to building foundation models for drug discovery and aging research.",include_decision_unstable,EXCLUDE,INCLUDE,,,"The abstract presents Longevity-LLM v0.1 fine-tuned on DNA methylation, proteomics, and other biological data for age prediction and proteomic profile generation, but no natural-language text component is described; the model operates solely on biological modalities. | The abstract introduces Longevity-LLM, a fine-tuned Qwen3-14B model that performs generative tasks such as proteomic profile generation and age prediction, indicating a generative architecture. It also shows foundation-model-like characteristics by leveraging a pretrained LLM and demonstrating broad applicability across modalities.","The abstract describes Longevity-LLM, a fine-tuned Qwen3-14B (foundation LLM) on multiple bio data types, performing generative tasks like age prediction and proteomic profile generation, confirming all criteria."
./protocol/screening_prompt_regression_cases.csv:112:62,TissueNarrator: Generative Modeling of Spatial Transcriptomics with Large Language Models.,"The intricate spatial organization and molecular communication among cells are fundamental to multicellular systems. Spatial transcriptomics (ST) enables gene expression profiling while preserving spatial context, providing rich data for studying cellular interactions and tissue dynamics. However, most existing computational approaches focus on embedding-based tasks and provide limited generative capacity for simulating cell behavior  in situ . Moreover, accurately interpreting spatial interactions requires extensive biological knowledge, which current models do not incorporate. Here, we introduce T  issue N  arrator , a framework that reformulates spatial omics analysis as a language modeling problem. By representing tissue sections as spatial sentences – rank-based gene lists augmented with spatial coordinates and metadata – T  issue N  arrator leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs) to learn spatially conditioned gene expression patterns. The model generates realistic, context-aware cellular profiles, predicts intercellular interactions, and performs  in silico perturbation analyses. Across multiple ST technologies (MERFISH, Perturb-FISH, and CosMx SMI), T  issue N  arrator achieves superior quantitative performance and recovers biologically meaning-ful ligand–receptor and signaling pathways. Furthermore, a conversational inference mode enables natural-language querying of tissue organization. By integrating pretrained biological knowledge with spatial context, T  issue N  arrator establishes a new, scalable generative paradigm for modeling, simulating, and reasoning about tissue systems.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,EXCLUDE,,,"TissueNarrator presents a generative model using LLMs to model spatial transcriptomics data via spatial sentences, with a conversational natural-language interface, clearly meeting all criteria for a primary model paper with both bio modality and text component and a substantive bridge. | Abstract introduces a generative framework using pretrained LLMs for spatial transcriptomics, clearly describing generative capacity. However, evidence that TissueNarrator itself is a foundation model (broad pretraining, transferable representations) is not provided; it leverages existing LLMs.","TissueNarrator leverages pretrained LLMs for generative spatial transcriptomics, consistent with a wrapper-style application rather than a primary model. All criteria are resolved from the abstract."
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/manuscript_draft.md:81:| iter2 wrapper relaxation | 25 | Clarified that use of pretrained LLM components is not automatically a wrapper when the abstract presents a new multimodal text-bio model or generator. | 13 | 8 | 4 | Increased INCLUDE recovery on benchmark-like records without eliminating uncertainty. |
./protocol/screening_prompt_templates/adjudicator_prompt.txt:51:- Do not label a paper `application_wrapper` solely because it uses a pretrained LLM component when the abstract presents a new multimodal model or framework for generating natural-language descriptions from biological data, or for generating biological outputs conditioned on natural-language/text-derived evidence.
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:204:1969,Cell2Text: Multimodal LLM for Generating Single-Cell Descriptions from RNA-Seq Data,"Single-cell RNA sequencing has transformed biology by enabling the measurement of gene expression at cellular resolution, providing information for cell types, states, and disease contexts. Recently, single-cell foundation models have emerged as powerful tools for learning transferable representations directly from expression profiles, improving performance on classification and clustering tasks. However, these models are limited to discrete prediction heads, which collapse cellular complexity into predefined labels that fail to capture the richer, contextual explanations biologists need. We introduce Cell2Text, a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA-seq profiles into structured natural language descriptions. By integrating gene-level embeddings from single-cell foundation models with pretrained large language models, Cell2Text generates coherent summaries that capture cellular identity, tissue origin, disease associations, and pathway activity, generalizing to unseen cells. Empirically, Cell2Text outperforms baselines on classification accuracy, demonstrates strong ontological consistency using PageRank-based similarity metrics, and achieves high semantic fidelity in text generation. These results demonstrate that coupling expression data with natural language offers both stronger predictive performance and inherently interpretable outputs, pointing to a scalable path for label-efficient characterization of unseen cells.",include_decision_unstable,Cell2Text: Multimodal LLM for Generating Single-Cell Descriptions from RNA-Seq Data,"Single-cell RNA sequencing has transformed biology by enabling the measurement of gene expression at cellular resolution, providing information for cell types, states, and disease contexts. Recently, single-cell foundation models have emerged as