nologies (MERFISH, Perturb-FISH, and CosMx SMI), T  issue N  arrator achieves superior quantitative performance and recovers biologically meaning-ful ligand–receptor and signaling pathways. Furthermore, a conversational inference mode enables natural-language querying of tissue organization. By integrating pretrained biological knowledge with spatial context, T  issue N  arrator establishes a new, scalable generative paradigm for modeling, simulating, and reasoning about tissue systems.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,conversational inference mode enables natural-language querying,conversational inference mode enables natural-language querying of tissue organization,"generates realistic, context-aware cellular profiles",clear_include,"Abstract presents TissueNarrator as a new generative framework that uses pretrained LLMs for spatial transcriptomics, with natural-language querying, so it is a primary model paper, not a wrapper.","Presents a new generative framework (TissueNarrator) that uses LLMs and a conversational interface for spatial transcriptomics, clearly including biological modality and text-bio bridge.","The paper introduces a framework that leverages pretrained LLMs to generate cellular profiles from spatial transcriptomics, indicating it is an application wrapper rather than a primary generative text-bio foundation model.","Abstract presents TissueNarrator as a new generative framework that uses pretrained LLMs for spatial transcriptomics, with natural-language querying, so it is a primary model paper, not a wrapper.",TissueNarrator: Generative Modeling of Spatial Transcriptomics with Large Language Models.,"The intricate spatial organization and molecular communication among cells are fundamental to multicellular systems. Spatial transcriptomics (ST) enables gene expression profiling while preserving spatial context, providing rich data for studying cellular interactions and tissue dynamics. However, most existing computational approaches focus on embedding-based tasks and provide limited generative capacity for simulating cell behavior  in situ . Moreover, accurately interpreting spatial interactions requires extensive biological knowledge, which current models do not incorporate. Here, we introduce T  issue N  arrator , a framework that reformulates spatial omics analysis as a language modeling problem. By representing tissue sections as spatial sentences – rank-based gene lists augmented with spatial coordinates and metadata – T  issue N  arrator leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs) to learn spatially conditioned gene expression patterns. The model generates realistic, context-aware cellular profiles, predicts intercellular interactions, and performs  in silico perturbation analyses. Across multiple ST technologies (MERFISH, Perturb-FISH, and CosMx SMI), T  issue N  arrator achieves superior quantitative performance and recovers biologically meaning-ful ligand–receptor and signaling pathways. Furthermore, a conversational inference mode enables natural-language querying of tissue organization. By integrating pretrained biological knowledge with spatial context, T  issue N  arrator establishes a new, scalable generative paradigm for modeling, simulating, and reasoning about tissue systems.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,conversational inference mode enables natural-language querying,natural-language querying of tissue organization; reformulates spatial omics analysis as a language modeling problem,"generates realistic, context-aware cellular profiles",clear_include,"The abstract presents TissueNarrator as a new generative framework that uses pretrained LLMs for spatial transcriptomics, with a conversational natural-language interface, meeting all inclusion criteria; not a wrapper because it reformulates the problem as language modeling and generates cellular profiles.","Presents TissueNarrator, a generative framework using LLMs for spatial transcriptomics, with natural-language querying, satisfying all criteria.","The paper describes TissueNarrator as a framework that leverages pretrained LLMs to generate cellular profiles; it is an application wrapper around existing generative models, not a new foundational generative model.","The abstract presents TissueNarrator as a new generative framework that uses pretrained LLMs for spatial transcriptomics, with a conversational natural-language interface, meeting all inclusion criteria; not a wrapper because it reformulates the problem as language modeling and generates cellular profiles.",TissueNarrator: Generative Modeling of Spatial Transcriptomics with Large Language Models.,"The intricate spatial organization and molecular communication among cells are fundamental to multicellular systems. Spatial transcriptomics (ST) enables gene expression profiling while preserving spatial context, providing rich data for studying cellular interactions and tissue dynamics. However, most existing computational approaches focus on embedding-based tasks and provide limited generative capacity for simulating cell behavior  in situ . Moreover, accurately interpreting spatial interactions requires extensive biological knowledge, which current models do not incorporate. Here, we introduce T  issue N  arrator , a framework that reformulates spatial omics analysis as a language modeling problem. By representing tissue sections as spatial sentences – rank-based gene lists augmented with spatial coordinates and metadata – T  issue N  arrator leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs) to learn spatially conditioned gene expression patterns. The model generates realistic, context-aware cellular profiles, predicts intercellular interactions, and performs  in silico perturbation analyses. Across multiple ST technologies (MERFISH, Perturb-FISH, and CosMx SMI), T  issue N  arrator achieves superior quantitative performance and recovers biologically meaning-ful ligand–receptor and signaling pathways. Furthermore, a conversational inference mode enables natural-language querying of tissue organization. By integrating pretrained biological knowledge with spatial context, T  issue N  arrator establishes a new, scalable generative paradigm for modeling, simulating, and reasoning about tissue systems.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract presents TissueNarrator as a new generative framework that uses LLMs to model spatial transcriptomics and provides natural-language querying, satisfying both text and biological modality criteria. | The abstract introduces TissueNarrator, a framework that reformulates spatial omics as a language modeling problem and explicitly states that the model generates realistic cellular profiles, indicating a generative decoder architecture; it also leverages pretrained large language models, providing foundation‑model evidence.","The abstract presents TissueNarrator as a new generative framework that uses LLMs to model spatial transcriptomics and provides natural-language querying, satisfying both text and biological modality criteria.","The abstract introduces TissueNarrator, a framework that reformulates spatial omics as a language modeling problem and explicitly states that the model generates realistic cellular profiles, indicating a generative decoder architecture; it also leverages pretrained large language models, providing foundation‑model evidence.",,TissueNarrator: Generative Modeling of Spatial Transcriptomics with Large Language Models.,"The intricate spatial organization and molecular communication among cells are fundamental to multicellular systems. Spatial transcriptomics (ST) enables gene expression profiling while preserving spatial context, providing rich data for studying cellular interactions and tissue dynamics. However, most existing computational approaches focus on embedding-based tasks and provide limited generative capacity for simulating cell behavior  in situ . Moreover, accurately interpreting spatial interactions requires extensive biological knowledge, which current models do not incorporate. Here, we introduce T  issue N  arrator , a framework that reformulates spatial omics analysis as a language modeling problem. By representing tissue sections as spatial sentences – rank-based gene lists augmented with spatial coordinates and metadata – T  issue N  arrator leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs) to learn spatially conditioned gene expression patterns. The model generates realistic, context-aware cellular profiles, predicts intercellular interactions, and performs  in silico perturbation analyses. Across multiple ST technologies (MERFISH, Perturb-FISH, and CosMx SMI), T  issue N  arrator achieves superior quantitative performance and recovers biologically meaning-ful ligand–receptor and signaling pathways. Furthermore, a conversational inference mode enables natural-language querying of tissue organization. By integrating pretrained biological knowledge with spatial context, T  issue N  arrator establishes a new, scalable generative paradigm for modeling, simulating, and reasoning about tissue systems.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract introduces TissueNarrator, a new multimodal generative model that treats spatial transcriptomics as language, uses LLMs, and provides natural‑language querying, satisfying all inclusion criteria. | The abstract introduces a new framework that generates realistic cellular profiles using pretrained LLMs, indicating a generative architecture and evidence of broad pretraining.","The abstract introduces TissueNarrator, a new multimodal generative model that treats spatial transcriptomics as language, uses LLMs, and provides natural‑language querying, satisfying all inclusion criteria.","The abstract introduces a new framework that generates realistic cellular profiles using pretrained LLMs, indicating a generative architecture and evidence of broad pretraining.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:585:2246,CellTok: Early-Fusion Multimodal Large Language Model for Single-Cell Transcriptomics via Tokenization,"Single-cell transcriptomic data provide a high-resolution view of cellular states and functions, offering critical insights into development, disease, and tissue heterogeneity. Existing foundation models for single-cell analysis typically embed cells as continuous vectors, limiting their generative flexibility and hindering integration with the knowledge accumulated in large language models (LLMs). In this work, we present CellTok, a multimodal LLM framework for unified analysis of scRNA-seq data and biological text. Each cell is tokenized into discrete codebook tokens via VQ-VAE and integrated into the LLM’s vocabulary using early fusion. This allows CellTok to process biological and textual inputs autoregressively, leveraging pretrained knowledge within LLMs to analyze cellular states and interactions. CellTok demonstrates strong performance in cell-level tasks such as annotation and generation, and enables previously unattainable large population-level tasks, including accurate prediction of intercellular communication networks, which is a key mechanism in tissue organization and disease. Furthermore, it retains interactive Q&A capabilities grounded in biomedical knowledge, offering a new paradigm for analyzing complex cellular systems. Our results suggest that the CellTok framework can extend the capabilities of LLMs to single-cell data, and suggests a direction for building more general mixed-modal models.",stable_include,CellTok: Early-Fusion Multimodal Large Language Model for Single-Cell Transcriptomics via Tokenization,"Single-cell transcriptomic data provide a high-resolution view of cellular states and functions, offering critical insights into development, disease, and tissue heterogeneity. Existing foundation models for single-cell analysis typically embed cells as continuous vectors, limiting their generative flexibility and hindering integration with the knowledge accumulated in large language models (LLMs). In this work, we present CellTok, a multimodal LLM framework for unified analysis of scRNA-seq data and biological text. Each cell is tokenized into discrete codebook tokens via VQ-VAE and integrated into the LLM’s vocabulary using early fusion. This allows CellTok to process biological and textual inputs autoregressively, leveraging pretrained knowledge within LLMs to analyze cellular states and interactions. CellTok demonstrates strong performance in cell-level tasks such as annotation and generation, and enables previously unattainable large population-level tasks, including accurate prediction of intercellular communication networks, which is a key mechanism in tissue organization and disease. Furthermore, it retains interactive Q&A capabilities grounded in biomedical knowledge, offering a new paradigm for analyzing complex cellular systems. Our results suggest that the CellTok framework can extend the capabilities of LLMs to single-cell data, and suggests a direction for building more general mixed-modal models.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,unclear,,,,,"CellTok presents a new multimodal LLM that tokenizes single-cell data and integrates it with biological text via early fusion, enabling autoregressive processing and Q&A. Both bio modality and text component are clearly central, and the text-bio bridge is substantive. | CellTok proposes a VQ-VAE tokenization of single-cell data integrated into an LLM via early fusion, with autoregressive generation and cell-level generative tasks, making it a primary generative model paper with some foundation model characteristics (unclear due to lack of explicit broad pretraining details).","CellTok presents a new multimodal LLM that tokenizes single-cell data and integrates it with biological text via early fusion, enabling autoregressive processing and Q&A. Both bio modality and text component are clearly central, and the text-bio bridge is substantive.","CellTok proposes a VQ-VAE tokenization of single-cell data integrated into an LLM via early fusion, with autoregressive generation and cell-level generative tasks, making it a primary generative model paper with some foundation model characteristics (unclear due to lack of explicit broad pretraining details).",,CellTok: Early-Fusion Multimodal Large Language Model for Single-Cell Transcriptomics via Tokenization,"Single-cell transcriptomic data provide a high-resolution view of cellular states and functions, offering critical insights into development, disease, and tissue heterogeneity. Existing foundation models for single-cell analysis typically embed cells as continuous vectors, limiting their generative flexibility and hindering integration with the knowledge accumulated in large language models (LLMs). In this work, we present CellTok, a multimodal LLM framework for unified analysis of scRNA-seq data and biological text. Each cell is tokenized into discrete codebook tokens via VQ-VAE and integrated into the LLM’s vocabulary using early fusion. This allows CellTok to process biological and textual inputs autoregressively, leveraging pretrained knowledge within LLMs to analyze cellular states and interactions. CellTok demonstrates strong performance in cell-level tasks such as annotation and generation, and enables previously unattainable large population-level tasks, including accurate prediction of intercellular communication networks, which is a key mechanism in tissue organization and disease. Furthermore, it retains interactive Q&A capabilities grounded in biomedical knowledge, offering a new paradigm for analyzing complex cellular systems. Our results suggest that the CellTok framework can extend the capabilities of LLMs to single-cell data, and suggests a direction for building more general mixed-modal models.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"CellTok presents a new multimodal LLM that tokenizes single-cell transcriptomics and integrates with biological text via early fusion, enabling generative cell analysis and Q&A, clearly meeting all criteria. | CellTok presents a multimodal LLM that tokenizes single-cell data via VQ-VAE and processes inputs autoregressively, demonstrating generative capabilities (cell generation, Q&A) and leveraging pretrained LLM knowledge, indicating a primary generative foundation model.","CellTok presents a new multimodal LLM that tokenizes single-cell transcriptomics and integrates with biological text via early fusion, enabling generative cell analysis and Q&A, clearly meeting all criteria.","CellTok presents a multimodal LLM that tokenizes single-cell data via VQ-VAE and processes inputs autoregressively, demonstrating generative capabilities (cell generation, Q&A) and leveraging pretrained LLM knowledge, indicating a primary generative foundation model.",,CellTok: Early-Fusion Multimodal Large Language Model for Single-Cell Transcriptomics via Tokenization,"Single-cell transcriptomic data provide a high-resolution view of cellular states and functions, offering critical insights into development, disease, and tissue heterogeneity. Existing foundation models for single-cell analysis typically embed cells as continuous vectors, limiting their generative flexibility and hindering integration with the knowledge accumulated in large language models (LLMs). In this work, we present CellTok, a multimodal LLM framework for unified analysis of scRNA-seq data and biological text. Each cell is tokenized into discrete codebook tokens via VQ-VAE and integrated into the LLM’s vocabulary using early fusion. This allows CellTok to process biological and textual inputs autoregressively, leveraging pretrained knowledge within LLMs to analyze cellular states and interactions. CellTok demonstrates strong performance in cell-level tasks such as annotation and generation, and enables previously unattainable large population-level tasks, including accurate prediction of intercellular communication networks, which is a key mechanism in tissue organization and disease. Furthermore, it retains interactive Q&A capabilities grounded in biomedical knowledge, offering a new paradigm for analyzing complex cellular systems. Our results suggest that the CellTok framework can extend the capabilities of LLMs to single-cell data, and suggests a direction for building more general mixed-modal models.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,unclear,,,,,"CellTok presents a new multimodal LLM that tokenizes single-cell transcriptomic data and integrates it with biological text via early fusion, enabling unified analysis and interactive Q&A, satisfying all criteria. | CellTok introduces a multimodal LLM framework that tokenizes single-cell data via VQ-VAE and processes autoregressively, enabling cell generation and Q&A, meeting generative model criteria.","CellTok presents a new multimodal LLM that tokenizes single-cell transcriptomic data and integrates it with biological text via early fusion, enabling unified analysis and interactive Q&A, satisfying all criteria.","CellTok introduces a multimodal LLM framework that tokenizes single-cell data via VQ-VAE and processes autoregressively, enabling cell generation and Q&A, meeting generative model criteria.",,CellTok: Early-Fusion Multimodal Large Language Model for Single-Cell Transcriptomics via Tokenization,"Single-cell transcriptomic data provide a high-resolution view of cellular states and functions, offering critical insights into development, disease, and tissue heterogeneity. Existing foundation models for single-cell analysis typically embed cells as continuous vectors, limiting their generative flexibility and hindering integration with the knowledge accumulated in large language models (LLMs). In this work, we present CellTok, a multimodal LLM framework for unified analysis of scRNA-seq data and biological text. Each cell is tokenized into discrete codebook tokens via VQ-VAE and integrated into the LLM’s vocabulary using early fusion. This allows CellTok to process biological and textual inputs autoregressively, leveraging pretrained knowledge within LLMs to analyze cellular states and interactions. CellTok demonstrates strong performance in cell-level tasks such as annotation and generation, and enables previously unattainable large population-level tasks, including accurate prediction of intercellular communication networks, which is a key mechanism in tissue organization and disease. Furthermore, it retains interactive Q&A capabilities grounded in biomedical knowledge, offering a new paradigm for analyzing complex cellular systems. Our results suggest that the CellTok framework can extend the capabilities of LLMs to single-cell data, and suggests a direction for building more general mixed-modal models.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract introduces CellTok, a new multimodal LLM that combines scRNA‑seq tokenization with natural‑language inputs and enables Q&A and generation, indicating a primary model with both biological and text components and a substantive text‑bio bridge. | The abstract presents CellTok as an autoregressive multimodal LLM that tokenizes cells and generates outputs, indicating a generative architecture built on pretrained LLM knowledge.","The abstract introduces CellTok, a new multimodal LLM that combines scRNA‑seq tokenization with natural‑language inputs and enables Q&A and generation, indicating a primary model with both biological and text components and a substantive text‑bio bridge.","The abstract presents CellTok as an autoregressive multimodal LLM that tokenizes cells and generates outputs, indicating a generative architecture built on pretrained LLM knowledge.",,CellTok: Early-Fusion Multimodal Large Language Model for Single-Cell Transcriptomics via Tokenization,"Single-cell transcriptomic data provide a high-resolution view of cellular states and functions, offering critical insights into development, disease, and tissue heterogeneity. Existing foundation models for single-cell analysis typically embed cells as continuous vectors, limiting their generative flexibility and hindering integration with the knowledge accumulated in large language models (LLMs). In this work, we present CellTok, a multimodal LLM framework for unified analysis of scRNA-seq data and biological text. Each cell is tokenized into discrete codebook tokens via VQ-VAE and integrated into the LLM’s vocabulary using early fusion. This allows CellTok to process biological and textual inputs autoregressively, leveraging pretrained knowledge within LLMs to analyze cellular states and interactions. CellTok demonstrates strong performance in cell-level tasks such as annotation and generation, and enables previously unattainable large population-level tasks, including accurate prediction of intercellular communication networks, which is a key mechanism in tissue organization and disease. Furthermore, it retains interactive Q&A capabilities grounded in biomedical knowledge, offering a new paradigm for analyzing complex cellular systems. Our results suggest that the CellTok framework can extend the capabilities of LLMs to single-cell data, and suggests a direction for building more general mixed-modal models.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract introduces CellTok as a new multimodal LLM that jointly handles scRNA‑seq tokenized data and natural‑language text, showing a primary model with both biological and text components and a substantive text‑bio bridge. | The abstract reports an autoregressive multimodal LLM that tokenizes cells and generates outputs, showing a generative architecture built on pretrained LLM knowledge.","The abstract introduces CellTok as a new multimodal LLM that jointly handles scRNA‑seq tokenized data and natural‑language text, showing a primary model with both biological and text components and a substantive text‑bio bridge.","The abstract reports an autoregressive multimodal LLM that tokenizes cells and generates outputs, showing a generative architecture built on pretrained LLM knowledge.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:654:1236,Language may be all omics needs: Harmonizing multimodal data for omics understanding with CellHermes,"Decoding cellular systems requires integrating diverse omics data, yet most models are trained from scratch on a single modality, restricting generalization. Here we present CellHermes, a biological language model that leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs) to integrate multimodal forms of omics data, such as transcriptomic profile and PPI network, through natural language for better omics understanding. By reformulating these datasets into question-answer pairs, CellHermes emulates multiple self-supervised learning paradigms within a single, universal, language-based framework with LORA fine-tuning on existing natural language-LLM, achieving comparable or even better performance compared to current single-cell foundation models. Within this framework, CellHermes functions as an encoder, a predictor and an explainer for supporting a range of downstream tasks. For encoder, CellHermes encodes biological entities with embeddings that enable accurate network discovery and cross-dataset generalization; For predictor, CellHermes unifies heterogeneous downstream tasks by translating them into natural language question-answers pairs, allowing a single model to perform multi-task prediction by instruction fine-tuning; For explainer, CellHermes explains molecular mechanisms by combining attention analysis with text-based reasoning, leveraging the interpretability and reasoning capabilities of LLMs. We evaluated the ability of CellHermes as an  encoder on representing genes and cells using 5 gene-level downstream tasks and 5 diverse single-cell datasets across different tissues compared with other single cell foundation models. We also evaluated CellHermes as a  predictor on BioUniBench, a new benchmark of 10 tasks across 7 databases for LLMs, including perturbation response, cell fitness estimation, gene-disease association, and cell type identification. All benchmarks suggest CellHermes can unify multiple biological tasks within one model without compromising performance. By applying CellHermes as an  explainer for a melanoma patients dataset, it can uncover potential key genes on tumor-reactive T cells. Together, CellHermes establishes natural language as a unifying medium for omics, offering a foundation that, in the future, potentially enables a more integrated and interpretable research loop of biological representation, prediction, and interpretability.",stable_include,Language may be all omics needs: Harmonizing multimodal data for omics understanding with CellHermes,"Decoding cellular systems requires integrating diverse omics data, yet most models are trained from scratch on a single modality, restricting generalization. Here we present CellHermes, a biological language model that leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs) to integrate multimodal forms of omics data, such as transcriptomic profile and PPI network, through natural language for better omics understanding. By reformulating these datasets into question-answer pairs, CellHermes emulates multiple self-supervised learning paradigms within a single, universal, language-based framework with LORA fine-tuning on existing natural language-LLM, achieving comparable or even better performance compared to current single-cell foundation models. Within this framework, CellHermes functions as an encoder, a predictor and an explainer for supporting a range of downstream tasks. For encoder, CellHermes encodes biological entities with embeddings that enable accurate network discovery and cross-dataset generalization; For predictor, CellHermes unifies heterogeneous downstream tasks by translating them into natural language question-answers pairs, allowing a single model to perform multi-task prediction by instruction fine-tuning; For explainer, CellHermes explains molecular mechanisms by combining attention analysis with text-based reasoning, leveraging the interpretability and reasoning capabilities of LLMs. We evaluated the ability of CellHermes as an  encoder on representing genes and cells using 5 gene-level downstream tasks and 5 diverse single-cell datasets across different tissues compared with other single cell foundation models. We also evaluated CellHermes as a  predictor on BioUniBench, a new benchmark of 10 tasks across 7 databases for LLMs, including perturbation response, cell fitness estimation, gene-disease association, and cell type identification. All benchmarks suggest CellHermes can unify multiple biological tasks within one model without compromising performance. By applying CellHermes as an  explainer for a melanoma patients dataset, it can uncover potential key genes on tumor-reactive T cells. Together, CellHermes establishes natural language as a unifying medium for omics, offering a foundation that, in the future, potentially enables a more integrated and interpretable research loop of biological representation, prediction, and interpretability.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"CellHermes is presented as a primary model that uses natural language question-answer pairs to integrate and process omics data, clearly satisfying both text component and text-bio bridge criteria. | CellHermes fine-tunes an existing LLM via LORA on question-answer pairs for multimodal omics tasks, demonstrating generative capabilities and foundation-model-like broad applicability.","CellHermes is presented as a primary model that uses natural language question-answer pairs to integrate and process omics data, clearly satisfying both text component and text-bio bridge criteria.","CellHermes fine-tunes an existing LLM via LORA on question-answer pairs for multimodal omics tasks, demonstrating generative capabilities and foundation-model-like broad applicability.",,Language may be all omics needs: Harmonizing multimodal data for omics understanding with CellHermes,"Decoding cellular systems requires integrating diverse omics data, yet most models are trained from scratch on a single modality, restricting generalization. Here we present CellHermes, a biological language model that leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs) to integrate multimodal forms of omics data, such as transcriptomic profile and PPI network, through natural language for better omics understanding. By reformulating these datasets into question-answer pairs, CellHermes emulates multiple self-supervised learning paradigms within a single, universal, language-based framework with LORA fine-tuning on existing natural language-LLM, achieving comparable or even better performance compared to current single-cell foundation models. Within this framework, CellHermes functions as an encoder, a predictor and an explainer for supporting a range of downstream tasks. For encoder, CellHermes encodes biological entities with embeddings that enable accurate network discovery and cross-dataset generalization; For predictor, CellHermes unifies heterogeneous downstream tasks by translating them into natural language question-answers pairs, allowing a single model to perform multi-task prediction by instruction fine-tuning; For explainer, CellHermes explains molecular mechanisms by combining attention analysis with text-based reasoning, leveraging the interpretability and reasoning capabilities of LLMs. We evaluated the ability of CellHermes as an  encoder on representing genes and cells using 5 gene-level downstream tasks and 5 diverse single-cell datasets across different tissues compared with other single cell foundation models. We also evaluated CellHermes as a  predictor on BioUniBench, a new benchmark of 10 tasks across 7 databases for LLMs, including perturbation response, cell fitness estimation, gene-disease association, and cell type identification. All benchmarks suggest CellHermes can unify multiple biological tasks within one model without compromising performance. By applying CellHermes as an  explainer for a melanoma patients dataset, it can uncover potential key genes on tumor-reactive T cells. Together, CellHermes establishes natural language as a unifying medium for omics, offering a foundation that, in the future, potentially enables a more integrated and interpretable research loop of biological representation, prediction, and interpretability.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"CellHermes is a primary model that integrates omics data via natural language question-answer pairs and text-based reasoning, clearly satisfying all inclusion criteria. | CellHermes fine-tunes a pretrained LLM with LORA on omics question-answer pairs, enabling generative prediction and explanation across multiple tasks, demonstrating foundation-model characteristics.","CellHermes is a primary model that integrates omics data via natural language question-answer pairs and text-based reasoning, clearly satisfying all inclusion criteria.","CellHermes fine-tunes a pretrained LLM with LORA on omics question-answer pairs, enabling generative prediction and explanation across multiple tasks, demonstrating foundation-model characteristics.",,Language may be all omics needs: Harmonizing multimodal data for omics understanding with CellHermes,"Decoding cellular systems requires integrating diverse omics data, yet most models are trained from scratch on a single modality, restricting generalization. Here we present CellHermes, a biological language model that leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs) to integrate multimodal forms of omics data, such as transcriptomic profile and PPI network, through natural language for better omics understanding. By reformulating these datasets into question-answer pairs, CellHermes emulates multiple self-supervised learning paradigms within a single, universal, language-based framework with LORA fine-tuning on existing natural language-LLM, achieving comparable or even better performance compared to current single-cell foundation models. Within this framework, CellHermes functions as an encoder, a predictor and an explainer for supporting a range of downstream tasks. For encoder, CellHermes encodes biological entities with embeddings that enable accurate network discovery and cross-dataset generalization; For predictor, CellHermes unifies heterogeneous downstream tasks by translating them into natural language question-answers pairs, allowing a single model to perform multi-task prediction by instruction fine-tuning; For explainer, CellHermes explains molecular mechanisms by combining attention analysis with text-based reasoning, leveraging the interpretability and reasoning capabilities of LLMs. We evaluated the ability of CellHermes as an  encoder on representing genes and cells using 5 gene-level downstream tasks and 5 diverse single-cell datasets across different tissues compared with other single cell foundation models. We also evaluated CellHermes as a  predictor on BioUniBench, a new benchmark of 10 tasks across 7 databases for LLMs, including perturbation response, cell fitness estimation, gene-disease association, and cell type identification. All benchmarks suggest CellHermes can unify multiple biological tasks within one model without compromising performance. By applying CellHermes as an  explainer for a melanoma patients dataset, it can uncover potential key genes on tumor-reactive T cells. Together, CellHermes establishes natural language as a unifying medium for omics, offering a foundation that, in the future, potentially enables a more integrated and interpretable research loop of biological representation, prediction, and interpretability.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"CellHermes is a primary candidate model that integrates omics data via natural language question-answer pairs, with clear text-bio bridge central to its functions as encoder, predictor, and explainer. | CellHermes fine-tunes an existing LLM via LORA to generate text answers for omics tasks, showing generative capability and foundation-model-like broad applicability across multiple biological tasks.","CellHermes is a primary candidate model that integrates omics data via natural language question-answer pairs, with clear text-bio bridge central to its functions as encoder, predictor, and explainer.","CellHermes fine-tunes an existing LLM via LORA to generate text answers for omics tasks, showing generative capability and foundation-model-like broad applicability across multiple biological tasks.",,Language may be all omics needs: Harmonizing multimodal data for omics understanding with CellHermes,"Decoding cellular systems requires integrating diverse omics data, yet most models are trained from scratch on a single modality, restricting generalization. Here we present CellHermes, a biological language model that leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs) to integrate multimodal forms of omics data, such as transcriptomic profile and PPI network, through natural language for better omics understanding. By reformulating these datasets into question-answer pairs, CellHermes emulates multiple self-supervised learning paradigms within a single, universal, language-based framework with LORA fine-tuning on existing natural language-LLM, achieving comparable or even better performance compared to current single-cell foundation models. Within this framework, CellHermes functions as an encoder, a predictor and an explainer for supporting a range of downstream tasks. For encoder, CellHermes encodes biological entities with embeddings that enable accurate network discovery and cross-dataset generalization; For predictor, CellHermes unifies heterogeneous downstream tasks by translating them into natural language question-answers pairs, allowing a single model to perform multi-task prediction by instruction fine-tuning; For explainer, CellHermes explains molecular mechanisms by combining attention analysis with text-based reasoning, leveraging the interpretability and reasoning capabilities of LLMs. We evaluated the ability of CellHermes as an  encoder on representing genes and cells using 5 gene-level downstream tasks and 5 diverse single-cell datasets across different tissues compared with other single cell foundation models. We also evaluated CellHermes as a  predictor on BioUniBench, a new benchmark of 10 tasks across 7 databases for LLMs, including perturbation response, cell fitness estimation, gene-disease association, and cell type identification. All benchmarks suggest CellHermes can unify multiple biological tasks within one model without compromising performance. By applying CellHermes as an  explainer for a melanoma patients dataset, it can uncover potential key genes on tumor-reactive T cells. Together, CellHermes establishes natural language as a unifying medium for omics, offering a foundation that, in the future, potentially enables a more integrated and interpretable research loop of biological representation, prediction, and interpretability.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,reformulating datasets into natural language question‑answer pairs,integrates multimodal omics data through natural language as a unifying medium,allows a single model to perform multi‑task prediction by generating answers and explanations,clear_include,"The abstract presents CellHermes as a new multimodal language model that leverages pretrained LLMs, uses natural‑language QA pairs, and generates answers/explanations, satisfying all inclusion criteria.","The abstract presents CellHermes as a new multimodal model that encodes omics data via natural‑language question‑answer reformulation, indicating a substantive text component and a central text‑bio bridge.","The abstract describes a model fine‑tuned on LLMs for question‑answer prediction but does not explicitly mention a decoder, autoregressive, or other generative mechanism, so generative status is unclear; however, it clearly leverages pretrained LLMs, indicating foundation‑model characteristics.","The abstract presents CellHermes as a new multimodal language model that leverages pretrained LLMs, uses natural‑language QA pairs, and generates answers/explanations, satisfying all inclusion criteria.",Language may be all omics needs: Harmonizing multimodal data for omics understanding with CellHermes,"Decoding cellular systems requires integrating diverse omics data, yet most models are trained from scratch on a single modality, restricting generalization. Here we present CellHermes, a biological language model that leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs) to integrate multimodal forms of omics data, such as transcriptomic profile and PPI network, through natural language for better omics understanding. By reformulating these datasets into question-answer pairs, CellHermes emulates multiple self-supervised learning paradigms within a single, universal, language-based framework with LORA fine-tuning on existing natural language-LLM, achieving comparable or even better performance compared to current single-cell foundation models. Within this framework, CellHermes functions as an encoder, a predictor and an explainer for supporting a range of downstream tasks. For encoder, CellHermes encodes biological entities with embeddings that enable accurate network discovery and cross-dataset generalization; For predictor, CellHermes unifies heterogeneous downstream tasks by translating them into natural language question-answers pairs, allowing a single model to perform multi-task prediction by instruction fine-tuning; For explainer, CellHermes explains molecular mechanisms by combining attention analysis with text-based reasoning, leveraging the interpretability and reasoning capabilities of LLMs. We evaluated the ability of CellHermes as an  encoder on representing genes and cells using 5 gene-level downstream tasks and 5 diverse single-cell datasets across different tissues compared with other single cell foundation models. We also evaluated CellHermes as a  predictor on BioUniBench, a new benchmark of 10 tasks across 7 databases for LLMs, including perturbation response, cell fitness estimation, gene-disease association, and cell type identification. All benchmarks suggest CellHermes can unify multiple biological tasks within one model without compromising performance. By applying CellHermes as an  explainer for a melanoma patients dataset, it can uncover potential key genes on tumor-reactive T cells. Together, CellHermes establishes natural language as a unifying medium for omics, offering a foundation that, in the future, potentially enables a more integrated and interpretable research loop of biological representation, prediction, and interpretability.",stable_include,EXCLUDE,EC3_not_generative,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,no,yes,,,,,"The abstract presents CellHermes as a new model that reformulates omics data into natural‑language question‑answer pairs and uses a pretrained LLM, providing clear evidence of a substantive text‑bio bridge. | The abstract presents CellHermes as an encoder, predictor, and explainer without mentioning a decoder or any generative mechanism, so it is not a generative model; however, it leverages pretrained LLMs and claims broad applicability, providing foundation‑model evidence.","The abstract presents CellHermes as a new model that reformulates omics data into natural‑language question‑answer pairs and uses a pretrained LLM, providing clear evidence of a substantive text‑bio bridge.","The abstract presents CellHermes as an encoder, predictor, and explainer without mentioning a decoder or any generative mechanism, so it is not a generative model; however, it leverages pretrained LLMs and claims broad applicability, providing foundation‑model evidence.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:656:882,Multimodal learning enables chat-based exploration of single-cell data,"CellWhisperer uses multimodal learning of transcriptomes and text to answer questions about single-cell RNA-sequencing data. Single-cell sequencing characterizes biological samples at unprecedented scale and detail, but data interpretation remains challenging. Here, we present CellWhisperer, an artificial intelligence (AI) model and software tool for chat-based interrogation of gene expression. We establish a multimodal embedding of transcriptomes and their textual annotations, using contrastive learning on 1 million RNA sequencing profiles with AI-curated descriptions. This embedding informs a large language model that answers user-provided questions about cells and genes in natural-language chats. We benchmark CellWhisperer’s performance for zero-shot prediction of cell types and other biological annotations and demonstrate its use for biological discovery in a meta-analysis of human embryonic development. We integrate a CellWhisperer chat box with the CELLxGENE browser, allowing users to interactively explore gene expression through a combined graphical and chat interface. In summary, CellWhisperer leverages large community-scale data repositories to connect transcriptomes and text, thereby enabling interactive exploration of single-cell RNA-sequencing data with natural-language chats.",stable_include,Multimodal learning enables chat-based exploration of single-cell data,"CellWhisperer uses multimodal learning of transcriptomes and text to answer questions about single-cell RNA-sequencing data. Single-cell sequencing characterizes biological samples at unprecedented scale and detail, but data interpretation remains challenging. Here, we present CellWhisperer, an artificial intelligence (AI) model and software tool for chat-based interrogation of gene expression. We establish a multimodal embedding of transcriptomes and their textual annotations, using contrastive learning on 1 million RNA sequencing profiles with AI-curated descriptions. This embedding informs a large language model that answers user-provided questions about cells and genes in natural-language chats. We benchmark CellWhisperer’s performance for zero-shot prediction of cell types and other biological annotations and demonstrate its use for biological discovery in a meta-analysis of human embryonic development. We integrate a CellWhisperer chat box with the CELLxGENE browser, allowing users to interactively explore gene expression through a combined graphical and chat interface. In summary, CellWhisperer leverages large community-scale data repositories to connect transcriptomes and text, thereby enabling interactive exploration of single-cell RNA-sequencing data with natural-language chats.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,"textual annotations, natural-language chats, user-provided questions",multimodal embedding of transcriptomes and their textual annotations using contrastive learning,large language model that answers user-provided questions about cells and genes in natural-language chats,clear_include,"CellWhisperer is presented as a new multimodal model that learns joint embeddings of transcriptomes and text, then uses an LLM for chat-based generation, which is a primary generative model paper, not a wrapper.","The abstract presents CellWhisperer as a new multimodal model that links transcriptomes and natural language for chat-based interrogation, clearly satisfying all criteria.","CellWhisperer uses a pretrained LLM for chat-based interrogation; the main contribution is a multimodal embedding, making it an application wrapper rather than a primary generative model.","CellWhisperer is presented as a new multimodal model that learns joint embeddings of transcriptomes and text, then uses an LLM for chat-based generation, which is a primary generative model paper, not a wrapper.",Multimodal learning enables chat-based exploration of single-cell data,"CellWhisperer uses multimodal learning of transcriptomes and text to answer questions about single-cell RNA-sequencing data. Single-cell sequencing characterizes biological samples at unprecedented scale and detail, but data interpretation remains challenging. Here, we present CellWhisperer, an artificial intelligence (AI) model and software tool for chat-based interrogation of gene expression. We establish a multimodal embedding of transcriptomes and their textual annotations, using contrastive learning on 1 million RNA sequencing profiles with AI-curated descriptions. This embedding informs a large language model that answers user-provided questions about cells and genes in natural-language chats. We benchmark CellWhisperer’s performance for zero-shot prediction of cell types and other biological annotations and demonstrate its use for biological discovery in a meta-analysis of human embryonic development. We integrate a CellWhisperer chat box with the CELLxGENE browser, allowing users to interactively explore gene expression through a combined graphical and chat interface. In summary, CellWhisperer leverages large community-scale data repositories to connect transcriptomes and text, thereby enabling interactive exploration of single-cell RNA-sequencing data with natural-language chats.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,unclear,"natural-language chats, textual annotations, user-provided questions","multimodal embedding of transcriptomes and text, connect transcriptomes and text","large language model that answers user-provided questions, chat-based interrogation",clear_include,"Abstract describes CellWhisperer as a new multimodal AI model that learns joint embeddings of transcriptomes and text to enable chat-based generation of answers, not a wrapper; all decisive criteria are clearly supported.","CellWhisperer is a new AI model that uses multimodal learning to align transcriptomes with natural-language text for chat-based question answering, clearly meeting all criteria for a primary text-bio model.","CellWhisperer uses a pre-existing large language model for chat-based responses; the core contribution is a multimodal embedding and integration, not a new generative foundation model.","Abstract describes CellWhisperer as a new multimodal AI model that learns joint embeddings of transcriptomes and text to enable chat-based generation of answers, not a wrapper; all decisive criteria are clearly supported.",Multimodal learning enables chat-based exploration of single-cell data,"CellWhisperer uses multimodal learning of transcriptomes and text to answer questions about single-cell RNA-sequencing data. Single-cell sequencing characterizes biological samples at unprecedented scale and detail, but data interpretation remains challenging. Here, we present CellWhisperer, an artificial intelligence (AI) model and software tool for chat-based interrogation of gene expression. We establish a multimodal embedding of transcriptomes and their textual annotations, using contrastive learning on 1 million RNA sequencing profiles with AI-curated descriptions. This embedding informs a large language model that answers user-provided questions about cells and genes in natural-language chats. We benchmark CellWhisperer’s performance for zero-shot prediction of cell types and other biological annotations and demonstrate its use for biological discovery in a meta-analysis of human embryonic development. We integrate a CellWhisperer chat box with the CELLxGENE browser, allowing users to interactively explore gene expression through a combined graphical and chat interface. In summary, CellWhisperer leverages large community-scale data repositories to connect transcriptomes and text, thereby enabling interactive exploration of single-cell RNA-sequencing data with natural-language chats.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,"textual annotations, natural-language chats, questions about cells and genes",multimodal embedding of transcriptomes and text via contrastive learning on 1 million RNA sequencing profiles with AI-curated descriptions,answers user-provided questions about cells and genes in natural-language chats,clear_include,"CellWhisperer presents a new multimodal model that learns joint embeddings of transcriptomes and text to enable chat-based interrogation, satisfying all inclusion criteria; it is not a wrapper because it introduces a novel model/framework.","CellWhisperer is a primary model that uses multimodal learning to connect transcriptomes and natural-language text for chat-based exploration, meeting all criteria.","CellWhisperer uses a multimodal embedding to inform a large language model for chat-based interrogation; the paper presents a tool that wraps an existing generative model for single-cell data exploration, not a new generative foundation model.","CellWhisperer presents a new multimodal model that learns joint embeddings of transcriptomes and text to enable chat-based interrogation, satisfying all inclusion criteria; it is not a wrapper because it introduces a novel model/framework.",Multimodal learning enables chat-based exploration of single-cell data,"CellWhisperer uses multimodal learning of transcriptomes and text to answer questions about single-cell RNA-sequencing data. Single-cell sequencing characterizes biological samples at unprecedented scale and detail, but data interpretation remains challenging. Here, we present CellWhisperer, an artificial intelligence (AI) model and software tool for chat-based interrogation of gene expression. We establish a multimodal embedding of transcriptomes and their textual annotations, using contrastive learning on 1 million RNA sequencing profiles with AI-curated descriptions. This embedding informs a large language model that answers user-provided questions about cells and genes in natural-language chats. We benchmark CellWhisperer’s performance for zero-shot prediction of cell types and other biological annotations and demonstrate its use for biological discovery in a meta-analysis of human embryonic development. We integrate a CellWhisperer chat box with the CELLxGENE browser, allowing users to interactively explore gene expression through a combined graphical and chat interface. In summary, CellWhisperer leverages large community-scale data repositories to connect transcriptomes and text, thereby enabling interactive exploration of single-cell RNA-sequencing data with natural-language chats.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract presents CellWhisperer as a new AI model that jointly learns transcriptome data and textual annotations, and uses a large language model to answer natural‑language questions, indicating a primary model with both biological modality and substantive text‑bio bridge. | The abstract presents CellWhisperer, a multimodal model trained on 1 M RNA profiles that leverages a large language model to generate natural‑language answers, indicating a generative architecture with foundation‑model‑like pretraining.","The abstract presents CellWhisperer as a new AI model that jointly learns transcriptome data and textual annotations, and uses a large language model to answer natural‑language questions, indicating a primary model with both biological modality and substantive text‑bio bridge.","The abstract presents CellWhisperer, a multimodal model trained on 1 M RNA profiles that leverages a large language model to generate natural‑language answers, indicating a generative architecture with foundation‑model‑like pretraining.",,Multimodal learning enables chat-based exploration of single-cell data,"CellWhisperer uses multimodal learning of transcriptomes and text to answer questions about single-cell RNA-sequencing data. Single-cell sequencing characterizes biological samples at unprecedented scale and detail, but data interpretation remains challenging. Here, we present CellWhisperer, an artificial intelligence (AI) model and software tool for chat-based interrogation of gene expression. We establish a multimodal embedding of transcriptomes and their textual annotations, using contrastive learning on 1 million RNA sequencing profiles with AI-curated descriptions. This embedding informs a large language model that answers user-provided questions about cells and genes in natural-language chats. We benchmark CellWhisperer’s performance for zero-shot prediction of cell types and other biological annotations and demonstrate its use for biological discovery in a meta-analysis of human embryonic development. We integrate a CellWhisperer chat box with the CELLxGENE browser, allowing users to interactively explore gene expression through a combined graphical and chat interface. In summary, CellWhisperer leverages large community-scale data repositories to connect transcriptomes and text, thereby enabling interactive exploration of single-cell RNA-sequencing data with natural-language chats.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract presents CellWhisperer as a new multimodal AI model that jointly embeds single‑cell RNA‑seq data and text, and uses a large language model for chat‑based interrogation, satisfying the criteria for a primary model with both biological modality and a substantive text‑bio bridge. | The abstract introduces CellWhisperer, a multimodal model that feeds a large language model to generate natural-language answers, showing a generative architecture and broad pretraining on 1 M RNA profiles.","The abstract presents CellWhisperer as a new multimodal AI model that jointly embeds single‑cell RNA‑seq data and text, and uses a large language model for chat‑based interrogation, satisfying the criteria for a primary model with both biological modality and a substantive text‑bio bridge.","The abstract introduces CellWhisperer, a multimodal model that feeds a large language model to generate natural-language answers, showing a generative architecture and broad pretraining on 1 M RNA profiles.",
