./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/prompt_templates/adjudicator_prompt.txt:51:- Do not label a paper `application_wrapper` solely because it uses a pretrained LLM component when the abstract presents a new multimodal model or framework for generating natural-language descriptions from biological data, or for generating biological outputs conditioned on natural-language/text-derived evidence.
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/inputs/screening_prompt_regression_cases.csv:78: Gregory Koytiger, Alice M. Walsh, Vaishali Marar, Kayla A. Johnson, Max Highsmith, Alexander R. Abbas, Andrew Stirn, Ariel Brumbaugh, Alex David, Darren Hui, Jeffrey Kahn, Sheng-Yong Niu, Liza J. Ray, Candace Savonen, Stein Setvik, Jeffrey T. Leek, Robert K. Bradley. Generative genomics accurately predicts cancer gene expression [abstract]. In: Proceedings of the American Association for Cancer Research Annual Meeting 2026; Part 1 (Regular Abstracts); 2026 Apr 17-22; San Diego, CA. Philadelphia (PA): AACR; Cancer Res 2026;86(7 Suppl):Abstract nr 4213.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,EXCLUDE,,,"GEM-1 is a generative genomics model trained on RNA-seq data and uses pretrained LLM embeddings from experimental metadata to condition predictions, establishing a substantive text-bio bridge. | The abstract explicitly describes GEM-1 as a generative genomics framework using a deep latent variable model trained on large-scale transcriptomic data, with evidence of broad pretraining and transferable representations.","The abstract describes GEM-1, a newly developed generative model for predicting gene expression from experimental metadata, making it a primary model paper. Text component is absent in the model's output, but the text-bio bridge is present via metadata harmonization and pretrained embeddings. Scope reviewer's 'application_wrapper' classification is overridden as the paper presents an original model, not a wrapper."
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/inputs/screening_prompt_regression_cases.csv:84:1969,Cell2Text: Multimodal LLM for Generating Single-Cell Descriptions from RNA-Seq Data,"Single-cell RNA sequencing has transformed biology by enabling the measurement of gene expression at cellular resolution, providing information for cell types, states, and disease contexts. Recently, single-cell foundation models have emerged as powerful tools for learning transferable representations directly from expression profiles, improving performance on classification and clustering tasks. However, these models are limited to discrete prediction heads, which collapse cellular complexity into predefined labels that fail to capture the richer, contextual explanations biologists need. We introduce Cell2Text, a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA-seq profiles into structured natural language descriptions. By integrating gene-level embeddings from single-cell foundation models with pretrained large language models, Cell2Text generates coherent summaries that capture cellular identity, tissue origin, disease associations, and pathway activity, generalizing to unseen cells. Empirically, Cell2Text outperforms baselines on classification accuracy, demonstrates strong ontological consistency using PageRank-based similarity metrics, and achieves high semantic fidelity in text generation. These results demonstrate that coupling expression data with natural language offers both stronger predictive performance and inherently interpretable outputs, pointing to a scalable path for label-efficient characterization of unseen cells.",include_decision_unstable,EXCLUDE,INCLUDE,,,"The abstract presents Cell2Text as a framework that integrates existing single-cell foundation models with pretrained LLMs, not as a new foundation model itself, so it is an application wrapper. Bio modality, text component, text-bio bridge, and generative status are clearly present.","Cell2Text is a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA-seq profiles into structured natural language descriptions, integrating gene-level embeddings with a pretrained LLM. This constitutes a primary model paper with a clear text-bio bridge. | The abstract introduces Cell2Text, a generative framework that produces text descriptions from scRNA-seq data, clearly indicating a generative model. However, evidence that Cell2Text itself is a foundation model is unclear as it relies on existing foundation models as components."
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/inputs/screening_prompt_regression_cases.csv:111:                This work constitutes an interim report from the initial sprint of our Multi-Modal AI Gym for Science (MMAI), an initiative dedicated to building foundation models for drug discovery and aging research.",include_decision_unstable,EXCLUDE,INCLUDE,,,"The abstract presents Longevity-LLM v0.1 fine-tuned on DNA methylation, proteomics, and other biological data for age prediction and proteomic profile generation, but no natural-language text component is described; the model operates solely on biological modalities. | The abstract introduces Longevity-LLM, a fine-tuned Qwen3-14B model that performs generative tasks such as proteomic profile generation and age prediction, indicating a generative architecture. It also shows foundation-model-like characteristics by leveraging a pretrained LLM and demonstrating broad applicability across modalities.","The abstract describes Longevity-LLM, a fine-tuned Qwen3-14B (foundation LLM) on multiple bio data types, performing generative tasks like age prediction and proteomic profile generation, confirming all criteria."
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/inputs/screening_prompt_regression_cases.csv:112:62,TissueNarrator: Generative Modeling of Spatial Transcriptomics with Large Language Models.,"The intricate spatial organization and molecular communication among cells are fundamental to multicellular systems. Spatial transcriptomics (ST) enables gene expression profiling while preserving spatial context, providing rich data for studying cellular interactions and tissue dynamics. However, most existing computational approaches focus on embedding-based tasks and provide limited generative capacity for simulating cell behavior  in situ . Moreover, accurately interpreting spatial interactions requires extensive biological knowledge, which current models do not incorporate. Here, we introduce T  issue N  arrator , a framework that reformulates spatial omics analysis as a language modeling problem. By representing tissue sections as spatial sentences – rank-based gene lists augmented with spatial coordinates and metadata – T  issue N  arrator leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs) to learn spatially conditioned gene expression patterns. The model generates realistic, context-aware cellular profiles, predicts intercellular interactions, and performs  in silico perturbation analyses. Across multiple ST technologies (MERFISH, Perturb-FISH, and CosMx SMI), T  issue N  arrator achieves superior quantitative performance and recovers biologically meaning-ful ligand–receptor and signaling pathways. Furthermore, a conversational inference mode enables natural-language querying of tissue organization. By integrating pretrained biological knowledge with spatial context, T  issue N  arrator establishes a new, scalable generative paradigm for modeling, simulating, and reasoning about tissue systems.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,EXCLUDE,,,"TissueNarrator presents a generative model using LLMs to model spatial transcriptomics data via spatial sentences, with a conversational natural-language interface, clearly meeting all criteria for a primary model paper with both bio modality and text component and a substantive bridge. | Abstract introduces a generative framework using pretrained LLMs for spatial transcriptomics, clearly describing generative capacity. However, evidence that TissueNarrator itself is a foundation model (broad pretraining, transferable representations) is not provided; it leverages existing LLMs.","TissueNarrator leverages pretrained LLMs for generative spatial transcriptomics, consistent with a wrapper-style application rather than a primary model. All criteria are resolved from the abstract."
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/inputs/prompt_regression_highsignal_25.csv:26: Gregory Koytiger, Alice M. Walsh, Vaishali Marar, Kayla A. Johnson, Max Highsmith, Alexander R. Abbas, Andrew Stirn, Ariel Brumbaugh, Alex David, Darren Hui, Jeffrey Kahn, Sheng-Yong Niu, Liza J. Ray, Candace Savonen, Stein Setvik, Jeffrey T. Leek, Robert K. Bradley. Generative genomics accurately predicts cancer gene expression [abstract]. In: Proceedings of the American Association for Cancer Research Annual Meeting 2026; Part 1 (Regular Abstracts); 2026 Apr 17-22; San Diego, CA. Philadelphia (PA): AACR; Cancer Res 2026;86(7 Suppl):Abstract nr 4213.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,EXCLUDE,,,"GEM-1 is a generative genomics model trained on RNA-seq data and uses pretrained LLM embeddings from experimental metadata to condition predictions, establishing a substantive text-bio bridge. | The abstract explicitly describes GEM-1 as a generative genomics framework using a deep latent variable model trained on large-scale transcriptomic data, with evidence of broad pretraining and transferable representations.","The abstract describes GEM-1, a newly developed generative model for predicting gene expression from experimental metadata, making it a primary model paper. Text component is absent in the model's output, but the text-bio bridge is present via metadata harmonization and pretrained embeddings. Scope reviewer's 'application_wrapper' classification is overridden as the paper presents an original model, not a wrapper."
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/inputs/prompt_regression_highsignal_25.csv:32:1969,Cell2Text: Multimodal LLM for Generating Single-Cell Descriptions from RNA-Seq Data,"Single-cell RNA sequencing has transformed biology by enabling the measurement of gene expression at cellular resolution, providing information for cell types, states, and disease contexts. Recently, single-cell foundation models have emerged as powerful tools for learning transferable representations directly from expression profiles, improving performance on classification and clustering tasks. However, these models are limited to discrete prediction heads, which collapse cellular complexity into predefined labels that fail to capture the richer, contextual explanations biologists need. We introduce Cell2Text, a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA-seq profiles into structured natural language descriptions. By integrating gene-level embeddings from single-cell foundation models with pretrained large language models, Cell2Text generates coherent summaries that capture cellular identity, tissue origin, disease associations, and pathway activity, generalizing to unseen cells. Empirically, Cell2Text outperforms baselines on classification accuracy, demonstrates strong ontological consistency using PageRank-based similarity metrics, and achieves high semantic fidelity in text generation. These results demonstrate that coupling expression data with natural language offers both stronger predictive performance and inherently interpretable outputs, pointing to a scalable path for label-efficient characterization of unseen cells.",include_decision_unstable,EXCLUDE,INCLUDE,,,"The abstract presents Cell2Text as a framework that integrates existing single-cell foundation models with pretrained LLMs, not as a new foundation model itself, so it is an application wrapper. Bio modality, text component, text-bio bridge, and generative status are clearly present.","Cell2Text is a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA-seq profiles into structured natural language descriptions, integrating gene-level embeddings with a pretrained LLM. This constitutes a primary model paper with a clear text-bio bridge. | The abstract introduces Cell2Text, a generative framework that produces text descriptions from scRNA-seq data, clearly indicating a generative model. However, evidence that Cell2Text itself is a foundation model is unclear as it relies on existing foundation models as components."
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/inputs/all_abstracts_input_4027.csv:737:719,Improving polygenic prediction from whole-genome sequencing data by leveraging predicted epigenomic features,improving polygenic prediction from wholegenome sequencing data by leveraging predicted epigenomic features,10.1073/pnas.2419202122,,,,"Significance Epi-PRS improves polygenic risk scoring by integrating genomic large language models (LLMs) to impute epigenomic signals as intermediaries between genotype and phenotype. This approach enables a more comprehensive assessment of personal variant impacts by incorporating nonlinear models, rare variants, and regulatory mechanisms. By leveraging the power of genomic LLM trained on massive amount of reference epigenomics data, Epi-PRS has demonstrated superior performance over existing PRS methods in predicting genetic risk for breast cancer and diabetes in UK Biobank data. These results highlight the potential of Epi-PRS to improve disease risk modeling and advance the field of precision medicine.",Zeng W.,2025,Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America,2025-06-17,,['scopus'],1,['10.1073/pnas.2419202122'],1,s2_doi,,,
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/inputs/all_abstracts_input_4027.csv:1845:1403,Leveraging genomic large language models to enhance causal genotype-brain-clinical pathways in Alzheimer's disease,leveraging genomic large language models to enhance causal genotypebrainclinical pathways in alzheimers disease,10.1101/2024.10.03.24314824,,,,"Abstract
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/inputs/all_abstracts_input_4027.csv:1846:                Genome-wide association studies (GWAS) have identified numerous Alzheimer’s disease (AD)- associated variants. However, how these variants contribute to the etiology of AD remains largely elusive. Recent advances in genomic large language models (LLMs) offer new opportunities to interpret the genetic variation observed in personal genome. In this study, we propose epiBrainLLM, a novel computational framework that leverages genomic LLM to enhance our understanding of the causal pathways from genotypes to brain measures to AD-related clinical phenotypes. epiBrainLLM will first convert the personal DNA sequence into a diverse set of genomic and epigenomic features using a pretrained genomic LLM and then use these features to further predict phenotypes. Across various experimental settings, epiBrainLLM significantly improves causal analysis compared to traditional genotype association approach. We conclude that epiBrainLLM provides a novel perspective for understanding the regulatory mechanisms underlying the AD disease etiology, potentially offering insights into complex disease mechanisms beyond AD.",Q. Liu; W. Zeng; H. Zhu; L. Li; W. Wong,2024,medRxiv,2024-10-04,,['semantic_scholar'],1,['10.1101/2024.10.03.24314824'],1,crossref,,,
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/inputs/all_abstracts_input_4027.csv:1888:1421,How to improve polygenic prediction from whole-genome sequencing data by leveraging predicted epigenomic features?,how to improve polygenic prediction from wholegenome sequencing data by leveraging predicted epigenomic features,10.1101/2024.10.04.24314860,,,,"Polygenic risk scores (PRS) are crucial in genetics for predicting individual susceptibility to complex diseases by aggregating the effects of numerous genetic variants. Whole-genome sequencing (WGS) has revolutionized our ability to detect rare and even de novo variants, creating an exciting opportunity for developing new PRS methods that can effectively leverage rare variants and capture the complex relationships among different variants. Furthermore, regulatory mechanisms play a crucial role in gene expression and disease manifestation, offering avenues to further enhance the performance and interpretation of PRS predictions. Through simulation studies, we highlighted aspects where current PRS methods face challenges when applied to WGS data, aiming to shed light on potential opportunities for further improvement. To address these challenges, we developed Epi-PRS, an approach that leverages the power of genomic large language models (LLM) to impute epigenomic signals across diverse cellular contexts, for use as intermediate variables between genotype and phenotype. A pretrained LLM is employed to transform genotypes into epigenomic signals using personal diploid sequences as inputs, and the genetic risk is then estimated based on the imputed personal epigenomic signals. Epi-PRS enhances the assessment of personal variant impacts, enabling a comprehensive and holistic consideration of genotypic and regulatory information within large genomic regions. Our simulation results demonstrated that incorporating the nuanced effects of non-linear models, rare variants, and regulatory information can provide more precise PRS prediction and better understanding of genetic risk. Applying Epi-PRS to real data from the UK Biobank, our results further showed that Epi-PRS significantly outperforms existing PRS methods in two major diseases: breast cancer and diabetes. This study suggests that PRS methods can benefit from incorporating non-linear models, rare variants, and regulatory information, highlighting the potential for significant advancements in disease risk modeling and enhancing the understanding of precision medicine. Significance Statement Epi-PRS improves polygenic risk scoring by integrating genomic large language models (LLMs) to impute epigenomic signals as intermediaries between genotype and phenotype. This approach enables a more comprehensive assessment of personal variant impacts by incorporating non-linear models, rare variants, and regulatory mechanisms. By leveraging the power of genomic LLM trained on massive amount of reference epigenomics data, Epi-PRS has demonstrated superior performance over existing PRS methods in predicting genetic risk for breast cancer and diabetes in UK Biobank data. These results highlight the potential of Epi-PRS to improve disease risk modeling and advance the field of precision medicine.",W. Zeng; H. Guo; Q. Liu; W. Wong,2024,medRxiv,2024-10-06,https://www.medrxiv.org/content/medrxiv/early/2024/10/06/2024.10.04.24314860.full.pdf,"['biorxiv_medrxiv', 'semantic_scholar']",2,['10.1101/2024.10.04.24314860'],2,,,,
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/inputs/all_abstracts_input_4027.csv:2257:1565,MetagenBERT: a Transformer-based Architecture using Foundational genomic Large Language Models for novel Metagenome Representation,metagenbert a transformerbased architecture using foundational genomic large language models for novel metagenome representation,,,,2601.03295v1,"Metagenomic disease prediction commonly relies on species abundance tables derived from large, incomplete reference catalogs, constraining resolution and discarding valuable information contained in DNA reads. To overcome these limitations, we introduce MetagenBERT, a Transformer based framework that produces end to end metagenome embeddings directly from raw DNA sequences, without taxonomic or functional annotations. Reads are embedded using foundational genomic language models (DNABERT2 and the microbiome specialized DNABERTMS), then aggregated through a scalable clustering strategy based on FAISS accelerated KMeans. Each metagenome is represented as a cluster abundance vector summarizing the distribution of its embedded reads. We evaluate this approach on five benchmark gut microbiome datasets (Cirrhosis, T2D, Obesity, IBD, CRC). MetagenBERT achieves competitive or superior AUC performance relative to species abundance baselines across most tasks. Concatenating both representations further improves prediction, demonstrating complementarity between taxonomic and embedding derived signals. Clustering remains robust when applied to as little as 10% of reads, highlighting substantial redundancy in metagenomes and enabling major computational gains. We additionally introduce MetagenBERT Glob Mcardis, a cross cohort variant trained on the large, phenotypically diverse MetaCardis cohort and transferred to other datasets, retaining predictive signal including for unseen phenotypes, indicating the feasibility of a foundation model for metagenome representation. Robustness analyses (PERMANOVA, PERMDISP, entropy) show consistent separation of different states across subsamples. Overall, MetagenBERT provides a scalable, annotation free representation of metagenomes pointing toward future phenotype aware generalization across heterogeneous cohorts and sequencing technologies.",Gaspar Roy; Eugeni Belda; Baptiste Hennecart; Y. Chevaleyre; E. Prifti; Jean-Daniel Zucker,2026,,2026-01-05,,"['arxiv', 'semantic_scholar']",2,[],2,,,,
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/inputs/all_abstracts_input_4027.csv:3277:                RNAs play critical roles in gene expression regulation, catalysis, genetic information transmission, and immune response. However, current RNA engineering approaches face limitations in designing RNA for efficient in vivo folding and functional execution. Here, we present a novel, zero-shot strategy that integrates genomic large language models (LLMs) with inverse folding models (IFMs) to generate RNA sequences that mirror natural evolutionary patterns while preserving the structural integrity of critical functional regions. Benchmarking 9 state-of-the-art genomic foundation models, we show that their unsupervised log-likelihood scores correlate strongly with RNA fitness, enabling zero-shot sequence design. Applied to fluorescent RNA aptamers, our approach yielded 20 Broccoli variants, over half of which achieved up to 155% in fluorescence and a twofold increase in binding affinity, with robust performance both in vitro and in vivo. For Pepper, two rounds of optimization produced 40 variants, 16 of which demonstrated up to 2.6 fold in fluorescence and a threefold boost in binding affinity, including five with superior in vivo brightness.This approach offers a scalable, efficient, and resource-light method for RNA design, with significant implications for RNA-based therapeutics and diagnostics. By leveraging general foundation models to facilitate RNA evolution in a zero-shot fashion, we believe our work represents a major advancement in the field of RNA engineering.",Yanjie Huang; Bibi Zhang; Jack X. Chen; Runze Ma; Zhongyue Zhang; Xianjun Chen; Yi Yang; Shuangjia Zheng,2025,bioRxiv,2025-04-09,,['semantic_scholar'],1,['10.1101/2025.04.08.647793'],1,crossref,,,
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/inputs/all_abstracts_input_4027.csv:3915:2381,Advances in Human Genome Resolution: The Role of Pan-Genomic Strategies and Fine-Tuning Pre-trained Genomic Models,advances in human genome resolution the role of pangenomic strategies and finetuning pretrained genomic models,10.18103/mra.v12i7.5571,,,,"The groundbreaking theory of DNA double helix structure has greatly promoted the development of molecular genetics, shaping and refining the genetic central dogma, thus enabling researchers to explore genotype-phenotype regulation at different levels. In particular, with the continued advancement of third-generation sequencing technology, an increasing number of highly accurate human genomes have been assembled, such as T2T-CHM13 and HG002. These high-quality genome sequences not only provide a more comprehensive human reference sequence, but also enable functional genomics studies within a unified coordinate system. To better explore and resolve the complex genetic information encompassed within human genome sequences, scientists have proposed novel research strategies, involving graphical pan-genome and pre-trained genomic models. The graphical pan-genomes provide population- level high-quality references, revealing the genomic diversity within populations and exploring the sequence complexity of specific regions, such as the KIR immune region. Concurrently, related studies of pre-trained models within the human genome offer new perspectives for interpreting sequence functions and delving into the hidden genetic codes, potentially leading to complete DNA decoding. Overall, graphical pan-genome and pre-trained genomic models represent two crucial strategies in genomics research, which will provide more new insights and make greater breakthroughs in the human genome. Together, these approaches have deepened our understanding of the human genome, fostered the development of bioinformatics ecosystems, and will contribute to the establishment and improvement of the entire field. Therefore, this review focuses on DNA sequencing, human genome assembly, high-quality pan-genome and pre-trained genomic large language models (LLMs), highlighting and summarizing the latest achievements and progress in human genome research, discussing existing challenges and providing future perspectives.",Lei Liu,2024,Medical Research Archives,,,['semantic_scholar'],1,['10.18103/mra.v12i7.5571'],1,,,,
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/inputs/all_abstracts_input_4027.csv:4130:2500,GenoCGC: A Hybrid CNN and Graph-based Transformer Model for Genomic Sequence Classification,genocgc a hybrid cnn and graphbased transformer model for genomic sequence classification,10.1109/ICIT64950.2025.11049132,,,,"Advancements in sequencing technologies have resulted in rapid growth in genomic data. This revolutionary technology has enabled conducting large-scale studies in biology and medicine. In bioinformatics, accurately classifying genomic sequences is a necessary step for understanding genetic variations, identifying disease mutations, and advancing precision medicine. However, it remains one of the key challenges in the field. Traditional machine learning approaches often struggle with the complexity of genomic data. However, deep learning approaches, such as transformer-based models, improve analysis by capturing long-range dependencies. We introduce GenoCGC, a hybrid model combining Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) and a Graph-based Transformer (Graphormer) for genomic sequence classification. GenoCGC uses CNNs for capturing local features while utilizing Graphormer to model complex dependencies. We compared GenoCGC with traditional classifiers and genomic large language models across multiple genomic benchmark tasks. Experimental results show that GenoCGC achieves promising performance, providing an accurate and practical solution for genomic sequence analysis.",Anas Abu-Doleh,2025,2025 12th International Conference on Information Technology (ICIT),2025-05-27,,['semantic_scholar'],1,['10.1109/ICIT64950.2025.11049132'],1,,,,
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/inputs/prompt_regression_iter5_unstable3.csv:6: Gregory Koytiger, Alice M. Walsh, Vaishali Marar, Kayla A. Johnson, Max Highsmith, Alexander R. Abbas, Andrew Stirn, Ariel Brumbaugh, Alex David, Darren Hui, Jeffrey Kahn, Sheng-Yong Niu, Liza J. Ray, Candace Savonen, Stein Setvik, Jeffrey T. Leek, Robert K. Bradley. Generative genomics accurately predicts cancer gene expression [abstract]. In: Proceedings of the American Association for Cancer Research Annual Meeting 2026; Part 1 (Regular Abstracts); 2026 Apr 17-22; San Diego, CA. Philadelphia (PA): AACR; Cancer Res 2026;86(7 Suppl):Abstract nr 4213.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,EXCLUDE,,,"GEM-1 is a generative genomics model trained on RNA-seq data and uses pretrained LLM embeddings from experimental metadata to condition predictions, establishing a substantive text-bio bridge. | The abstract explicitly describes GEM-1 as a generative genomics framework using a deep latent variable model trained on large-scale transcriptomic data, with evidence of broad pretraining and transferable representations.","The abstract describes GEM-1, a newly developed generative model for predicting gene expression from experimental metadata, making it a primary model paper. Text component is absent in the model's output, but the text-bio bridge is present via metadata harmonization and pretrained embeddings. Scope reviewer's 'application_wrapper' classification is overridden as the paper presents an original model, not a wrapper."
./analysis/fulltext_uncertain_disagreement_current_llm_20260512/current_6_complete_uncertain_or_disagreement_fulltext_targets.csv:1236:2500,GenoCGC: A Hybrid CNN and Graph-based Transformer Model for Genomic Sequence Classification,10.1109/ICIT64950.2025.11049132,,,,,2025.0,2025 12th International Conference on Information Technology (ICIT),"Advancements in sequencing technologies have resulted in rapid growth in genomic data. This revolutionary technology has enabled conducting large-scale studies in biology and medicine. In bioinformatics, accurately classifying genomic sequences is a necessary step for understanding genetic variations, identifying disease mutations, and advancing precision medicine. However, it remains one of the key challenges in the field. Traditional machine learning approaches often struggle with the complexity of genomic data. However, deep learning approaches, such as transformer-based models, improve analysis by capturing long-range dependencies. We introduce GenoCGC, a hybrid model combining Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) and a Graph-based Transformer (Graphormer) for genomic sequence classification. GenoCGC uses CNNs for capturing local features while utilizing Graphormer to model complex dependencies. We compared GenoCGC with traditional classifiers and genomic large language models across multiple genomic benchmark tasks. Experimental results show that GenoCGC achieves promising performance, providing an accurate and practical solution for genomic sequence analysis.",['semantic_scholar'],EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,UNCERTAIN
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/nemotron3_super_120b_fp8_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11581/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.jsonl:240:{"cluster_id": 3667, "title": "ST-LLM: Spatial Transcriptomics Embedding with Large Language Models", "title_normalized": "stllm spatial transcriptomics embedding with large language models", "doi": "10.1609/aaai.v40i40.40713", "preprint_doi": NaN, "pmid": NaN, "arxiv_id": NaN, "abstract": "Spatial transcriptomics provides unprecedented opportunities to analyze gene patterns while preserving spatial tissue architecture. However, traditional deep learning methods for spatial transcriptomics analysis face significant challenges in multi-modal data integration, spatial dependency modeling, and biological knowledge incorporation, while existing large language models lack explicit spatial modeling capabilities for transcriptomic data. So we first present a Spatial Transcriptomics Embedding with Large Language Models (ST-LLM), a novel simple and effective approach that transforms intricate spatial graph structures into structured textual representations suitable for large language models (LLMs). ST-LLM dynamically constructs graph adjacency construction using reinforcement learning paradigms to adaptively optimize spatial relationships, converts the resulting graphs into hierarchical textual descriptions with spatial context, and leverages pre-trained semantic understanding to generate high-dimensional spatial-aware representations. Comprehensive experiments on 14 datasets demonstrate that ST-LLM achieves comparable or better performance than traditional model. ST-LLM shows that LLMs embeddings provide a new simple and effective path to encoding spatial transcriptomics biological knowledge.", "authors": "Zhetao Xu; Xiaohua Wan; Le Li; Shuang Feng; Yiming Zhang; Fa Zhang; Bin Hu", "year": 2026.0, "venue": "AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence", "date": "2026-03-14", "url": NaN, "sources": "['google_scholar', 'semantic_scholar']", "n_sources": 2, "all_dois": "['10.1609/aaai.v40i40.40713']", "duplicate_count": 2, "abstract_source": NaN, "abstract_enriched_from": NaN, "abstract_old_len": NaN, "update_batch": "2026-03-01_to_2026-04-14", "_pilot_row_id": 202, "round-A_scope_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "application_wrapper", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "yes", "text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "converts the resulting graphs into hierarchical textual descriptions with spatial context", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "boundary_case": "clear_exclude", "decision_rationale": "The abstract describes ST-LLM as converting spatial graphs into textual descriptions to obtain LLM embeddings for downstream spatial transcriptomics analysis, using an external LLM only as a feature extractor, making it an application wrapper rather than a primary text-bio generative model."}, "round-A_scope_reviewer_paper_type": "application_wrapper", "round-A_scope_reviewer_bio_modality_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_component_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "round-A_scope_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "round-A_scope_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "converts the resulting graphs into hierarchical textual descriptions with spatial context", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "round-A_scope_reviewer_boundary_case": "clear_exclude", "round-A_scope_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The abstract describes ST-LLM as converting spatial graphs into textual descriptions to obtain LLM embeddings for downstream spatial transcriptomics analysis, using an external LLM only as a feature extractor, making it an application wrapper rather than a primary text-bio generative model.", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "generative_model_present": "unclear", "foundation_model_evidence": "unclear", "primary_exclusion_code": "none", "uncertainty_reason": "generative_status_unclear", "evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_generative_model": "leverages pre-trained semantic understanding to generate high-dimensional spatial-aware representations", "boundary_case": "generative_boundary", "decision_rationale": "The abstract describes converting graphs to text and using pretrained LLMs to generate representations, but does not specify an explicit decoder, autoregressive, or other generative mechanism."}, "round-A_architecture_reviewer_paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_generative_model_present": "unclear", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_foundation_model_evidence": "unclear", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "generative_status_unclear", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "leverages pre-trained semantic understanding to generate high-dimensional spatial-aware representations", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_boundary_case": "generative_boundary", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The abstract describes converting graphs to text and using pretrained LLMs to generate representations, but does not specify an explicit decoder, autoregressive, or other generative mechanism.", "pilot_roundA_scope_gate": "EXCLUDE", "pilot_roundA_architecture_gate": "UNCERTAIN", "pilot_needs_adjudication": true, "round-B_adjudicator_output": {"paper_type": "application_wrapper", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "yes", "text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "generative_model_present": "unclear", "foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "uncertainty_reason": "generative_status_unclear", "evidence_for_text_component": "transforms intricate spatial graph structures into structured textual representations suitable for large language models (LLMs)", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "boundary_case": "thin_abstract_boundary", "decision_rationale": "The abstract describes converting spatial graphs into textual descriptions and using frozen LLM embeddings for downstream representation, indicating an application wrapper use of LLMs without explicit text-conditioned generative mechanism."}, "round-B_adjudicator_paper_type": "application_wrapper", "round-B_adjudicator_bio_modality_present": "yes", "round-B_adjudicator_text_component_present": "yes", "round-B_adjudicator_text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "round-B_adjudicator_generative_model_present": "unclear", "round-B_adjudicator_foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "round-B_adjudicator_primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "round-B_adjudicator_uncertainty_reason": "generative_status_unclear", "round-B_adjudicator_evidence_for_text_component": "transforms intricate spatial graph structures into structured textual representations suitable for large language models (LLMs)", "round-B_adjudicator_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "round-B_adjudicator_evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "round-B_adjudicator_boundary_case": "thin_abstract_boundary", "round-B_adjudicator_decision_rationale": "The abstract describes converting spatial graphs into textual descriptions and using frozen LLM embeddings for downstream representation, indicating an application wrapper use of LLMs without explicit text-conditioned generative mechanism."}
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/nemotron3_super_120b_fp8_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11581/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.csv:667:3667,ST-LLM: Spatial Transcriptomics Embedding with Large Language Models,stllm spatial transcriptomics embedding with large language models,10.1609/aaai.v40i40.40713,,,,"Spatial transcriptomics provides unprecedented opportunities to analyze gene patterns while preserving spatial tissue architecture. However, traditional deep learning methods for spatial transcriptomics analysis face significant challenges in multi-modal data integration, spatial dependency modeling, and biological knowledge incorporation, while existing large language models lack explicit spatial modeling capabilities for transcriptomic data. So we first present a Spatial Transcriptomics Embedding with Large Language Models (ST-LLM), a novel simple and effective approach that transforms intricate spatial graph structures into structured textual representations suitable for large language models (LLMs). ST-LLM dynamically constructs graph adjacency construction using reinforcement learning paradigms to adaptively optimize spatial relationships, converts the resulting graphs into hierarchical textual descriptions with spatial context, and leverages pre-trained semantic understanding to generate high-dimensional spatial-aware representations. Comprehensive experiments on 14 datasets demonstrate that ST-LLM achieves comparable or better performance than traditional model. ST-LLM shows that LLMs embeddings provide a new simple and effective path to encoding spatial transcriptomics biological knowledge.",Zhetao Xu; Xiaohua Wan; Le Li; Shuang Feng; Yiming Zhang; Fa Zhang; Bin Hu,2026.0,AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence,2026-03-14,,"['google_scholar', 'semantic_scholar']",2,['10.1609/aaai.v40i40.40713'],2,,,,2026-03-01_to_2026-04-14,202,"{""paper_type"": ""application_wrapper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""yes"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""application_wrapper"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""converts the resulting graphs into hierarchical textual descriptions with spatial context"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes ST-LLM as converting spatial graphs into textual descriptions to obtain LLM embeddings for downstream spatial transcriptomics analysis, using an external LLM only as a feature extractor, making it an application wrapper rather than a primary text-bio generative model.""}",application_wrapper,yes,yes,no,application_wrapper,none,converts the resulting graphs into hierarchical textual descriptions with spatial context,none,not_assessed,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes ST-LLM as converting spatial graphs into textual descriptions to obtain LLM embeddings for downstream spatial transcriptomics analysis, using an external LLM only as a feature extractor, making it an application wrapper rather than a primary text-bio generative model.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""unclear"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""unclear"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""generative_status_unclear"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""leverages pre-trained semantic understanding to generate high-dimensional spatial-aware representations"", ""boundary_case"": ""generative_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes converting graphs to text and using pretrained LLMs to generate representations, but does not specify an explicit decoder, autoregressive, or other generative mechanism.""}",primary_model_paper,unclear,unclear,none,generative_status_unclear,not_assessed,not_assessed,leverages pre-trained semantic understanding to generate high-dimensional spatial-aware representations,generative_boundary,"The abstract describes converting graphs to text and using pretrained LLMs to generate representations, but does not specify an explicit decoder, autoregressive, or other generative mechanism.",EXCLUDE,UNCERTAIN,True,"{""paper_type"": ""application_wrapper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""yes"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""generative_model_present"": ""unclear"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""application_wrapper"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""generative_status_unclear"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""transforms intricate spatial graph structures into structured textual representations suitable for large language models (LLMs)"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""thin_abstract_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes converting spatial graphs into textual descriptions and using frozen LLM embeddings for downstream representation, indicating an application wrapper use of LLMs without explicit text-conditioned generative mechanism.""}",application_wrapper,yes,yes,no,unclear,yes,application_wrapper,generative_status_unclear,transforms intricate spatial graph structures into structured textual representations suitable for large language models (LLMs),none,none,thin_abstract_boundary,"The abstract describes converting spatial graphs into textual descriptions and using frozen LLM embeddings for downstream representation, indicating an application wrapper use of LLMs without explicit text-conditioned generative mechanism."
./analysis/fulltext_uncertain_disagreement_current_llm_20260512/current_6_complete_decision_matrix.csv:1986:719,Improving polygenic prediction from whole-genome sequencing data by leveraging predicted epigenomic features,10.1073/pnas.2419202122,,,,,2025.0,Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America,"Significance Epi-PRS improves polygenic risk scoring by integrating genomic large language models (LLMs) to impute epigenomic signals as intermediaries between genotype and phenotype. This approach enables a more comprehensive assessment of personal variant impacts by incorporating nonlinear models, rare variants, and regulatory mechanisms. By leveraging the power of genomic LLM trained on massive amount of reference epigenomics data, Epi-PRS has demonstrated superior performance over existing PRS methods in predicting genetic risk for breast cancer and diabetes in UK Biobank data. These results highlight the potential of Epi-PRS to improve disease risk modeling and advance the field of precision medicine.",['scopus'],EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE
./analysis/fulltext_uncertain_disagreement_current_llm_20260512/current_6_complete_decision_matrix.csv:3082:1403,Leveraging genomic large language models to enhance causal genotype-brain-clinical pathways in Alzheimer's disease,10.1101/2024.10.03.24314824,,,,,2024.0,medRxiv,"Abstract
./analysis/fulltext_uncertain_disagreement_current_llm_20260512/current_6_complete_decision_matrix.csv:3083:                Genome-wide association studies (GWAS) have identified numerous Alzheimer’s disease (AD)- associated variants. However, how these variants contribute to the etiology of AD remains largely elusive. Recent advances in genomic large language models (LLMs) offer new opportunities to interpret the genetic variation observed in personal genome. In this study, we propose epiBrainLLM, a novel computational framework that leverages genomic LLM to enhance our understanding of the causal pathways from genotypes to brain measures to AD-related clinical phenotypes. epiBrainLLM will first convert the personal DNA sequence into a diverse set of genomic and epigenomic features using a pretrained genomic LLM and then use these features to further predict phenotypes. Across various experimental settings, epiBrainLLM significantly improves causal analysis compared to traditional genotype association approach. We conclude that epiBrainLLM provides a novel perspective for understanding the regulatory mechanisms underlying the AD disease etiology, potentially offering insights into complex disease mechanisms beyond AD.",['semantic_scholar'],EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE
./analysis/fulltext_uncertain_disagreement_current_llm_20260512/current_6_complete_decision_matrix.csv:3295:1421,How to improve polygenic prediction from whole-genome sequencing data by leveraging predicted epigenomic features?,10.1101/2024.10.04.24314860,,,,https://www.medrxiv.org/content/medrxiv/early/2024/10/06/2024.10.04.24314860.full.pdf,2024.0,medRxiv,"Polygenic risk scores (PRS) are crucial in genetics for predicting individual susceptibility to complex diseases by aggregating the effects of numerous genetic variants. Whole-genome sequencing (WGS) has revolutionized our ability to detect rare and even de novo variants, creating an exciting opportunity for developing new PRS methods that can effectively leverage rare variants and capture the complex relationships among different variants. Furthermore, regulatory mechanisms play a crucial role in gene expression and disease manifestation, offering avenues to further enhance the performance and interpretation of PRS predictions. Through simulation studies, we highlighted aspects where current PRS methods face challenges when applied to WGS data, aiming to shed light on potential opportunities for further improvement. To address these challenges, we developed Epi-PRS, an approach that leverages the power of genomic large language models (LLM) to impute epigenomic signals across diverse cellular contexts, for use as intermediate variables between genotype and phenotype. A pretrained LLM is employed to transform genotypes into epigenomic signals using personal diploid sequences as inputs, and the genetic risk is then estimated based on the imputed personal epigenomic signals. Epi-PRS enhances the assessment of personal variant impacts, enabling a comprehensive and holistic consideration of genotypic and regulatory information within large genomic regions. Our simulation results demonstrated that incorporating the nuanced effects of non-linear models, rare variants, and regulatory information can provide more precise PRS prediction and better understanding of genetic risk. Applying Epi-PRS to real data from the UK Biobank, our results further showed that Epi-PRS significantly outperforms existing PRS methods in two major diseases: breast cancer and diabetes. This study suggests that PRS methods can benefit from incorporating non-linear models, rare variants, and regulatory information, highlighting the potential for significant advancements in disease risk modeling and enhancing the understanding of precision medicine. Significance Statement Epi-PRS improves polygenic risk scoring by integrating genomic large language models (LLMs) to impute epigenomic signals as intermediaries between genotype and phenotype. This approach enables a more comprehensive assessment of personal variant impacts by incorporating non-linear models, rare variants, and regulatory mechanisms. By leveraging the power of genomic LLM trained on massive amount of reference epigenomics data, Epi-PRS has demonstrated superior performance over existing PRS methods in predicting genetic risk for breast cancer and diabetes in UK Biobank data. These results highlight the potential of Epi-PRS to improve disease risk modeling and advance the field of precision medicine.","['biorxiv_medrxiv', 'semantic_scholar']",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE
./analysis/fulltext_uncertain_disagreement_current_llm_20260512/current_6_complete_decision_matrix.csv:4494:                RNAs play critical roles in gene expression regulation, catalysis, genetic information transmission, and immune response. However, current RNA engineering approaches face limitations in designing RNA for efficient in vivo folding and functional execution. Here, we present a novel, zero-shot strategy that integrates genomic large language models (LLMs) with inverse folding models (IFMs) to generate RNA sequences that mirror natural evolutionary patterns while preserving the structural integrity of critical functional regions. Benchmarking 9 state-of-the-art genomic foundation models, we show that their unsupervised log-likelihood scores correlate strongly with RNA fitness, enabling zero-shot sequence design. Applied to fluorescent RNA aptamers, our approach yielded 20 Broccoli variants, over half of which achieved up to 155% in fluorescence and a twofold increase in binding affinity, with robust performance both in vitro and in vivo. For Pepper, two rounds of optimization produced 40 variants, 16 of which demonstrated up to 2.6 fold in fluorescence and a threefold boost in binding affinity, including five with superior in vivo brightness.This approach offers a scalable, efficient, and resource-light method for RNA design, with significant implications for RNA-based therapeutics and diagnostics. By leveraging general foundation models to facilitate RNA evolution in a zero-shot fashion, we believe our work represents a major advancement in the field of RNA engineering.",['semantic_scholar'],EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE
./analysis/fulltext_uncertain_disagreement_current_llm_20260512/current_6_complete_decision_matrix.csv:5161:2381,Advances in Human Genome Resolution: The Role of Pan-Genomic Strategies and Fine-Tuning Pre-trained Genomic Models,10.18103/mra.v12i7.5571,,,,,2024.0,Medical Research Archives,"The groundbreaking theory of DNA double helix structure has greatly promoted the development of molecular genetics, shaping and refining the genetic central dogma, thus enabling researchers to explore genotype-phenotype regulation at different levels. In particular, with the continued advancement of third-generation sequencing technology, an increasing number of highly accurate human genomes have been assembled, such as T2T-CHM13 and HG002. These high-quality genome sequences not only provide a more comprehensive human reference sequence, but also enable functional genomics studies within a unified coordinate system. To better explore and resolve the complex genetic information encompassed within human genome sequences, scientists have proposed novel research strategies, involving graphical pan-genome and pre-trained genomic models. The graphical pan-genomes provide population- level high-quality references, revealing the genomic diversity within populations and exploring the sequence complexity of specific regions, such as the KIR immune region. Concurrently, related studies of pre-trained models within the human genome offer new perspectives for interpreting sequence functions and delving into the hidden genetic codes, potentially leading to complete DNA decoding. Overall, graphical pan-genome and pre-trained genomic models represent two crucial strategies in genomics research, which will provide more new insights and make greater breakthroughs in the human genome. Together, these approaches have deepened our understanding of the human genome, fostered the development of bioinformatics ecosystems, and will contribute to the establishment and improvement of the entire field. Therefore, this review focuses on DNA sequencing, human genome assembly, high-quality pan-genome and pre-trained genomic large language models (LLMs), highlighting and summarizing the latest achievements and progress in human genome research, discussing existing challenges and providing future perspectives.",['semantic_scholar'],EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE
./analysis/fulltext_uncertain_disagreement_current_llm_20260512/current_6_complete_decision_matrix.csv:5376:2500,GenoCGC: A Hybrid CNN and Graph-based Transformer Model for Genomic Sequence Classification,10.1109/ICIT64950.2025.11049132,,,,,2025.0,2025 12th International Conference on Information Technology (ICIT),"Advancements in sequencing technologies have resulted in rapid growth in genomic data. This revolutionary technology has enabled conducting large-scale studies in biology and medicine. In bioinformatics, accurately classifying genomic sequences is a necessary step for understanding genetic variations, identifying disease mutations, and advancing precision medicine. However, it remains one of the key challenges in the field. Traditional machine learning approaches often struggle with the complexity of genomic data. However, deep learning approaches, such as transformer-based models, improve analysis by capturing long-range dependencies. We introduce GenoCGC, a hybrid model combining Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) and a Graph-based Transformer (Graphormer) for genomic sequence classification. GenoCGC uses CNNs for capturing local features while utilizing Graphormer to model complex dependencies. We compared GenoCGC with traditional classifiers and genomic large language models across multiple genomic benchmark tasks. Experimental results show that GenoCGC achieves promising performance, providing an accurate and practical solution for genomic sequence analysis.",['semantic_scholar'],EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,UNCERTAIN
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/nemotron3_super_120b_fp8_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11581/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:667:3667,ST-LLM: Spatial Transcriptomics Embedding with Large Language Models,stllm spatial transcriptomics embedding with large language models,10.1609/aaai.v40i40.40713,,,,"Spatial transcriptomics provides unprecedented opportunities to analyze gene patterns while preserving spatial tissue architecture. However, traditional deep learning methods for spatial transcriptomics analysis face significant challenges in multi-modal data integration, spatial dependency modeling, and biological knowledge incorporation, while existing large language models lack explicit spatial modeling capabilities for transcriptomic data. So we first present a Spatial Transcriptomics Embedding with Large Language Models (ST-LLM), a novel simple and effective approach that transforms intricate spatial graph structures into structured textual representations suitable for large language models (LLMs). ST-LLM dynamically constructs graph adjacency construction using reinforcement learning paradigms to adaptively optimize spatial relationships, converts the resulting graphs into hierarchical textual descriptions with spatial context, and leverages pre-trained semantic understanding to generate high-dimensional spatial-aware representations. Comprehensive experiments on 14 datasets demonstrate that ST-LLM achieves comparable or better performance than traditional model. ST-LLM shows that LLMs embeddings provide a new simple and effective path to encoding spatial transcriptomics biological knowledge.",Zhetao Xu; Xiaohua Wan; Le Li; Shuang Feng; Yiming Zhang; Fa Zhang; Bin Hu,2026.0,AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence,2026-03-14,,"['google_scholar', 'semantic_scholar']",2,['10.1609/aaai.v40i40.40713'],2,,,,2026-03-01_to_2026-04-14,202,"{""paper_type"": ""application_wrapper"", ""bio_modality_p