not_assessed", "evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "boundary_case": "wrapper_boundary", "decision_rationale": "The abstract presents epiBrainLLM as a framework that leverages a pretrained genomic LLM to generate features and predict phenotypes, without describing its own generative mechanism; therefore it is an application wrapper rather than a primary generative model."}, "round-A_architecture_reviewer_paper_type": "application_wrapper", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_generative_model_present": "no", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_boundary_case": "wrapper_boundary", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The abstract presents epiBrainLLM as a framework that leverages a pretrained genomic LLM to generate features and predict phenotypes, without describing its own generative mechanism; therefore it is an application wrapper rather than a primary generative model.", "pilot_roundA_scope_gate": "EXCLUDE", "pilot_roundA_architecture_gate": "EXCLUDE", "pilot_needs_adjudication": false}
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.jsonl:1323:{"cluster_id": 1421, "title": "How to improve polygenic prediction from whole-genome sequencing data by leveraging predicted epigenomic features?", "title_normalized": "how to improve polygenic prediction from wholegenome sequencing data by leveraging predicted epigenomic features", "doi": "10.1101/2024.10.04.24314860", "preprint_doi": NaN, "pmid": NaN, "arxiv_id": NaN, "abstract": "Polygenic risk scores (PRS) are crucial in genetics for predicting individual susceptibility to complex diseases by aggregating the effects of numerous genetic variants. Whole-genome sequencing (WGS) has revolutionized our ability to detect rare and even de novo variants, creating an exciting opportunity for developing new PRS methods that can effectively leverage rare variants and capture the complex relationships among different variants. Furthermore, regulatory mechanisms play a crucial role in gene expression and disease manifestation, offering avenues to further enhance the performance and interpretation of PRS predictions. Through simulation studies, we highlighted aspects where current PRS methods face challenges when applied to WGS data, aiming to shed light on potential opportunities for further improvement. To address these challenges, we developed Epi-PRS, an approach that leverages the power of genomic large language models (LLM) to impute epigenomic signals across diverse cellular contexts, for use as intermediate variables between genotype and phenotype. A pretrained LLM is employed to transform genotypes into epigenomic signals using personal diploid sequences as inputs, and the genetic risk is then estimated based on the imputed personal epigenomic signals. Epi-PRS enhances the assessment of personal variant impacts, enabling a comprehensive and holistic consideration of genotypic and regulatory information within large genomic regions. Our simulation results demonstrated that incorporating the nuanced effects of non-linear models, rare variants, and regulatory information can provide more precise PRS prediction and better understanding of genetic risk. Applying Epi-PRS to real data from the UK Biobank, our results further showed that Epi-PRS significantly outperforms existing PRS methods in two major diseases: breast cancer and diabetes. This study suggests that PRS methods can benefit from incorporating non-linear models, rare variants, and regulatory information, highlighting the potential for significant advancements in disease risk modeling and enhancing the understanding of precision medicine. Significance Statement Epi-PRS improves polygenic risk scoring by integrating genomic large language models (LLMs) to impute epigenomic signals as intermediaries between genotype and phenotype. This approach enables a more comprehensive assessment of personal variant impacts by incorporating non-linear models, rare variants, and regulatory mechanisms. By leveraging the power of genomic LLM trained on massive amount of reference epigenomics data, Epi-PRS has demonstrated superior performance over existing PRS methods in predicting genetic risk for breast cancer and diabetes in UK Biobank data. These results highlight the potential of Epi-PRS to improve disease risk modeling and advance the field of precision medicine.", "authors": "W. Zeng; H. Guo; Q. Liu; W. Wong", "year": 2024.0, "venue": "medRxiv", "date": "2024-10-06", "url": "https://www.medrxiv.org/content/medrxiv/early/2024/10/06/2024.10.04.24314860.full.pdf", "sources": "['biorxiv_medrxiv', 'semantic_scholar']", "n_sources": 2, "all_dois": "['10.1101/2024.10.04.24314860']", "duplicate_count": 2, "abstract_source": NaN, "abstract_enriched_from": NaN, "abstract_old_len": NaN, "update_batch": NaN, "_pilot_row_id": 1301, "round-A_scope_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "no", "text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "primary_exclusion_code": "EC2_no_text_component", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "none", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "boundary_case": "clear_exclude", "decision_rationale": "The abstract presents a new PRS method using a genomic LLM to impute epigenomic signals, but it contains no mention of natural-language text or a text‑bio mapping, so it lacks a substantive text component and bridge."}, "round-A_scope_reviewer_paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "round-A_scope_reviewer_bio_modality_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_component_present": "no", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "round-A_scope_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "EC2_no_text_component", "round-A_scope_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "round-A_scope_reviewer_boundary_case": "clear_exclude", "round-A_scope_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The abstract presents a new PRS method using a genomic LLM to impute epigenomic signals, but it contains no mention of natural-language text or a text‑bio mapping, so it lacks a substantive text component and bridge.", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "application_wrapper", "generative_model_present": "yes", "foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_generative_model": "A pretrained LLM is employed to transform genotypes into epigenomic signals", "boundary_case": "wrapper_boundary", "decision_rationale": "Epi-PRS uses an existing pretrained genomic LLM to generate epigenomic signals for PRS, representing a wrapper around a generative model rather than a novel primary model."}, "round-A_architecture_reviewer_paper_type": "application_wrapper", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_generative_model_present": "yes", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "A pretrained LLM is employed to transform genotypes into epigenomic signals", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_boundary_case": "wrapper_boundary", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_decision_rationale": "Epi-PRS uses an existing pretrained genomic LLM to generate epigenomic signals for PRS, representing a wrapper around a generative model rather than a novel primary model.", "pilot_roundA_scope_gate": "EXCLUDE", "pilot_roundA_architecture_gate": "EXCLUDE", "pilot_needs_adjudication": true, "round-B_adjudicator_output": {"paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "no", "text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "generative_model_present": "yes", "foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "primary_exclusion_code": "EC2_no_text_component", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "genomic large language models (LLM) to impute epigenomic signals", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "genomic large language models (LLM) to impute epigenomic signals", "evidence_for_generative_model": "impute epigenomic signals", "boundary_case": "clear_exclude", "decision_rationale": "The abstract describes a genomic LLM used to predict epigenomic signals from DNA, with no natural‑language text input or output, so there is no text component or text‑bio bridge; thus the paper is excluded under EC2_no_text_component."}, "round-B_adjudicator_paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "round-B_adjudicator_bio_modality_present": "yes", "round-B_adjudicator_text_component_present": "no", "round-B_adjudicator_text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "round-B_adjudicator_generative_model_present": "yes", "round-B_adjudicator_foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "round-B_adjudicator_primary_exclusion_code": "EC2_no_text_component", "round-B_adjudicator_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-B_adjudicator_evidence_for_text_component": "genomic large language models (LLM) to impute epigenomic signals", "round-B_adjudicator_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "genomic large language models (LLM) to impute epigenomic signals", "round-B_adjudicator_evidence_for_generative_model": "impute epigenomic signals", "round-B_adjudicator_boundary_case": "clear_exclude", "round-B_adjudicator_decision_rationale": "The abstract describes a genomic LLM used to predict epigenomic signals from DNA, with no natural‑language text input or output, so there is no text component or text‑bio bridge; thus the paper is excluded under EC2_no_text_component."}
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.jsonl:1440:{"cluster_id": 1565, "title": "MetagenBERT: a Transformer-based Architecture using Foundational genomic Large Language Models for novel Metagenome Representation", "title_normalized": "metagenbert a transformerbased architecture using foundational genomic large language models for novel metagenome representation", "doi": NaN, "preprint_doi": NaN, "pmid": NaN, "arxiv_id": "2601.03295v1", "abstract": "Metagenomic disease prediction commonly relies on species abundance tables derived from large, incomplete reference catalogs, constraining resolution and discarding valuable information contained in DNA reads. To overcome these limitations, we introduce MetagenBERT, a Transformer based framework that produces end to end metagenome embeddings directly from raw DNA sequences, without taxonomic or functional annotations. Reads are embedded using foundational genomic language models (DNABERT2 and the microbiome specialized DNABERTMS), then aggregated through a scalable clustering strategy based on FAISS accelerated KMeans. Each metagenome is represented as a cluster abundance vector summarizing the distribution of its embedded reads. We evaluate this approach on five benchmark gut microbiome datasets (Cirrhosis, T2D, Obesity, IBD, CRC). MetagenBERT achieves competitive or superior AUC performance relative to species abundance baselines across most tasks. Concatenating both representations further improves prediction, demonstrating complementarity between taxonomic and embedding derived signals. Clustering remains robust when applied to as little as 10% of reads, highlighting substantial redundancy in metagenomes and enabling major computational gains. We additionally introduce MetagenBERT Glob Mcardis, a cross cohort variant trained on the large, phenotypically diverse MetaCardis cohort and transferred to other datasets, retaining predictive signal including for unseen phenotypes, indicating the feasibility of a foundation model for metagenome representation. Robustness analyses (PERMANOVA, PERMDISP, entropy) show consistent separation of different states across subsamples. Overall, MetagenBERT provides a scalable, annotation free representation of metagenomes pointing toward future phenotype aware generalization across heterogeneous cohorts and sequencing technologies.", "authors": "Gaspar Roy; Eugeni Belda; Baptiste Hennecart; Y. Chevaleyre; E. Prifti; Jean-Daniel Zucker", "year": 2026.0, "venue": NaN, "date": "2026-01-05", "url": NaN, "sources": "['arxiv', 'semantic_scholar']", "n_sources": 2, "all_dois": "[]", "duplicate_count": 2, "abstract_source": NaN, "abstract_enriched_from": NaN, "abstract_old_len": NaN, "update_batch": NaN, "_pilot_row_id": 1444, "round-A_scope_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "no", "text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "primary_exclusion_code": "none", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "none", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "boundary_case": "none", "decision_rationale": "The abstract presents MetagenBERT, a new transformer architecture that processes raw DNA reads via genomic language models, with no mention of natural-language text, prompts, or captions, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge."}, "round-A_scope_reviewer_paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "round-A_scope_reviewer_bio_modality_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_component_present": "no", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "round-A_scope_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "round-A_scope_reviewer_boundary_case": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The abstract presents MetagenBERT, a new transformer architecture that processes raw DNA reads via genomic language models, with no mention of natural-language text, prompts, or captions, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge.", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "generative_model_present": "no", "foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "primary_exclusion_code": "none", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "boundary_case": "none", "decision_rationale": "The abstract presents MetagenBERT, a Transformer that encodes raw DNA reads using existing genomic LLMs; it does not describe a generative decoder, but it leverages large pretrained models and shows transferability, indicating foundation-model characteristics."}, "round-A_architecture_reviewer_paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_generative_model_present": "no", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_boundary_case": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The abstract presents MetagenBERT, a Transformer that encodes raw DNA reads using existing genomic LLMs; it does not describe a generative decoder, but it leverages large pretrained models and shows transferability, indicating foundation-model characteristics.", "pilot_roundA_scope_gate": "EXCLUDE", "pilot_roundA_architecture_gate": "EXCLUDE", "pilot_needs_adjudication": false}
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.jsonl:1486:{"cluster_id": 1608, "title": "Universal Single-Cell Transcriptomic Aging Clock powered by LLMs reveals targets to slow cellular aging", "title_normalized": "universal singlecell transcriptomic aging clock powered by llms reveals targets to slow cellular aging", "doi": "10.1093/geroni/igaf122.4305", "preprint_doi": NaN, "pmid": NaN, "arxiv_id": NaN, "abstract": "Abstract Large language models such as GPT have shown impressive performance in capturing structure and meaning from natural language. We adapt this capability to biology by representing single-cell transcriptomes as ordered sequences of gene names, analogous to sentences in text. Each cell’s expression profile is converted into a “cell sentence,” enabling pretrained language models to be fine-tuned directly on single-cell data for age prediction. Trained on millions of cells spanning diverse tissues and life stages, the model learns a universal latent representation of aging. It captures both cell-type–specific trajectories and conserved molecular signatures, generalizing across tissues and species. Unlike other foundational models that must be trained from scratch on omics data, our framework leverages already well-validated LLMs, taking advantage of their robust priors while avoiding the cost and instability of building new architectures. The resulting clock achieves state-of-the-art accuracy in predicting biological age at single-cell resolution. Embeddings highlight aging-associated processes such as immune activation, proteostasis decline, and metabolic shifts, aligning with known hallmarks of aging. Importantly, the model can also identify genes whose modulation decreases predicted transcriptional age, nominating candidate targets for therapeutic intervention. These features make the approach valuable both for measuring aging and for prioritizing interventions that may slow or reverse it. This work establishes the first universal, multi-tissue aging clock at single-cell resolution, demonstrating that pretrained language models can be directly adapted to biological data to map, measure, and potentially modify cellular aging.", "authors": "Raghav Sehgal", "year": 2025.0, "venue": "Innovation in aging", "date": "2025-12-01", "url": NaN, "sources": "['semantic_scholar']", "n_sources": 1, "all_dois": "['10.1093/geroni/igaf122.4305']", "duplicate_count": 1, "abstract_source": NaN, "abstract_enriched_from": NaN, "abstract_old_len": NaN, "update_batch": NaN, "_pilot_row_id": 1486, "round-A_scope_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "no", "text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "primary_exclusion_code": "none", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "representing single-cell transcriptomes as ordered sequences of gene names, analogous to sentences in text", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "representing single-cell transcriptomes as ordered sequences of gene names... fine‑tuned directly on single-cell data", "evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "boundary_case": "none", "decision_rationale": "The work presents a new model (primary_model_paper) that uses single‑cell transcriptomic data (bio modality). The only text‑like element is gene‑name sequencing, not natural language, so there is no substantive text component nor a natural‑language‑bio bridge."}, "round-A_scope_reviewer_paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "round-A_scope_reviewer_bio_modality_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_component_present": "no", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "round-A_scope_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "representing single-cell transcriptomes as ordered sequences of gene names, analogous to sentences in text", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "representing single-cell transcriptomes as ordered sequences of gene names... fine‑tuned directly on single-cell data", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "round-A_scope_reviewer_boundary_case": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The work presents a new model (primary_model_paper) that uses single‑cell transcriptomic data (bio modality). The only text‑like element is gene‑name sequencing, not natural language, so there is no substantive text component nor a natural‑language‑bio bridge.", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "generative_model_present": "no", "foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "primary_exclusion_code": "none", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "boundary_case": "clear_exclude", "decision_rationale": "The abstract describes fine‑tuning a pretrained LLM to predict cellular age, with no mention of a generative mechanism; it cites leveraging a large language model, indicating foundation‑model characteristics."}, "round-A_architecture_reviewer_paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_generative_model_present": "no", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_boundary_case": "clear_exclude", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The abstract describes fine‑tuning a pretrained LLM to predict cellular age, with no mention of a generative mechanism; it cites leveraging a large language model, indicating foundation‑model characteristics.", "pilot_roundA_scope_gate": "EXCLUDE", "pilot_roundA_architecture_gate": "EXCLUDE", "pilot_needs_adjudication": false}
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.jsonl:1563:{"cluster_id": 1693, "title": "TridentTCR: Predicting T Cell Receptor Specificity for Antigens and Autoimmune-Related Targets via a Topology-Aware Graph and Large Language Model", "title_normalized": "tridenttcr predicting t cell receptor specificity for antigens and autoimmunerelated targets via a topologyaware graph and large language model", "doi": "10.1101/2025.06.27.661632", "preprint_doi": NaN, "pmid": NaN, "arxiv_id": NaN, "abstract": "T cells recognize and eliminate diseased cells by binding their T cell receptors (TCRs) to short endogenous peptides presented on the cell surface, commonly referred to as antigens. Accurate prediction of antigen–TCR specificity is essential for advancing T cell-based immunotherapies while minimizing autoimmune toxicity. However, current computational methods struggle to generalize across diverse TCR repertoires and reliably differentiate therapeutic antigens from autoimmune-related antigens (arAgs). Here, we introduce TridentTCR, a computational framework integrating a pretrained large language model (LLM) for high-fidelity sequence embedding with a topology-aware graph neural network to capture structural insights from the antigen–TCR interaction network. TridentTCR significantly advances TCR specificity prediction by enabling robust trinary classification, distinguishing TCR interactions involving general antigens, arAgs, and non-binding. Additionally, TridentTCR outperforms existing state-of-the-art models in binary classification (binding vs. non-binding). Validation on independent clinical datasets demonstrated strong generalization capability for unseen TCRs and antigens. In a disease-specific context, TridentTCR identifies notable cross-reactivity between ZnT8 186-194 -specific TCRs and a mimotope from Bacteroides stercoris , suggesting that T cell cross-reactivity may contribute to the initiation of type 1 diabetes. Furthermore, we introduced a quantitative metric, antigenic immune response entropy (AIRE), which leverages TridentTCR predictions alongside clonotype frequencies and repertoire diversity to precisely quantify antigen-specific immune responses from single-cell profiling data. Collectively, TridentTCR provides an interpretable and clinically relevant tool, enabling improved understanding of TCR specificity, cross-reactivity, and off-target autoimmune risks in clinical immunotherapy.", "authors": "Rui Niu; Xiaoying Kong; Jiren Zhou; Yanli Li; Xuequn Shang", "year": 2025.0, "venue": "bioRxiv", "date": "2025-06-27", "url": NaN, "sources": "['biorxiv_medrxiv', 'semantic_scholar']", "n_sources": 2, "all_dois": "['10.1101/2025.06.27.661632']", "duplicate_count": 2, "abstract_source": NaN, "abstract_enriched_from": NaN, "abstract_old_len": NaN, "update_batch": NaN, "_pilot_row_id": 1566, "round-A_scope_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "no", "text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "primary_exclusion_code": "none", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "none", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "boundary_case": "none", "decision_rationale": "The abstract introduces a new computational framework (TridentTCR) for TCR‑antigen specificity prediction, indicating a primary model paper. It centers on biological sequences (TCRs, antigens) but mentions only a pretrained LLM used for sequence embedding, without natural‑language text, prompts, or documents, so no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge is evident."}, "round-A_scope_reviewer_paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "round-A_scope_reviewer_bio_modality_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_component_present": "no", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "round-A_scope_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "round-A_scope_reviewer_boundary_case": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The abstract introduces a new computational framework (TridentTCR) for TCR‑antigen specificity prediction, indicating a primary model paper. It centers on biological sequences (TCRs, antigens) but mentions only a pretrained LLM used for sequence embedding, without natural‑language text, prompts, or documents, so no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge is evident.", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "generative_model_present": "no", "foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "primary_exclusion_code": "EC3_not_generative", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "boundary_case": "clear_exclude", "decision_rationale": "The abstract describes TridentTCR as a prediction (trinary/binary classification) framework and mentions no decoder, autoregressive, or other generative mechanism; it only uses a pretrained LLM for embeddings."}, "round-A_architecture_reviewer_paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_generative_model_present": "no", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "EC3_not_generative", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_boundary_case": "clear_exclude", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The abstract describes TridentTCR as a prediction (trinary/binary classification) framework and mentions no decoder, autoregressive, or other generative mechanism; it only uses a pretrained LLM for embeddings.", "pilot_roundA_scope_gate": "EXCLUDE", "pilot_roundA_architecture_gate": "EXCLUDE", "pilot_needs_adjudication": false}
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.jsonl:1831:{"cluster_id": 1969, "title": "Cell2Text: Multimodal LLM for Generating Single-Cell Descriptions from RNA-Seq Data", "title_normalized": "cell2text multimodal llm for generating singlecell descriptions from rnaseq data", "doi": "10.48550/arXiv.2509.24840", "preprint_doi": NaN, "pmid": NaN, "arxiv_id": "2509.24840", "abstract": "Single-cell RNA sequencing has transformed biology by enabling the measurement of gene expression at cellular resolution, providing information for cell types, states, and disease contexts. Recently, single-cell foundation models have emerged as powerful tools for learning transferable representations directly from expression profiles, improving performance on classification and clustering tasks. However, these models are limited to discrete prediction heads, which collapse cellular complexity into predefined labels that fail to capture the richer, contextual explanations biologists need. We introduce Cell2Text, a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA-seq profiles into structured natural language descriptions. By integrating gene-level embeddings from single-cell foundation models with pretrained large language models, Cell2Text generates coherent summaries that capture cellular identity, tissue origin, disease associations, and pathway activity, generalizing to unseen cells. Empirically, Cell2Text outperforms baselines on classification accuracy, demonstrates strong ontological consistency using PageRank-based similarity metrics, and achieves high semantic fidelity in text generation. These results demonstrate that coupling expression data with natural language offers both stronger predictive performance and inherently interpretable outputs, pointing to a scalable path for label-efficient characterization of unseen cells.", "authors": 