age text", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "no natural-language bridge described", "evidence_for_generative_model": "LLM used for regression, not generation", "boundary_case": "wrapper_boundary", "decision_rationale": "The abstract proposes a pipeline that uses existing transformer encoders and a large language model for regression on tRNA embeddings, without introducing a new multimodal generative model or natural-language text component, thus it is an application‑wrapper study lacking a text‑bio bridge."}, "round-B_adjudicator_paper_type": "application_wrapper", "round-B_adjudicator_bio_modality_present": "yes", "round-B_adjudicator_text_component_present": "no", "round-B_adjudicator_text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "round-B_adjudicator_generative_model_present": "no", "round-B_adjudicator_foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "round-B_adjudicator_primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "round-B_adjudicator_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-B_adjudicator_evidence_for_text_component": "no mention of natural language text", "round-B_adjudicator_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "no natural-language bridge described", "round-B_adjudicator_evidence_for_generative_model": "LLM used for regression, not generation", "round-B_adjudicator_boundary_case": "wrapper_boundary", "round-B_adjudicator_decision_rationale": "The abstract proposes a pipeline that uses existing transformer encoders and a large language model for regression on tRNA embeddings, without introducing a new multimodal generative model or natural-language text component, thus it is an application‑wrapper study lacking a text‑bio bridge."}
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.jsonl:1927:{"cluster_id": 2042, "title": "Capturing Natural Evolution in Function-guided RNA Design via Genomic Foundation Models", "title_normalized": "capturing natural evolution in functionguided rna design via genomic foundation models", "doi": "10.1101/2025.04.08.647793", "preprint_doi": NaN, "pmid": NaN, "arxiv_id": NaN, "abstract": "Summary\n                RNAs play critical roles in gene expression regulation, catalysis, genetic information transmission, and immune response. However, current RNA engineering approaches face limitations in designing RNA for efficient in vivo folding and functional execution. Here, we present a novel, zero-shot strategy that integrates genomic large language models (LLMs) with inverse folding models (IFMs) to generate RNA sequences that mirror natural evolutionary patterns while preserving the structural integrity of critical functional regions. Benchmarking 9 state-of-the-art genomic foundation models, we show that their unsupervised log-likelihood scores correlate strongly with RNA fitness, enabling zero-shot sequence design. Applied to fluorescent RNA aptamers, our approach yielded 20 Broccoli variants, over half of which achieved up to 155% in fluorescence and a twofold increase in binding affinity, with robust performance both in vitro and in vivo. For Pepper, two rounds of optimization produced 40 variants, 16 of which demonstrated up to 2.6 fold in fluorescence and a threefold boost in binding affinity, including five with superior in vivo brightness.This approach offers a scalable, efficient, and resource-light method for RNA design, with significant implications for RNA-based therapeutics and diagnostics. By leveraging general foundation models to facilitate RNA evolution in a zero-shot fashion, we believe our work represents a major advancement in the field of RNA engineering.", "authors": "Yanjie Huang; Bibi Zhang; Jack X. Chen; Runze Ma; Zhongyue Zhang; Xianjun Chen; Yi Yang; Shuangjia Zheng", "year": 2025.0, "venue": "bioRxiv", "date": "2025-04-09", "url": NaN, "sources": "['semantic_scholar']", "n_sources": 1, "all_dois": "['10.1101/2025.04.08.647793']", "duplicate_count": 1, "abstract_source": "crossref", "abstract_enriched_from": NaN, "abstract_old_len": NaN, "update_batch": NaN, "_pilot_row_id": 1909, "round-A_scope_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "no", "text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "primary_exclusion_code": "none", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "none", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "boundary_case": "none", "decision_rationale": "The paper introduces a new zero‑shot RNA design method using genomic LLMs and inverse folding models (primary model). RNA sequences constitute a biological modality, but the abstract contains no mention of natural‑language text, prompts, or documents, so no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge is evident."}, "round-A_scope_reviewer_paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "round-A_scope_reviewer_bio_modality_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_component_present": "no", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "round-A_scope_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "round-A_scope_reviewer_boundary_case": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The paper introduces a new zero‑shot RNA design method using genomic LLMs and inverse folding models (primary model). RNA sequences constitute a biological modality, but the abstract contains no mention of natural‑language text, prompts, or documents, so no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge is evident.", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "application_wrapper", "generative_model_present": "yes", "foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_generative_model": "integrates genomic large language models (LLMs) ... to generate RNA sequences", "boundary_case": "wrapper_boundary", "decision_rationale": "The abstract describes a zero‑shot strategy that uses existing genomic LLMs to generate RNA sequences, without introducing a new primary generative model, thus it is an application wrapper."}, "round-A_architecture_reviewer_paper_type": "application_wrapper", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_generative_model_present": "yes", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "integrates genomic large language models (LLMs) ... to generate RNA sequences", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_boundary_case": "wrapper_boundary", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The abstract describes a zero‑shot strategy that uses existing genomic LLMs to generate RNA sequences, without introducing a new primary generative model, thus it is an application wrapper.", "pilot_roundA_scope_gate": "EXCLUDE", "pilot_roundA_architecture_gate": "EXCLUDE", "pilot_needs_adjudication": true, "round-B_adjudicator_output": {"paper_type": "application_wrapper", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "no", "text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "generative_model_present": "yes", "foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "none", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "evidence_for_generative_model": "generate RNA sequences that mirror natural evolutionary patterns", "boundary_case": "wrapper_boundary", "decision_rationale": "The abstract presents a zero‑shot strategy that integrates existing genomic LLMs with inverse‑folding models to generate RNA sequences, with no mention of natural‑language text or a multimodal text‑bio model, fitting the definition of a wrapper‑style use of foundation models."}, "round-B_adjudicator_paper_type": "application_wrapper", "round-B_adjudicator_bio_modality_present": "yes", "round-B_adjudicator_text_component_present": "no", "round-B_adjudicator_text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "round-B_adjudicator_generative_model_present": "yes", "round-B_adjudicator_foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "round-B_adjudicator_primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "round-B_adjudicator_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-B_adjudicator_evidence_for_text_component": "none", "round-B_adjudicator_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "round-B_adjudicator_evidence_for_generative_model": "generate RNA sequences that mirror natural evolutionary patterns", "round-B_adjudicator_boundary_case": "wrapper_boundary", "round-B_adjudicator_decision_rationale": "The abstract presents a zero‑shot strategy that integrates existing genomic LLMs with inverse‑folding models to generate RNA sequences, with no mention of natural‑language text or a multimodal text‑bio model, fitting the definition of a wrapper‑style use of foundation models."}
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.jsonl:2100:{"cluster_id": 2246, "title": "CellTok: Early-Fusion Multimodal Large Language Model for Single-Cell Transcriptomics via Tokenization", "title_normalized": "celltok earlyfusion multimodal large language model for singlecell transcriptomics via tokenization", "doi": "10.1101/2025.10.22.684047", "preprint_doi": NaN, "pmid": NaN, "arxiv_id": NaN, "abstract": "Single-cell transcriptomic data provide a high-resolution view of cellular states and functions, offering critical insights into development, disease, and tissue heterogeneity. Existing foundation models for single-cell analysis typically embed cells as continuous vectors, limiting their generative flexibility and hindering integration with the knowledge accumulated in large language models (LLMs). In this work, we present CellTok, a multimodal LLM framework for unified analysis of scRNA-seq data and biological text. Each cell is tokenized into discrete codebook tokens via VQ-VAE and integrated into the LLM’s vocabulary using early fusion. This allows CellTok to process biological and textual inputs autoregressively, leveraging pretrained knowledge within LLMs to analyze cellular states and interactions. CellTok demonstrates strong performance in cell-level tasks such as annotation and generation, and enables previously unattainable large population-level tasks, including accurate prediction of intercellular communication networks, which is a key mechanism in tissue organization and disease. Furthermore, it retains interactive Q&A capabilities grounded in biomedical knowledge, offering a new paradigm for analyzing complex cellular systems. Our results suggest that the CellTok framework can extend the capabilities of LLMs to single-cell data, and suggests a direction for building more general mixed-modal models.", "authors": "Chuxi Xiao; Haiyang Bian; Yixin Chen; Lei Wei; Xuegong Zhang", "year": 2025.0, "venue": "bioRxiv", "date": "2025-10-24", "url": NaN, "sources": "['biorxiv_medrxiv', 'google_scholar', 'semantic_scholar']", "n_sources": 3, "all_dois": "['10.1101/2025.10.22.684047']", "duplicate_count": 3, "abstract_source": NaN, "abstract_enriched_from": NaN, "abstract_old_len": NaN, "update_batch": NaN, "_pilot_row_id": 2101, "round-A_scope_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "yes", "text_bio_bridge_present": "yes", "primary_exclusion_code": "none", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "process biological and textual inputs autoregressively; interactive Q&A capabilities grounded in biomedical knowledge", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "Each cell is tokenized ... integrated into the LLM’s vocabulary using early fusion; process biological and textual inputs autoregressively", "evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "boundary_case": "none", "decision_rationale": "The abstract introduces CellTok as a multimodal LLM that tokenizes scRNA‑seq data and jointly processes it with natural‑language text, showing a primary model with both biological and text components and a substantive text‑bio bridge."}, "round-A_scope_reviewer_paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "round-A_scope_reviewer_bio_modality_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_component_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_bio_bridge_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "process biological and textual inputs autoregressively; interactive Q&A capabilities grounded in biomedical knowledge", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "Each cell is tokenized ... integrated into the LLM’s vocabulary using early fusion; process biological and textual inputs autoregressively", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "round-A_scope_reviewer_boundary_case": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The abstract introduces CellTok as a multimodal LLM that tokenizes scRNA‑seq data and jointly processes it with natural‑language text, showing a primary model with both biological and text components and a substantive text‑bio bridge.", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "generative_model_present": "yes", "foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "primary_exclusion_code": "none", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_generative_model": "processes biological and textual inputs autoregressively", "boundary_case": "clear_include", "decision_rationale": "The abstract states that CellTok processes inputs autoregressively and enables generation, and it leverages pretrained LLM knowledge, indicating a primary generative foundation model."}, "round-A_architecture_reviewer_paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_generative_model_present": "yes", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "processes biological and textual inputs autoregressively", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_boundary_case": "clear_include", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The abstract states that CellTok processes inputs autoregressively and enables generation, and it leverages pretrained LLM knowledge, indicating a primary generative foundation model.", "pilot_roundA_scope_gate": "PASS", "pilot_roundA_architecture_gate": "PASS", "pilot_needs_adjudication": false}
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.jsonl:2104:{"cluster_id": 2248, "title": "Long-range gene expression prediction with token alignment of large language model", "title_normalized": "longrange gene expression prediction with token alignment of large language model", "doi": "10.48550/arXiv.2410.01858", "preprint_doi": NaN, "pmid": NaN, "arxiv_id": "2410.01858", "abstract": "Gene expression is a cellular process that plays a fundamental role in human phenotypical variations and diseases. Despite advances of deep learning models for gene expression prediction, recent benchmarks have revealed their inability to learn distal regulatory grammar. Here, we address this challenge by leveraging a pretrained large language model to enhance gene expression prediction. We introduce Genetic sequence Token Alignment (GTA), which aligns genetic sequence features with natural language tokens, allowing for symbolic reasoning of genomic sequence features via the frozen language model. This cross-modal adaptation learns the regulatory grammar and allows us to further incorporate gene-specific human annotations as prompts, enabling in-context learning that is not possible with existing models. Trained on lymphoblastoid cells, GTA was evaluated on cells from the Geuvadis consortium and outperforms state-of-the-art models such as Enformer, achieving a Spearman correlation of 0.65, a 10\\% improvement. Additionally, GTA offers improved interpretation of long-range interactions through the identification of the most meaningful sections of the input genetic context. GTA represents a powerful and novel cross-modal approach to gene expression prediction by utilizing a pretrained language model, in a paradigm shift from conventional gene expression models trained only on sequence data.", "authors": "Edouardo Honig; Huixin Zhan; Ying Nian Wu; Zijun Zhang", "year": 2024.0, "venue": "arXiv.org", "date": "2024-10-02", "url": NaN, "sources": "['arxiv', 'semantic_scholar']", "n_sources": 2, "all_dois": "['10.48550/arXiv.2410.01858']", "duplicate_count": 2, "abstract_source": NaN, "abstract_enriched_from": NaN, "abstract_old_len": NaN, "update_batch": NaN, "_pilot_row_id": 2103, "round-A_scope_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "yes", "text_bio_bridge_present": "yes", "primary_exclusion_code": "none", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "aligns genetic sequence features with natural language tokens", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "cross‑modal adaptation learns the regulatory grammar", "evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "boundary_case": "none", "decision_rationale": "The abstract presents GTA as a new model that aligns genetic sequences with natural‑language tokens and uses prompts, demonstrating a primary multimodal model with both biological and text components and a substantive text‑bio bridge."}, "round-A_scope_reviewer_paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "round-A_scope_reviewer_bio_modality_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_component_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_bio_bridge_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "aligns genetic sequence features with natural language tokens", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "cross‑modal adaptation learns the regulatory grammar", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "round-A_scope_reviewer_boundary_case": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The abstract presents GTA as a new model that aligns genetic sequences with natural‑language tokens and uses prompts, demonstrating a primary multimodal model with both biological and text components and a substantive text‑bio bridge.", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "generative_model_present": "no", "foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "primary_exclusion_code": "none", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "boundary_case": "clear_exclude", "decision_rationale": "The abstract introduces GTA, a model that predicts gene expression using a frozen large language model but does not describe any generative mechanism (e.g., decoder, autoregressive generation). It does leverage a pretrained LLM, providing foundation‑model evidence."}, "round-A_architecture_reviewer_paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_generative_model_present": "no", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_boundary_case": "clear_exclude", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The abstract introduces GTA, a model that predicts gene expression using a frozen large language model but does not describe any generative mechanism (e.g., decoder, autoregressive generation). It does leverage a pretrained LLM, providing foundation‑model evidence.", "pilot_roundA_scope_gate": "PASS", "pilot_roundA_architecture_gate": "EXCLUDE", "pilot_needs_adjudication": false}
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.jsonl:2229:{"cluster_id": 2381, "title": "Advances in Human Genome Resolution: The Role of Pan-Genomic Strategies and Fine-Tuning Pre-trained Genomic Models", "title_normalized": "advances in human genome resolution the role of pangenomic strategies and finetuning pretrained genomic models", "doi": "10.18103/mra.v12i7.5571", "preprint_doi": NaN, "pmid": NaN, "arxiv_id": NaN, "abstract": "The groundbreaking theory of DNA double helix structure has greatly promoted the development of molecular genetics, shaping and refining the genetic central dogma, thus enabling researchers to explore genotype-phenotype regulation at different levels. In particular, with the continued advancement of third-generation sequencing technology, an increasing number of highly accurate human genomes have been assembled, such as T2T-CHM13 and HG002. These high-quality genome sequences not only provide a more comprehensive human reference sequence, but also enable functional genomics studies within a unified coordinate system. To better explore and resolve the complex genetic information encompassed within human genome sequences, scientists have proposed novel research strategies, involving graphical pan-genome and pre-trained genomic models. The graphical pan-genomes provide population- level high-quality references, revealing the genomic diversity within populations and exploring the sequence complexity of specific regions, such as the KIR immune region. Concurrently, related studies of pre-trained models within the human genome offer new perspectives for interpreting sequence functions and delving into the hidden genetic codes, potentially leading to complete DNA decoding. Overall, graphical pan-genome and pre-trained genomic models represent two crucial strategies in genomics research, which will provide more new insights and make greater breakthroughs in the human genome. Together, these approaches have deepened our understanding of the human genome, fostered the development of bioinformatics ecosystems, and will contribute to the establishment and improvement of the entire field. Therefore, this review focuses on DNA sequencing, human genome assembly, high-quality pan-genome and pre-trained genomic large language models (LLMs), highlighting and summarizing the latest achievements and progress in human genome research, discussing existing challenges and providing future perspectives.", "authors": "Lei Liu", "year": 2024.0, "venue": "Medical Research Archives", "date": NaN, "url": NaN, "sources": "['semantic_scholar']", "n_sources": 1, "all_dois": "['10.18103/mra.v12i7.5571']", "duplicate_count": 1, "abstract_source": NaN, "abstract_enriched_from": NaN, "abstract_old_len": NaN, "update_batch": NaN, "_pilot_row_id": 2230, "round-A_scope_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "review_editorial", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "no", "text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "primary_exclusion_code": "review_editorial", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "none", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "boundary_case": "clear_exclude", "decision_rationale": "The abstract explicitly states it is a review and summarizes advances in pan‑genome and pre‑trained genomic LLMs, with no mention of natural‑language text or a text‑bio mapping."}, "round-A_scope_reviewer_paper_type": "review_editorial", "round-A_scope_reviewer_bio_modality_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_component_present": "no", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "round-A_scope_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "review_editorial", "round-A_scope_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "round-A_scope_reviewer_boundary_case": "clear_exclude", "round-A_scope_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The abstract explicitly states it is a review and summarizes advances in pan‑genome and pre‑trained genomic LLMs, with no mention of natural‑language text or a text‑bio mapping.", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "review_editorial", "generative_model_present": "no", "foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "primary_exclusion_code": "review_editorial", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "boundary_case": "clear_exclude", "decision_rationale": "The abstract is a review summarizing pan‑genome and pre‑trained genomic LLMs, not presenting a new generative architecture."}, "round-A_architecture_reviewer_paper_type": "review_editorial", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_generative_model_present": "no", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "review_editorial", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_boundary_case": "clear_exclude", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The abstract is a review summarizing pan‑genome and pre‑trained genomic LLMs, not presenting a new generative architecture.", "pilot_roundA_scope_gate": "EXCLUDE", "pilot_roundA_architecture_gate": "EXCLUDE", "pilot_needs_adjudication": false}
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.jsonl:2335:{"cluster_id": 2500, "title": "GenoCGC: A Hybrid CNN and Graph-based Transformer Model for Genomic Sequence Classification", "title_normalized": "genocgc a hybrid cnn and graphbased transformer model for genomic sequence classification", "doi": "10.1109/ICIT64950.2025.11049132", "preprint_doi": NaN, "pmid": NaN, "arxiv_id": NaN, "abstract": "Advancements in sequencing technologies have resulted in rapid growth in genomic data. This revolutionary technology has enabled conducting large-scale studies in biology and medicine. In bioinformatics, accurately classifying genomic sequences is a necessary step for understanding genetic variations, identifying disease mutations, and advancing precision medicine. However, it remains one of the key challenges in the field. Traditional machine learning approaches often struggle with the complexity of genomic data. However, deep learning approaches, such as transformer-based models, improve analysis by capturing long-range dependencies. We introduce GenoCGC, a hybrid model combining Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) and a Graph-based Transformer (Graphormer) for genomic sequence classification. GenoCGC uses CNNs for capturing local features while utilizing Graphormer to model complex dependencies. We compared GenoCGC with traditional classifiers and genomic large language models across multiple genomic benchmark tasks. Experimental results show that GenoCGC achieves promising performance, providing an accurate and practical solution for genomic sequence analysis.", "authors": "Anas Abu-Doleh", "year": 2025.0, "venue": "2025 12th International Conference on Information Technology (ICIT)", "date": "2025-05-27", "url": NaN, "sources": "['semantic_scholar']", "n_sources": 1, "all_dois": "['10.1109/ICIT64950.2025.11049132']", "duplicate_count": 1, "abstract_source": NaN, "abstract_enriched_from": NaN, "abstract_old_len": NaN, "update_batch": NaN, "_pilot_row_id": 2340, "round-A_scope_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "no", "text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "primary_exclusion_code": "none", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "none", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "boundary_case": "none", "decision_rationale": "The paper introduces a new hybrid CNN‑Graph Transformer model for genomic sequence classification, with no mention of natural‑language text, prompts, or textual descriptions; therefore it lacks a substantive text component and bridge."}, "round-A_scope_reviewer_paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "round-A_scope_reviewer_bio_modality_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_component_present": "no", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "round-A_scope_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "round-A_scope_reviewer_boundary_case": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The paper introduces a new hybrid CNN‑Graph Transformer model for genomic sequence classification, with no mention of natural‑language text, prompts, or textual descriptions; therefore it lacks a substantive text component and bridge.", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "generative_model_present": "no", "foundation_model_evidence": "no", "primary_exclusion_code": "EC3_not_generative", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "boundary_case": "clear_exclude", "decision_rationale": "The abstract introduces a hybrid CNN‑Graph Transformer for genomic sequence classification and reports performance comparisons, with no mention of generation or generative mechanisms."}, "round-A_architecture_reviewer_paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_generative_model_present": "no", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_foundation_model_evidence": "no", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "EC3_not_generative", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_boundary_case": "clear_exclude", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The abstract introduces a hybrid CNN‑Graph Transformer for genomic sequence classification and reports performance comparisons, with no mention of generation or generative mechanisms.", "pilot_roundA_scope_gate": "EXCLUDE", "pilot_roundA_architecture_gate": "EXCLUDE", "pilot_needs_adjudication": false}
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.jsonl:2595:{"cluster_id": 2769, "title": "AI-discovered tuning laws explain neuronal population code geometry", "title_normalized": "aidiscovered tuning laws explain neuronal population code geometry", "doi": "10.1101/2025.11.12.688086", "preprint_doi": NaN, "pmid": NaN, "arxiv_id": NaN, "abstract": "The activity of visual cortical neurons forms a population code representing image stimuli. There is, however, a discrepancy between our understanding of this code at the single-cell and population levels: direct measurements indicate the population code is high-dimensional, but established models of single-cell tuning give rise to low-dimensional codes. We reconciled this discrepancy by developing an AI science system to find a new parsimonious, interpretable equation for visual cortical orientation tuning. Candidate equations were expressed as short computer programs and evolved by Large Language Models (LLMs) using graphical diagnostics. The resulting equation not only improved single-cell fits, but also accurately modelled the population code’s high-dimensional geometry. A novel parameter of the AI-discovered equation, which controls single-cell tuning smoothness, gives rise to high-dimensional population codes. The same parameter drives high-dimensional coding in head-direction cells, suggesting a common coding strategy across brain regions. We used this equation to hypothesize a circuit mechanism generating high-dimensional population codes, and to demonstrate the advantages of these codes in a simulated hyperacuity task. These results show that tuning smoothness has a key role in controlling population code geometry, and demonstrate how AI equation discovery can deliver interpretable models accelerating scientific understanding in neuroscience and beyond.", "authors": "Tilbury R, Kwon D, Haydaroğlu A, Ratliff J, Schmutz V, Carandini M, Miller K, Stachenfeld K, Harris KD.", "year": 2025.0, "venue": NaN, "date": "2025-11-13", "url": NaN, "sources": "['biorxiv_medrxiv']", "n_sources": 1, "all_dois": "['10.1101/2025.11.12.688086']", "duplicate_count": 1, "abstract_source": NaN, "abstract_enriched_from": NaN, "abstract_old_len": NaN, "update_batch": NaN, "_pilot_row_id": 2597, "round-A_scope_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "no", "text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "primary_exclusion_code": "EC2_no_text_component", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "evolved by Large Language Models (LLMs) using graphical diagnostics", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "boundary_case": "none", "decision_rationale": "The paper introduces a new AI system that uses LLMs to evolve equations for neuronal population coding, showing a clear biological modality, but the abstract describes only program generation, not natural‑language text or a text‑bio mapping."}, "round-A_scope_reviewer_paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "round-A_scope_reviewer_bio_modality_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_component_present": "no", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "round-A_scope_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "EC2_no_text_component", "round-A_scope_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "evolved by Large Language Models (LLMs) using graphical diagnostics", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "round-A_scope_reviewer_boundary_case": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The paper introduces a new AI system that uses LLMs to evolve equations for neuronal population coding, showing a clear biological modality, but the abstract describes only program generation, not natural‑language text or a text‑bio mapping.", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "generative_model_present": "yes", "foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "primary_exclusion_code": "none", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_generative_model": "evolved by Large Language Models (LLMs)", "boundary_case": "generative_boundary", "decision_rationale": "The abstract states that candidate equations are evolved by LLMs, showing a generative mechanism, and that pretrained LLMs are used, indicating foundation‑model characteristics."}, "round-A_architecture_reviewer_paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_generative_model_present": "yes", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "evolved by Large Language Models (LLMs)", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_boundary_case": "generative_boundary", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The abstract states that candidate equations are evolved by LLMs, showing a generative mechanism, and that pretrained LLMs are used, indicating foundation‑model characteristics.", "pilot_roundA_scope_gate": "EXCLUDE", "pilot_roundA_architecture_gate": "PASS", "pilot_needs_adjudication": false}
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement/cross_model_decisions.csv:180:1969,Cell2Text: Multimodal LLM for Generating Single-Cell Descriptions from RNA-Seq Data,"Single-cell RNA sequencing has transformed biology by enabling the measurement of gene expression at cellular resolution, providing information for cell types, states, and disease contexts. Recently, single-cell foundation models have emerged as powerful tools for learning transferable representations directly from expression profiles, improving performance on classification and clustering tasks. However, these models are limited to discrete prediction heads, which collapse cellular complexity into predefined labels that fail to capture the richer, contextual explanations biologists need. We introduce Cell2Text, a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA-seq profiles into structured natural language descriptions. By integrating gene-level embeddings from single-cell foundation models with pretrained large language models, Cell2Text generates coherent summaries that capture cellular identity, tissue origin, disease associations, and pathway activity, generalizing to unseen cells. Empirically, Cell2Text outperforms baselines on classification accuracy, demonstrates strong ontological consistency using PageRank-based similarity metrics, and achieves high semantic fidelity in text generation. These results demonstrate that coupling expression data with natural language offers both stronger predictive performance and inherently interpretable outputs, pointing to a scalable path for label-efficient characterization of unseen cells.",include_decision_unstable,Cell2Text: Multimodal LLM for Generating Single-Cell Descriptions from RNA-Seq Data,"Single-cell RNA sequencing has transformed biology by enabling the measurement of gene expression at cellular resolution, providing information for cell types, states, and disease contexts. Recently, single-cell foundation models have emerged as powerful tools for learning transferable representations directly from expression profiles, improving performance on classification and clustering tasks. However, these models are limited to discrete prediction heads, which collapse cellular complexity into predefined labels that fail to capture the richer, contextual explanations biologists need. We introduce Cell2Text, a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA-seq profiles into structured natural language descriptions. By integrating gene-level embeddings from single-cell foundation models with pretrained large language models, Cell2Text generates coherent summaries that capture cellular identity, tissue origin, disease associations, and pathway activity, generalizing to unseen cells. Empirically, Cell2Text outperforms baselines on classification accuracy, demonstrates strong ontological consistency using PageRank-based similarity metrics, and achieves high semantic fidelity in text generation. These results demonstrate that coupling expression data with natural language offers both stronger predictive performance and inherently interpretable outputs, pointing to a scalable path for label-efficient characterization of unseen cells.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,no,,,,,"The abstract presents Cell2Text as a multimodal LLM that generates natural language descriptions from single-cell RNA-seq data, clearly satisfying all criteria for a generative text-bio foundational model. | Cell2Text is a generative framework that translates scRNA-seq profiles into structured natural language descriptions, explicitly generating text. The abstract describes a generative model with evidence of text generation and integration of single-cell foundation models.","The abstract presents Cell2Text as a multimodal LLM that generates natural language descriptions from single-cell RNA-seq data, clearly satisfying all criteria for a generative text-bio foundational model.","Cell2Text is a generative framewo