s, satisfying all criteria for a primary text-bio foundational model. | Cell2Text is a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA-seq profiles into natural language descriptions, using single-cell foundation models and pretrained LLMs to generate coherent summaries, indicating a primary generative model paper.","Paper presents Cell2Text, a multimodal generative model that translates single-cell RNA-seq data into natural language descriptions, satisfying all criteria for a primary text-bio foundational model.","Cell2Text is a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA-seq profiles into natural language descriptions, using single-cell foundation models and pretrained LLMs to generate coherent summaries, indicating a primary generative model paper.",,Cell2Text: Multimodal LLM for Generating Single-Cell Descriptions from RNA-Seq Data,"Single-cell RNA sequencing has transformed biology by enabling the measurement of gene expression at cellular resolution, providing information for cell types, states, and disease contexts. Recently, single-cell foundation models have emerged as powerful tools for learning transferable representations directly from expression profiles, improving performance on classification and clustering tasks. However, these models are limited to discrete prediction heads, which collapse cellular complexity into predefined labels that fail to capture the richer, contextual explanations biologists need. We introduce Cell2Text, a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA-seq profiles into structured natural language descriptions. By integrating gene-level embeddings from single-cell foundation models with pretrained large language models, Cell2Text generates coherent summaries that capture cellular identity, tissue origin, disease associations, and pathway activity, generalizing to unseen cells. Empirically, Cell2Text outperforms baselines on classification accuracy, demonstrates strong ontological consistency using PageRank-based similarity metrics, and achieves high semantic fidelity in text generation. These results demonstrate that coupling expression data with natural language offers both stronger predictive performance and inherently interpretable outputs, pointing to a scalable path for label-efficient characterization of unseen cells.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract presents Cell2Text as a new multimodal generative framework that maps scRNA‑seq data to natural‑language descriptions, indicating a primary model with a biological modality, a substantive text component, and a clear text‑bio bridge. | Cell2Text is described as a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA‑seq profiles into natural‑language descriptions, indicating a decoder‑style generation mechanism; it integrates gene‑level embeddings from single‑cell foundation models with pretrained LLMs, evidencing foundation‑model characteristics.","The abstract presents Cell2Text as a new multimodal generative framework that maps scRNA‑seq data to natural‑language descriptions, indicating a primary model with a biological modality, a substantive text component, and a clear text‑bio bridge.","Cell2Text is described as a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA‑seq profiles into natural‑language descriptions, indicating a decoder‑style generation mechanism; it integrates gene‑level embeddings from single‑cell foundation models with pretrained LLMs, evidencing foundation‑model characteristics.",,Cell2Text: Multimodal LLM for Generating Single-Cell Descriptions from RNA-Seq Data,"Single-cell RNA sequencing has transformed biology by enabling the measurement of gene expression at cellular resolution, providing information for cell types, states, and disease contexts. Recently, single-cell foundation models have emerged as powerful tools for learning transferable representations directly from expression profiles, improving performance on classification and clustering tasks. However, these models are limited to discrete prediction heads, which collapse cellular complexity into predefined labels that fail to capture the richer, contextual explanations biologists need. We introduce Cell2Text, a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA-seq profiles into structured natural language descriptions. By integrating gene-level embeddings from single-cell foundation models with pretrained large language models, Cell2Text generates coherent summaries that capture cellular identity, tissue origin, disease associations, and pathway activity, generalizing to unseen cells. Empirically, Cell2Text outperforms baselines on classification accuracy, demonstrates strong ontological consistency using PageRank-based similarity metrics, and achieves high semantic fidelity in text generation. These results demonstrate that coupling expression data with natural language offers both stronger predictive performance and inherently interpretable outputs, pointing to a scalable path for label-efficient characterization of unseen cells.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract presents Cell2Text as a new multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA‑seq data into natural‑language descriptions, indicating a primary model with both a biological modality and a substantive text‑bio bridge. | The abstract presents Cell2Text as a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA‑seq profiles into natural‑language descriptions, indicating a generative decoder mechanism, and it builds on gene‑level embeddings from single‑cell foundation models and pretrained LLMs, evidencing foundation‑model characteristics.","The abstract presents Cell2Text as a new multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA‑seq data into natural‑language descriptions, indicating a primary model with both a biological modality and a substantive text‑bio bridge.","The abstract presents Cell2Text as a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA‑seq profiles into natural‑language descriptions, indicating a generative decoder mechanism, and it builds on gene‑level embeddings from single‑cell foundation models and pretrained LLMs, evidencing foundation‑model characteristics.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement/cross_model_decisions.csv:264:62,TissueNarrator: Generative Modeling of Spatial Transcriptomics with Large Language Models.,"The intricate spatial organization and molecular communication among cells are fundamental to multicellular systems. Spatial transcriptomics (ST) enables gene expression profiling while preserving spatial context, providing rich data for studying cellular interactions and tissue dynamics. However, most existing computational approaches focus on embedding-based tasks and provide limited generative capacity for simulating cell behavior  in situ . Moreover, accurately interpreting spatial interactions requires extensive biological knowledge, which current models do not incorporate. Here, we introduce T  issue N  arrator , a framework that reformulates spatial omics analysis as a language modeling problem. By representing tissue sections as spatial sentences – rank-based gene lists augmented with spatial coordinates and metadata – T  issue N  arrator leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs) to learn spatially conditioned gene expression patterns. The model generates realistic, context-aware cellular profiles, predicts intercellular interactions, and performs  in silico perturbation analyses. Across multiple ST technologies (MERFISH, Perturb-FISH, and CosMx SMI), T  issue N  arrator achieves superior quantitative performance and recovers biologically meaning-ful ligand–receptor and signaling pathways. Furthermore, a conversational inference mode enables natural-language querying of tissue organization. By integrating pretrained biological knowledge with spatial context, T  issue N  arrator establishes a new, scalable generative paradigm for modeling, simulating, and reasoning about tissue systems.",include_decision_unstable,TissueNarrator: Generative Modeling of Spatial Transcriptomics with Large Language Models.,"The intricate spatial organization and molecular communication among cells are fundamental to multicellular systems. Spatial transcriptomics (ST) enables gene expression profiling while preserving spatial context, providing rich data for studying cellular interactions and tissue dynamics. However, most existing computational approaches focus on embedding-based tasks and provide limited generative capacity for simulating cell behavior  in situ . Moreover, accurately interpreting spatial interactions requires extensive biological knowledge, which current models do not incorporate. Here, we introduce T  issue N  arrator , a framework that reformulates spatial omics analysis as a language modeling problem. By representing tissue sections as spatial sentences – rank-based gene lists augmented with spatial coordinates and metadata – T  issue N  arrator leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs) to learn spatially conditioned gene expression patterns. The model generates realistic, context-aware cellular profiles, predicts intercellular interactions, and performs  in silico perturbation analyses. Across multiple ST technologies (MERFISH, Perturb-FISH, and CosMx SMI), T  issue N  arrator achieves superior quantitative performance and recovers biologically meaning-ful ligand–receptor and signaling pathways. Furthermore, a conversational inference mode enables natural-language querying of tissue organization. By integrating pretrained biological knowledge with spatial context, T  issue N  arrator establishes a new, scalable generative paradigm for modeling, simulating, and reasoning about tissue systems.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,no,,,,,"TissueNarrator presents a new generative framework that models spatial transcriptomics using LLMs and includes a conversational natural-language interface, clearly satisfying all criteria as a primary model paper. | Abstract explicitly describes a generative framework that produces cellular profiles and performs in silico perturbations, satisfying generative model criterion. Foundation model evidence is absent for the candidate model itself as it leverages pretrained LLMs without claiming broad pretraining or transferable representations for TissueNarrator.","TissueNarrator presents a new generative framework that models spatial transcriptomics using LLMs and includes a conversational natural-language interface, clearly satisfying all criteria as a primary model paper.","Abstract explicitly describes a generative framework that produces cellular profiles and performs in silico perturbations, satisfying generative model criterion. Foundation model evidence is absent for the candidate model itself as it leverages pretrained LLMs without claiming broad pretraining or transferable representations for TissueNarrator.",,TissueNarrator: Generative Modeling of Spatial Transcriptomics with Large Language Models.,"The intricate spatial organization and molecular communication among cells are fundamental to multicellular systems. Spatial transcriptomics (ST) enables gene expression profiling while preserving spatial context, providing rich data for studying cellular interactions and tissue dynamics. However, most existing computational approaches focus on embedding-based tasks and provide limited generative capacity for simulating cell behavior  in situ . Moreover, accurately interpreting spatial interactions requires extensive biological knowledge, which current models do not incorporate. Here, we introduce T  issue N  arrator , a framework that reformulates spatial omics analysis as a language modeling problem. By representing tissue sections as spatial sentences – rank-based gene lists augmented with spatial coordinates and metadata – T  issue N  arrator leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs) to learn spatially conditioned gene expression patterns. The model generates realistic, context-aware cellular profiles, predicts intercellular interactions, and performs  in silico perturbation analyses. Across multiple ST technologies (MERFISH, Perturb-FISH, and CosMx SMI), T  issue N  arrator achieves superior quantitative performance and recovers biologically meaning-ful ligand–receptor and signaling pathways. Furthermore, a conversational inference mode enables natural-language querying of tissue organization. By integrating pretrained biological knowledge with spatial context, T  issue N  arrator establishes a new, scalable generative paradigm for modeling, simulating, and reasoning about tissue systems.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,conversational inference mode enables natural-language querying,conversational inference mode enables natural-language querying of tissue organization,"generates realistic, context-aware cellular profiles",clear_include,"Abstract presents TissueNarrator as a new generative framework that uses pretrained LLMs for spatial transcriptomics, with natural-language querying, so it is a primary model paper, not a wrapper.","Presents a new generative framework (TissueNarrator) that uses LLMs and a conversational interface for spatial transcriptomics, clearly including biological modality and text-bio bridge.","The paper introduces a framework that leverages pretrained LLMs to generate cellular profiles from spatial transcriptomics, indicating it is an application wrapper rather than a primary generative text-bio foundation model.","Abstract presents TissueNarrator as a new generative framework that uses pretrained LLMs for spatial transcriptomics, with natural-language querying, so it is a primary model paper, not a wrapper.",TissueNarrator: Generative Modeling of Spatial Transcriptomics with Large Language Models.,"The intricate spatial organization and molecular communication among cells are fundamental to multicellular systems. Spatial transcriptomics (ST) enables gene expression profiling while preserving spatial context, providing rich data for studying cellular interactions and tissue dynamics. However, most existing computational approaches focus on embedding-based tasks and provide limited generative capacity for simulating cell behavior  in situ . Moreover, accurately interpreting spatial interactions requires extensive biological knowledge, which current models do not incorporate. Here, we introduce T  issue N  arrator , a framework that reformulates spatial omics analysis as a language modeling problem. By representing tissue sections as spatial sentences – rank-based gene lists augmented with spatial coordinates and metadata – T  issue N  arrator leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs) to learn spatially conditioned gene expression patterns. The model generates realistic, context-aware cellular profiles, predicts intercellular interactions, and performs  in silico perturbation analyses. Across multiple ST technologies (MERFISH, Perturb-FISH, and CosMx SMI), T  issue N  arrator achieves superior quantitative performance and recovers biologically meaning-ful ligand–receptor and signaling pathways. Furthermore, a conversational inference mode enables natural-language querying of tissue organization. By integrating pretrained biological knowledge with spatial context, T  issue N  arrator establishes a new, scalable generative paradigm for modeling, simulating, and reasoning about tissue systems.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract presents TissueNarrator as a new generative framework that uses LLMs to model spatial transcriptomics and provides natural-language querying, satisfying both text and biological modality criteria. | The abstract introduces TissueNarrator, a framework that reformulates spatial omics as a language modeling problem and explicitly states that the model generates realistic cellular profiles, indicating a generative decoder architecture; it also leverages pretrained large language models, providing foundation‑model evidence.","The abstract presents TissueNarrator as a new generative framework that uses LLMs to model spatial transcriptomics and provides natural-language querying, satisfying both text and biological modality criteria.","The abstract introduces TissueNarrator, a framework that reformulates spatial omics as a language modeling problem and explicitly states that the model generates realistic cellular profiles, indicating a generative decoder architecture; it also leverages pretrained large language models, providing foundation‑model evidence.",,TissueNarrator: Generative Modeling of Spatial Transcriptomics with Large Language Models.,"The intricate spatial organization and molecular communication among cells are fundamental to multicellular systems. Spatial transcriptomics (ST) enables gene expression profiling while preserving spatial context, providing rich data for studying cellular interactions and tissue dynamics. However, most existing computational approaches focus on embedding-based tasks and provide limited generative capacity for simulating cell behavior  in situ . Moreover, accurately interpreting spatial interactions requires extensive biological knowledge, which current models do not incorporate. Here, we introduce T  issue N  arrator , a framework that reformulates spatial omics analysis as a language modeling problem. By representing tissue sections as spatial sentences – rank-based gene lists augmented with spatial coordinates and metadata – T  issue N  arrator leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs) to learn spatially conditioned gene expression patterns. The model generates realistic, context-aware cellular profiles, predicts intercellular interactions, and performs  in silico perturbation analyses. Across multiple ST technologies (MERFISH, Perturb-FISH, and CosMx SMI), T  issue N  arrator achieves superior quantitative performance and recovers biologically meaning-ful ligand–receptor and signaling pathways. Furthermore, a conversational inference mode enables natural-language querying of tissue organization. By integrating pretrained biological knowledge with spatial context, T  issue N  arrator establishes a new, scalable generative paradigm for modeling, simulating, and reasoning about tissue systems.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract introduces TissueNarrator, a new multimodal generative model that treats spatial transcriptomics as language, uses LLMs, and provides natural‑language querying, satisfying all inclusion criteria. | The abstract introduces a new framework that generates realistic cellular profiles using pretrained LLMs, indicating a generative architecture and evidence of broad pretraining.","The abstract introduces TissueNarrator, a new multimodal generative model that treats spatial transcriptomics as language, uses LLMs, and provides natural‑language querying, satisfying all inclusion criteria.","The abstract introduces a new framework that generates realistic cellular profiles using pretrained LLMs, indicating a generative architecture and evidence of broad pretraining.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement/cross_model_decisions.csv:503:2246,CellTok: Early-Fusion Multimodal Large Language Model for Single-Cell Transcriptomics via Tokenization,"Single-cell transcriptomic data provide a high-resolution view of cellular states and functions, offering critical insights into development, disease, and tissue heterogeneity. Existing foundation models for single-cell analysis typically embed cells as continuous vectors, limiting their generative flexibility and hindering integration with the knowledge accumulated in large language models (LLMs). In this work, we present CellTok, a multimodal LLM framework for unified analysis of scRNA-seq data and biological text. Each cell is tokenized into discrete codebook tokens via VQ-VAE and integrated into the LLM’s vocabulary using early fusion. This allows CellTok to process biological and textual inputs autoregressively, leveraging pretrained knowledge within LLMs to analyze cellular states and interactions. CellTok demonstrates strong performance in cell-level tasks such as annotation and generation, and enables previously unattainable large population-level tasks, including accurate prediction of intercellular communication networks, which is a key mechanism in tissue organization and disease. Furthermore, it retains interactive Q&A capabilities grounded in biomedical knowledge, offering a new paradigm for analyzing complex cellular systems. Our results suggest that the CellTok framework can extend the capabilities of LLMs to single-cell data, and suggests a direction for building more general mixed-modal models.",stable_include,CellTok: Early-Fusion Multimodal Large Language Model for Single-Cell Transcriptomics via Tokenization,"Single-cell transcriptomic data provide a high-resolution view of cellular states and functions, offering critical insights into development, disease, and tissue heterogeneity. Existing foundation models for single-cell analysis typically embed cells as continuous vectors, limiting their generative flexibility and hindering integration with the knowledge accumulated in large language models (LLMs). In this work, we present CellTok, a multimodal LLM framework for unified analysis of scRNA-seq data and biological text. Each cell is tokenized into discrete codebook tokens via VQ-VAE and integrated into the LLM’s vocabulary using early fusion. This allows CellTok to process biological and textual inputs autoregressively, leveraging pretrained knowledge within LLMs to analyze cellular states and interactions. CellTok demonstrates strong performance in cell-level tasks such as annotation and generation, and enables previously unattainable large population-level tasks, including accurate prediction of intercellular communication networks, which is a key mechanism in tissue organization and disease. Furthermore, it retains interactive Q&A capabilities grounded in biomedical knowledge, offering a new paradigm for analyzing complex cellular systems. Our results suggest that the CellTok framework can extend the capabilities of LLMs to single-cell data, and suggests a direction for building more general mixed-modal models.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,unclear,,,,,"CellTok presents a new multimodal LLM that tokenizes single-cell data and integrates it with biological text via early fusion, enabling autoregressive processing and Q&A. Both bio modality and text component are clearly central, and the text-bio bridge is substantive. | CellTok proposes a VQ-VAE tokenization of single-cell data integrated into an LLM via early fusion, with autoregressive generation and cell-level generative tasks, making it a primary generative model paper with some foundation model characteristics (unclear due to lack of explicit broad pretraining details).","CellTok presents a new multimodal LLM that tokenizes single-cell data and integrates it with biological text via early fusion, enabling autoregressive processing and Q&A. Both bio modality and text component are clearly central, and the text-bio bridge is substantive.","CellTok proposes a VQ-VAE tokenization of single-cell data integrated into an LLM via early fusion, with autoregressive generation and cell-level generative tasks, making it a primary generative model paper with some foundation model characteristics (unclear due to lack of explicit broad pretraining details).",,CellTok: Early-Fusion Multimodal Large Language Model for Single-Cell Transcriptomics via Tokenization,"Single-cell transcriptomic data provide a high-resolution view of cellular states and functions, offering critical insights into development, disease, and tissue heterogeneity. Existing foundation models for single-cell analysis typically embed cells as continuous vectors, limiting their generative flexibility and hindering integration with the knowledge accumulated in large language models (LLMs). In this work, we present CellTok, a multimodal LLM framework for unified analysis of scRNA-seq data and biological text. Each cell is tokenized into discrete codebook tokens via VQ-VAE and integrated into the LLM’s vocabulary using early fusion. This allows CellTok to process biological and textual inputs autoregressively, leveraging pretrained knowledge within LLMs to analyze cellular states and interactions. CellTok demonstrates strong performance in cell-level tasks such as annotation and generation, and enables previously unattainable large population-level tasks, including accurate prediction of intercellular communication networks, which is a key mechanism in tissue organization and disease. Furthermore, it retains interactive Q&A capabilities grounded in biomedical knowledge, offering a new paradigm for analyzing complex cellular systems. Our results suggest that the CellTok framework can extend the capabilities of LLMs to single-cell data, and suggests a direction for building more general mixed-modal models.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"CellTok presents a new multimodal LLM that tokenizes single-cell transcriptomics and integrates with biological text via early fusion, enabling generative cell analysis and Q&A, clearly meeting all criteria. | CellTok presents a multimodal LLM that tokenizes single-cell data via VQ-VAE and processes inputs autoregressively, demonstrating generative capabilities (cell generation, Q&A) and leveraging pretrained LLM knowledge, indicating a primary generative foundation model.","CellTok presents a new multimodal LLM that tokenizes single-cell transcriptomics and integrates with biological text via early fusion, enabling generative cell analysis and Q&A, clearly meeting all criteria.","CellTok presents a multimodal LLM that tokenizes single-cell data via VQ-VAE and processes inputs autoregressively, demonstrating generative capabilities (cell generation, Q&A) and leveraging pretrained LLM knowledge, indicating a primary generative foundation model.",,CellTok: Early-Fusion Multimodal Large Language Model for Single-Cell Transcriptomics via Tokenization,"Single-cell transcriptomic data provide a high-resolution view of cellular states and functions, offering critical insights into development, disease, and tissue heterogeneity. Existing foundation models for single-cell analysis typically embed cells as continuous vectors, limiting their generative flexibility and hindering integration with the knowledge accumulated in large language models (LLMs). In this work, we present CellTok, a multimodal LLM framework for unified analysis of scRNA-seq data and biological text. Each cell is tokenized into discrete codebook tokens via VQ-VAE and integrated into the LLM’s vocabulary using early fusion. This allows CellTok to process biological and textual inputs autoregressively, leveraging pretrained knowledge within LLMs to analyze cellular states and interactions. CellTok demonstrates strong performance in cell-level tasks such as annotation and generation, and enables previously unattainable large population-level tasks, including accurate prediction of intercellular communication networks, which is a key mechanism in tissue organization and disease. Furthermore, it retains interactive Q&A capabilities grounded in biomedical knowledge, offering a new paradigm for analyzing complex cellular systems. Our results suggest that the CellTok framework can extend the capabilities of LLMs to single-cell data, and suggests a direction for building more general mixed-modal models.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract introduces CellTok, a new multimodal LLM that combines scRNA‑seq tokenization with natural‑language inputs and enables Q&A and generation, indicating a primary model with both biological and text components and a substantive text‑bio bridge. | The abstract presents CellTok as an autoregressive multimodal LLM that tokenizes cells and generates outputs, indicating a generative architecture built on pretrained LLM knowledge.","The abstract introduces CellTok, a new multimodal LLM that combines scRNA‑seq tokenization with natural‑language inputs and enables Q&A and generation, indicating a primary model with both biological and text components and a substantive text‑bio bridge.","The abstract presents CellTok as an autoregressive multimodal LLM that tokenizes cells and generates outputs, indicating a generative architecture built on pretrained LLM knowledge.",,CellTok: Early-Fusion Multimodal Large Language Model for Single-Cell Transcriptomics via Tokenization,"Single-cell transcriptomic data provide a high-resolution view of cellular states and functions, offering critical insights into development, disease, and tissue heterogeneity. Existing foundation models for single-cell analysis typically embed cells as continuous vectors, limiting their generative flexibility and hindering integration with the knowledge accumulated in large language models (LLMs). In this work, we present CellTok, a multimodal LLM framework for unified analysis of scRNA-seq data and biological text. Each cell is tokenized into discrete codebook tokens via VQ-VAE and integrated into the LLM’s vocabulary using early fusion. This allows CellTok to process biological and textual inputs autoregressively, leveraging pretrained knowledge within LLMs to analyze cellular states and interactions. CellTok demonstrates strong performance in cell-level tasks such as annotation and generation, and enables previously unattainable large population-level tasks, including accurate prediction of intercellular communication networks, which is a key mechanism in tissue organization and disease. Furthermore, it retains interactive Q&A capabilities grounded in biomedical knowledge, offering a new paradigm for analyzing complex cellular systems. Our results suggest that the CellTok framework can extend the capabilities of LLMs to single-cell data, and suggests a direction for building more general mixed-modal models.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract introduces CellTok as a new multimodal LLM that jointly handles scRNA‑seq tokenized data and natural‑language text, showing a primary model with both biological and text components and a substantive text‑bio bridge. | The abstract reports an autoregressive multimodal LLM that tokenizes cells and generates outputs, showing a generative architecture built on pretrained LLM knowledge.","The abstract introduces CellTok as a new multimodal LLM that jointly handles scRNA‑seq tokenized data and natural‑language text, showing a primary model with both biological and text components and a substantive text‑bio bridge.","The abstract reports an autoregressive multimodal LLM that tokenizes cells and generates outputs, showing a generative architecture built on pretrained LLM knowledge.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement/cross_model_decisions.csv:562:1236,Language may be all omics needs: Harmonizing multimodal data for omics understanding with CellHermes,"Decoding cellular systems requires integrating diverse omics data, yet most models are trained from scratch on a single modality, restricting generalization. Here we present CellHermes, a biological language model that leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs) to integrate multimodal forms of omics data, such as transcriptomic profile and PPI network, through natural language for better omics understanding. By reformulating these datasets into question-answer pairs, CellHermes emulates multiple self-supervised learning paradigms within a single, universal, language-based framework with LORA fine-tuning on existing natural language-LLM, achieving comparable or even better performance compared to current single-cell foundation models. Within this framework, CellHermes functions as an encoder, a predictor and an explainer for supporting a range of downstream tasks. For encoder, CellHermes encodes biological entities with embeddings that enable accurate network discovery and cross-dataset generalization; For predictor, CellHermes unifies heterogeneous downstream tasks by translating them into natural language question-answers pairs, allowing a single model to perform multi-task prediction by instruction fine-tuning; For explainer, CellHermes explains molecular mechanisms by combining attention analysis with text-based reasoning, leveraging the interpretability and reasoning capabilities of LLMs. We evaluated the ability of CellHermes as an  encoder on representing genes and cells using 5 gene-level downstream tasks and 5 diverse single-cell datasets across different tissues compared with other single cell foundation models. We also evaluated CellHermes as a  predictor on BioUniBench, a new benchmark of 10 tasks across 7 databases for LLMs, including perturbation response, cell fitness estimation, gene-disease association, and cell type identification. All benchmarks suggest CellHermes can unify multiple biological tasks within one model without compromising performance. By applying CellHermes as an  explainer for a melanoma patients dataset, it can uncover potential key genes on tumor-reactive T cells. Together, CellHermes establishes natural language as a unifying medium for omics, offering a foundation that, in the future, potentially enables a more integrated and interpretable research loop of biological representation, prediction, and interpretability.",stable_include,Language may be all omics needs: Harmonizing multimodal data for omics understanding with CellHermes,"Decoding cellular systems requires integrating diverse omics data, yet most models are trained from scratch on a single modality, restricting generalization. Here we present CellHermes, a biological language model that leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs) to integrate multimodal forms of omics data, such as transcriptomic profile and PPI network, through natural language for better omics understanding. By reformulating these datasets into question-answer pairs, CellHermes emulates multiple self-supervised learning paradigms within a single, universal, language-based framework with LORA fine-tuning on existing natural language-LLM, achieving comparable or even better performance compared to current single-cell foundation models. Within this framework, CellHermes functions as an encoder, a predictor and an explainer for supporting a range of downstream tasks. For encoder, CellHermes encodes biological entities with embeddings that enable accurate network discovery and cross-dataset generalization; For predictor, CellHermes unifies heterogeneous downstream tasks by translating them into natural language question-answers pairs, allowing a single model to perform multi-task prediction by instruction fine-tuning; For explainer, CellHermes explains molecular mechanisms by combining attention analysis with text-based reasoning, leveraging the interpretability and reasoning capabilities of LLMs. We evaluated the ability of CellHermes as an  encoder on representing genes and cells using 5 gene-level downstream tasks and 5 diverse single-cell datasets across different tissues compared with other single cell foundation models. We also evaluated CellHermes as a  predictor on BioUniBench, a new benchmark of 10 tasks across 7 databases for LLMs, including perturbation response, cell fitness estimation, gene-disease association, and cell type identification. All benchmarks suggest CellHermes can unify multiple biological tasks within one model without compromising performance. By applying CellHermes as an  explainer for a melanoma patients dataset, it can uncover potential key genes on tumor-reactive T cells. Together, CellHermes establishes natural language as a unifying medium for omics, offering a foundation that, in the future, potentially enables a more integrated and interpretable research loop of biological representation, prediction, and interpretability.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"CellHermes is presented as a primary model that uses natural language question-answer pairs to integrate and process omics data, clearly satisfying both text component and text-bio bridge criteria. | CellHermes fine-tunes an existing LLM via LORA on question-answer pairs for multimodal omics tasks, demonstrating generative capabilities and foundation-model-like broad applicability.","CellHermes is presented as a primary model that uses natural language question-answer pairs to integrate and process omics data, clearly satisfying both text component and text-bio bridge criteria.","CellHermes fine-tunes an existing LLM via LORA on question-answer pairs for multimodal omics tasks, demonstrating generative capabilities and foundation-model-like broad applicability.",,Language may be all omics needs: Harmonizing multimodal data for omics understanding with CellHermes,"Decoding cellular systems requires integrating diverse omics data, yet most models are trained from scratch on a single modality, restricting generalization. Here we present CellHermes, a biological language model that leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs) to integrate multimodal forms of omics data, such as transcriptomic profile and PPI network, through natural language for better omics understanding. By reformulating these datasets into question-answer pairs, CellHermes emulates multiple self-supervised learning paradigms within a single, universal, language-based framework with LORA fine-tuning on existing natural language-LLM, achieving comparable or even better performance compared to current single-cell foundation models. Within this framework, CellHermes functions as an encoder, a predictor and an explainer for supporting a range of downstream tasks. For encoder, CellHermes encodes biological entities with embeddings that enable accurate network discovery and cross-dataset generalization; For predictor, CellHermes unifies heterogeneous downstream tasks by translating them into natural language question-answers pairs, allowing a single model to perform multi-task prediction by instruction fine-tuning; For explainer, CellHermes explains molecular mechanisms by combining attention analysis with text-based reasoning, leveraging the interpretability and reasoning capabilities of LLMs. We evaluated the ability of CellHermes as an  encoder on representing genes and cells using 5 gene-level downstream tasks and 5 diverse single-cell datasets across different tissues compared with other single cell foundation models. We also evaluated CellHermes as a  predictor on BioUniBench, a new benchmark of 10 tasks across 7 databases for LLMs, including perturbation response, cell fitness estimation, gene-disease association, and cell type identification. All benchmarks suggest CellHermes can unify multiple biological tasks within one model without compromising performance. By applying CellHermes as an  explainer for a melanoma patients dataset, it can uncover potential key genes on tumor-reactive T cells. Together, CellHermes establishes natural language as a unifying medium for omics, offering a foundation that, in the future, potentially enables a more integrated and interpretable research loop of biological representation, prediction, and interpretability.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"CellHermes is a primary model that integrates omics data via natural language question-answer pairs and text-based reasoning, clearly satisfying all inclusion criteria. | CellHermes fine-tunes a pretrained LLM with LORA on omics question-answer pairs, enabling generative prediction and explanation across multiple tasks, demonstrating foundation-model characteristics.","CellHermes is a primary model that integrates omics data via natural language question-answer pairs and text-based reasoning, clearly satisfying all inclusion criteria.","CellHermes fine-tunes a pretrained LLM with LORA on omics question-answer pairs, enabling generative prediction and explanation across multiple tasks, demonstrating foundation-model characteristics.",,Language may be all omics needs: Harmonizing multimodal data for omics understanding with CellHermes,"Decoding cellular systems requires integrating diverse omics data, yet most models are trained from scratch on a single modality, restricting generalization. Here we present CellHermes, a biological language model that leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs) to integrate multimodal forms of omics data, such as transcriptomic profile and PPI network, through natural language for better omics understanding. By reformulating these datasets into question-answer pairs, CellHermes emulates multiple self-supervised learning paradigms within a single, universal, language-based framework with LORA fine-tuning on existing natural