./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.csv:64:62,TissueNarrator: Generative Modeling of Spatial Transcriptomics with Large Language Models.,tissuenarrator generative modeling of spatial transcriptomics with large language models,10.1101/2025.11.24.690325,,41394628.0,,"The intricate spatial organization and molecular communication among cells are fundamental to multicellular systems. Spatial transcriptomics (ST) enables gene expression profiling while preserving spatial context, providing rich data for studying cellular interactions and tissue dynamics. However, most existing computational approaches focus on embedding-based tasks and provide limited generative capacity for simulating cell behavior  in situ . Moreover, accurately interpreting spatial interactions requires extensive biological knowledge, which current models do not incorporate. Here, we introduce T  issue N  arrator , a framework that reformulates spatial omics analysis as a language modeling problem. By representing tissue sections as spatial sentences – rank-based gene lists augmented with spatial coordinates and metadata – T  issue N  arrator leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs) to learn spatially conditioned gene expression patterns. The model generates realistic, context-aware cellular profiles, predicts intercellular interactions, and performs  in silico perturbation analyses. Across multiple ST technologies (MERFISH, Perturb-FISH, and CosMx SMI), T  issue N  arrator achieves superior quantitative performance and recovers biologically meaning-ful ligand–receptor and signaling pathways. Furthermore, a conversational inference mode enables natural-language querying of tissue organization. By integrating pretrained biological knowledge with spatial context, T  issue N  arrator establishes a new, scalable generative paradigm for modeling, simulating, and reasoning about tissue systems.",Liu Sizhe; Tang Junjie; Ma Jian; Liang Shaoheng,2025.0,bioRxiv : the preprint server for biology,,,"['biorxiv_medrxiv', 'google_scholar', 'pubmed', 'semantic_scholar']",4,['10.1101/2025.11.24.690325'],4,,,,,62,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""yes"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""conversational inference mode enables natural-language querying of tissue organization"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""reformulates spatial omics analysis as a language modeling problem"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents TissueNarrator as a new framework that treats spatial transcriptomics as language, uses pretrained LLMs, and provides natural-language querying, satisfying primary model, biological modality, text component, and text‑bio bridge criteria.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,none,none,conversational inference mode enables natural-language querying of tissue organization,reformulates spatial omics analysis as a language modeling problem,not_assessed,none,"The abstract presents TissueNarrator as a new framework that treats spatial transcriptomics as language, uses pretrained LLMs, and provides natural-language querying, satisfying primary model, biological modality, text component, and text‑bio bridge criteria.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""yes"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""model generates realistic, context-aware cellular profiles"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_include"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents TissueNarrator, a new framework that leverages pretrained LLMs to generate realistic cellular profiles and simulate tissue dynamics, indicating a generative architecture with foundation-model pretraining.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,yes,none,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,"model generates realistic, context-aware cellular profiles",clear_include,"The abstract presents TissueNarrator, a new framework that leverages pretrained LLMs to generate realistic cellular profiles and simulate tissue dynamics, indicating a generative architecture with foundation-model pretraining.",PASS,PASS,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.csv:145:143,Interpretable Transfer Learning for Cancer Drug Resistance: Candidate Target Identification.,interpretable transfer learning for cancer drug resistance candidate target identification,10.3390/cimb47090753,,41020875.0,,"Tumor drug resistance exhibits substantial heterogeneity across cancer types, reflecting distinct molecular mechanisms in each malignancy. To characterize this complexity, we developed a pan-cancer transfer learning framework that integrates bulk RNA-seq data with a residual variational autoencoder (Res VAE) backbone. Five models were trained on the Genomics of Drug Sensitivity in Cancer (GDSC) dataset, which includes drug response profiles for 72 chemotherapeutic agents. Among them, three models are specially designed by incorporating variational autoencoders and large pretrained models (LLMs): the LLM large VAE (VAE_LL), the LLM small VAE (VAE_LS), and the LLM distillation VAE (VAE_LD). Random Forest (RF) and eXtreme Gradient Boosting (XGB) were included as ensemble learning baselines. After internal cross-validation, the top four models (VAE_LL, VAE_LD, XGB, and RF) were applied to five representative TCGA cohorts comprising 1,836 patients. For each cancer type, resistance to nine clinically relevant first-line drugs was modeled, resulting in 180 drug–cancer prediction tasks. Among all models, VAE_LD achieved the best overall performance, with a mean AUC of 0.81 and an F1 score of 0.92 on the GDSC benchmark, and maintained strong predictive power in the clinical validation phase. Interpretation analyses identified tumor-specific resistance biomarkers with clinical significance. In lung adenocarcinoma, elevated expression of TFF1 was repeatedly associated with resistance to Gefitinib and correlated with poor patient prognosis, indicating its potential as a therapeutic target. In glioblastoma, OPALIN, LTF, IL2RA, and SLC17A7 were implicated in Temozolomide resistance through pathways related to epithelial differentiation and angiogenesis. In conclusion, the VAE_LD model offers a high-performing and interpretable approach for predicting drug resistance across multiple tumor types. It supports the identification of clinically actionable biomarkers and provides a robust framework for precision oncology applications.",Zhang Wenjie; Wu Xisong; Chen Liang; Wan Xinyue,2025.0,Current issues in molecular biology,,,"['pubmed', 'scopus', 'semantic_scholar']",3,['10.3390/cimb47090753'],3,,,,,143,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""unclear"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""unclear"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""text_component_unclear"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""large pretrained models (LLMs)"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The work presents new VAE models (primary model) using bulk RNA‑seq data (bio modality), but the abstract only mentions incorporating LLMs without specifying natural‑language text input or output, leaving the text component and text‑bio bridge unclear.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,unclear,unclear,none,text_component_unclear,large pretrained models (LLMs),none,not_assessed,none,"The work presents new VAE models (primary model) using bulk RNA‑seq data (bio modality), but the abstract only mentions incorporating LLMs without specifying natural‑language text input or output, leaving the text component and text‑bio bridge unclear.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""yes"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""variational autoencoder (Res VAE) backbone"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract introduces VAE-based models (a generative architecture) and explicitly incorporates large pretrained LLMs, fitting the criteria for a primary generative text‑bio foundational model.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,yes,none,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,variational autoencoder (Res VAE) backbone,none,"The abstract introduces VAE-based models (a generative architecture) and explicitly incorporates large pretrained LLMs, fitting the criteria for a primary generative text‑bio foundational model.",UNCERTAIN,PASS,True,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""generative_model_present"": ""yes"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC2_no_text_component"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""variational autoencoder (Res VAE) backbone"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents a VAE‑based model for drug‑resistance prediction using RNA‑seq data and mentions large pretrained LLMs, but it contains no natural‑language text input, output, or alignment, so there is no text‑bio bridge; therefore the paper is excluded under EC2_no_text_component.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,yes,yes,EC2_no_text_component,none,none,none,variational autoencoder (Res VAE) backbone,clear_exclude,"The abstract presents a VAE‑based model for drug‑resistance prediction using RNA‑seq data and mentions large pretrained LLMs, but it contains no natural‑language text input, output, or alignment, so there is no text‑bio bridge; therefore the paper is excluded under EC2_no_text_component."
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.csv:737:719,Improving polygenic prediction from whole-genome sequencing data by leveraging predicted epigenomic features,improving polygenic prediction from wholegenome sequencing data by leveraging predicted epigenomic features,10.1073/pnas.2419202122,,,,"Significance Epi-PRS improves polygenic risk scoring by integrating genomic large language models (LLMs) to impute epigenomic signals as intermediaries between genotype and phenotype. This approach enables a more comprehensive assessment of personal variant impacts by incorporating nonlinear models, rare variants, and regulatory mechanisms. By leveraging the power of genomic LLM trained on massive amount of reference epigenomics data, Epi-PRS has demonstrated superior performance over existing PRS methods in predicting genetic risk for breast cancer and diabetes in UK Biobank data. These results highlight the potential of Epi-PRS to improve disease risk modeling and advance the field of precision medicine.",Zeng W.,2025.0,Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America,2025-06-17,,['scopus'],1,['10.1073/pnas.2419202122'],1,s2_doi,,,,697,"{""paper_type"": ""application_wrapper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""application_wrapper"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents a polygenic risk scoring method that leverages a genomic LLM to impute epigenomic signals, with no mention of natural‑language text or a text‑bio mapping; thus it functions as an application wrapper rather than a primary text‑bio generative model.""}",application_wrapper,yes,no,no,application_wrapper,none,none,none,not_assessed,none,"The abstract presents a polygenic risk scoring method that leverages a genomic LLM to impute epigenomic signals, with no mention of natural‑language text or a text‑bio mapping; thus it functions as an application wrapper rather than a primary text‑bio generative model.","{""paper_type"": ""application_wrapper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""application_wrapper"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""wrapper_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes Epi-PRS that uses a pretrained genomic LLM to impute epigenomic signals for polygenic risk scoring, without presenting a new generative architecture; thus it is an application wrapper rather than a primary generative model.""}",application_wrapper,no,yes,application_wrapper,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,wrapper_boundary,"The abstract describes Epi-PRS that uses a pretrained genomic LLM to impute epigenomic signals for polygenic risk scoring, without presenting a new generative architecture; thus it is an application wrapper rather than a primary generative model.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.csv:998:952,A BERT-GNN Approach for Metastatic Breast Cancer Prediction Using Histopathology Reports,a bertgnn approach for metastatic breast cancer prediction using histopathology reports,10.3390/diagnostics14131365,,,,"Metastatic breast cancer (MBC) continues to be a leading cause of cancer-related deaths among women. This work introduces an innovative non-invasive breast cancer classification model designed to improve the identification of cancer metastases. While this study marks the initial exploration into predicting MBC, additional investigations are essential to validate the occurrence of MBC. Our approach combines the strengths of large language models (LLMs), specifically the bidirectional encoder representations from transformers (BERT) model, with the powerful capabilities of graph neural networks (GNNs) to predict MBC patients based on their histopathology reports. This paper introduces a BERT-GNN approach for metastatic breast cancer prediction (BG-MBC) that integrates graph information derived from the BERT model. In this model, nodes are constructed from patient medical records, while BERT embeddings are employed to vectorise representations of the words in histopathology reports, thereby capturing semantic information crucial for classification by employing three distinct approaches (namely univariate selection, extra trees classifier for feature importance, and Shapley values to identify the features that have the most significant impact). Identifying the most crucial 30 features out of 676 generated as embeddings during model training, our model further enhances its predictive capabilities. The BG-MBC model achieves outstanding accuracy, with a detection rate of 0.98 and an area under curve (AUC) of 0.98, in identifying MBC patients. This remarkable performance is credited to the model’s utilisation of attention scores generated by the LLM from histopathology reports, effectively capturing pertinent features for classification.",Basaad  A.,2024.0,Diagnostics,2024-07-01,,['scopus'],1,['10.3390/diagnostics14131365'],1,s2_doi,,,,880,"{""paper_type"": ""application_wrapper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""no"", ""text_component_present"": ""yes"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""application_wrapper"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""BERT embeddings are employed to vectorise representations of the words in histopathology reports"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""predict MBC patients based on their histopathology reports"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The work introduces a BERT‑GNN classifier that uses BERT embeddings of histopathology report text as features for a downstream graph neural network, i.e., it wraps an existing LLM rather than presenting a novel generative text‑bio foundational model.""}",application_wrapper,no,yes,yes,application_wrapper,none,BERT embeddings are employed to vectorise representations of the words in histopathology reports,predict MBC patients based on their histopathology reports,not_assessed,none,"The work introduces a BERT‑GNN classifier that uses BERT embeddings of histopathology report text as features for a downstream graph neural network, i.e., it wraps an existing LLM rather than presenting a novel generative text‑bio foundational model.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC3_not_generative"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract proposes a BERT‑GNN classification model for metastatic breast cancer prediction and does not mention any generative mechanism (e.g., decoder, autoregressive generation). It uses BERT embeddings, indicating use of a pretrained LLM, but the work is purely predictive.""}",primary_model_paper,no,yes,EC3_not_generative,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract proposes a BERT‑GNN classification model for metastatic breast cancer prediction and does not mention any generative mechanism (e.g., decoder, autoregressive generation). It uses BERT embeddings, indicating use of a pretrained LLM, but the work is purely predictive.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,True,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""yes"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""yes"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC3_not_generative"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""BERT embeddings are employed to vectorise representations of the words in histopathology reports"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""nodes are constructed from patient medical records, while BERT embeddings ... capture semantic information ... for classification"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes a BERT‑GNN classification model using text embeddings and patient record graphs, with no indication of generative capabilities; therefore it is excluded under EC3_not_generative.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,no,yes,EC3_not_generative,none,BERT embeddings are employed to vectorise representations of the words in histopathology reports,"nodes are constructed from patient medical records, while BERT embeddings ... capture semantic information ... for classification",none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a BERT‑GNN classification model using text embeddings and patient record graphs, with no indication of generative capabilities; therefore it is excluded under EC3_not_generative."
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.csv:1402:1236,Language may be all omics needs: Harmonizing multimodal data for omics understanding with CellHermes,language may be all omics needs harmonizing multimodal data for omics understanding with cellhermes,10.1101/2025.11.07.687322,,,,"Decoding cellular systems requires integrating diverse omics data, yet most models are trained from scratch on a single modality, restricting generalization. Here we present CellHermes, a biological language model that leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs) to integrate multimodal forms of omics data, such as transcriptomic profile and PPI network, through natural language for better omics understanding. By reformulating these datasets into question-answer pairs, CellHermes emulates multiple self-supervised learning paradigms within a single, universal, language-based framework with LORA fine-tuning on existing natural language-LLM, achieving comparable or even better performance compared to current single-cell foundation models. Within this framework, CellHermes functions as an encoder, a predictor and an explainer for supporting a range of downstream tasks. For encoder, CellHermes encodes biological entities with embeddings that enable accurate network discovery and cross-dataset generalization; For predictor, CellHermes unifies heterogeneous downstream tasks by translating them into natural language question-answers pairs, allowing a single model to perform multi-task prediction by instruction fine-tuning; For explainer, CellHermes explains molecular mechanisms by combining attention analysis with text-based reasoning, leveraging the interpretability and reasoning capabilities of LLMs. We evaluated the ability of CellHermes as an  encoder on representing genes and cells using 5 gene-level downstream tasks and 5 diverse single-cell datasets across different tissues compared with other single cell foundation models. We also evaluated CellHermes as a  predictor on BioUniBench, a new benchmark of 10 tasks across 7 databases for LLMs, including perturbation response, cell fitness estimation, gene-disease association, and cell type identification. All benchmarks suggest CellHermes can unify multiple biological tasks within one model without compromising performance. By applying CellHermes as an  explainer for a melanoma patients dataset, it can uncover potential key genes on tumor-reactive T cells. Together, CellHermes establishes natural language as a unifying medium for omics, offering a foundation that, in the future, potentially enables a more integrated and interpretable research loop of biological representation, prediction, and interpretability.",Yicheng Gao; Weixu Wang; Yuheng Zhao; Kejing Dong; Caihua Shan; Weizhong Zheng; Till Richter; Zekai Li; Siming Chen; Fabian J. Theis; Qi Liu,2025.0,bioRxiv,2025-11-28,,"['biorxiv_medrxiv', 'google_scholar', 'semantic_scholar']",3,['10.1101/2025.11.07.687322'],3,,,,,1125,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""yes"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""reformulating these datasets into question‑answer pairs"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""translating them into natural language question‑answers pairs"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents CellHermes as a new model that uses pretrained LLMs and natural‑language QA reformulation to integrate omics data, indicating a substantive text component and a clear text‑bio bridge.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,none,none,reformulating these datasets into question‑answer pairs,translating them into natural language question‑answers pairs,not_assessed,none,"The abstract presents CellHermes as a new model that uses pretrained LLMs and natural‑language QA reformulation to integrate omics data, indicating a substantive text component and a clear text‑bio bridge.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents CellHermes as a new model built on pretrained LLMs for encoding, prediction, and explanation, without mentioning any decoder or generative mechanism; it clearly leverages a pretrained LLM, providing foundation‑model evidence.""}",primary_model_paper,no,yes,none,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,none,"The abstract presents CellHermes as a new model built on pretrained LLMs for encoding, prediction, and explanation, without mentioning any decoder or generative mechanism; it clearly leverages a pretrained LLM, providing foundation‑model evidence.",PASS,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.csv:1845:1403,Leveraging genomic large language models to enhance causal genotype-brain-clinical pathways in Alzheimer's disease,leveraging genomic large language models to enhance causal genotypebrainclinical pathways in alzheimers disease,10.1101/2024.10.03.24314824,,,,"Abstract
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.csv:1846:                Genome-wide association studies (GWAS) have identified numerous Alzheimer’s disease (AD)- associated variants. However, how these variants contribute to the etiology of AD remains largely elusive. Recent advances in genomic large language models (LLMs) offer new opportunities to interpret the genetic variation observed in personal genome. In this study, we propose epiBrainLLM, a novel computational framework that leverages genomic LLM to enhance our understanding of the causal pathways from genotypes to brain measures to AD-related clinical phenotypes. epiBrainLLM will first convert the personal DNA sequence into a diverse set of genomic and epigenomic features using a pretrained genomic LLM and then use these features to further predict phenotypes. Across various experimental settings, epiBrainLLM significantly improves causal analysis compared to traditional genotype association approach. We conclude that epiBrainLLM provides a novel perspective for understanding the regulatory mechanisms underlying the AD disease etiology, potentially offering insights into complex disease mechanisms beyond AD.",Q. Liu; W. Zeng; H. Zhu; L. Li; W. Wong,2024.0,medRxiv,2024-10-04,,['semantic_scholar'],1,['10.1101/2024.10.03.24314824'],1,crossref,,,,1284,"{""paper_type"": ""application_wrapper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""application_wrapper"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents epiBrainLLM, which uses a pretrained genomic LLM to extract features from DNA and predict phenotypes, without describing a new model or natural-language text component, thus it is an application wrapper lacking a substantive text-bio bridge.""}",application_wrapper,yes,no,no,application_wrapper,none,none,none,not_assessed,none,"The abstract presents epiBrainLLM, which uses a pretrained genomic LLM to extract features from DNA and predict phenotypes, without describing a new model or natural-language text component, thus it is an application wrapper lacking a substantive text-bio bridge.","{""paper_type"": ""application_wrapper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""application_wrapper"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""wrapper_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents epiBrainLLM as a framework that leverages a pretrained genomic LLM to generate features and predict phenotypes, without describing its own generative mechanism; therefore it is an application wrapper rather than a primary generative model.""}",application_wrapper,no,yes,application_wrapper,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,wrapper_boundary,"The abstract presents epiBrainLLM as a framework that leverages a pretrained genomic LLM to generate features and predict phenotypes, without describing its own generative mechanism; therefore it is an application wrapper rather than a primary generative model.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.csv:1888:1421,How to improve polygenic prediction from whole-genome sequencing data by leveraging predicted epigenomic features?,how to improve polygenic prediction from wholegenome sequencing data by leveraging predicted epigenomic features,10.1101/2024.10.04.24314860,,,,"Polygenic risk scores (PRS) are crucial in genetics for predicting individual susceptibility to complex diseases by aggregating the effects of numerous genetic variants. Whole-genome sequencing (WGS) has revolutionized our ability to detect rare and even de novo variants, creating an exciting opportunity for developing new PRS methods that can effectively leverage rare variants and capture the complex relationships among different variants. Furthermore, regulatory mechanisms play a crucial role in gene expression and disease manifestation, offering avenues to further enhance the performance and interpretation of PRS predictions. Through simulation studies, we highlighted aspects where current PRS methods face challenges when applied to WGS data, aiming to shed light on potential opportunities for further improvement. To address these challenges, we developed Epi-PRS, an approach that leverages the power of genomic large language models (LLM) to impute epigenomic signals across diverse cellular contexts, for use as intermediate variables between genotype and phenotype. A pretrained LLM is employed to transform genotypes into epigenomic signals using personal diploid sequences as inputs, and the genetic risk is then estimated based on the imputed personal epigenomic signals. Epi-PRS enhances the assessment of personal variant impacts, enabling a comprehensive and holistic consideration of genotypic and regulatory information within large genomic regions. Our simulation results demonstrated that incorporating the nuanced effects of non-linear models, rare variants, and regulatory information can provide more precise PRS prediction and better understanding of genetic risk. Applying Epi-PRS to real data from the UK Biobank, our results further showed that Epi-PRS significantly outperforms existing PRS methods in two major diseases: breast cancer and diabetes. This study suggests that PRS methods can benefit from incorporating non-linear models, rare variants, and regulatory information, highlighting the potential for significant advancements in disease risk modeling and enhancing the understanding of precision medicine. Significance Statement Epi-PRS improves polygenic risk scoring by integrating genomic large language models (LLMs) to impute epigenomic signals as intermediaries between genotype and phenotype. This approach enables a more comprehensive assessment of personal variant impacts by incorporating non-linear models, rare variants, and regulatory mechanisms. By leveraging the power of genomic LLM trained on massive amount of reference epigenomics data, Epi-PRS has demonstrated superior performance over existing PRS methods in predicting genetic risk for breast cancer and diabetes in UK Biobank data. These results highlight the potential of Epi-PRS to improve disease risk modeling and advance the field of precision medicine.",W. Zeng; H. Guo; Q. Liu; W. Wong,2024.0,medRxiv,2024-10-06,https://www.medrxiv.org/content/medrxiv/early/2024/10/06/2024.10.04.24314860.full.pdf,"['biorxiv_medrxiv', 'semantic_scholar']",2,['10.1101/2024.10.04.24314860'],2,,,,,1301,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC2_no_text_component"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents a new PRS method using a genomic LLM to impute epigenomic signals, but it contains no mention of natural-language text or a text‑bio mapping, so it lacks a substantive text component and bridge.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,EC2_no_text_component,none,none,none,not_assessed,clear_exclude,"The abstract presents a new PRS method using a genomic LLM to impute epigenomic signals, but it contains no mention of natural-language text or a text‑bio mapping, so it lacks a substantive text component and bridge.","{""paper_type"": ""application_wrapper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""yes"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""application_wrapper"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""A pretrained LLM is employed to transform genotypes into epigenomic signals"", ""boundary_case"": ""wrapper_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""Epi-PRS uses an existing pretrained genomic LLM to generate epigenomic signals for PRS, representing a wrapper around a generative model rather than a novel primary model.""}",application_wrapper,yes,yes,application_wrapper,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,A pretrained LLM is employed to transform genotypes into epigenomic signals,wrapper_boundary,"Epi-PRS uses an existing pretrained genomic LLM to generate epigenomic signals for PRS, representing a wrapper around a generative model rather than a novel primary model.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,True,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""generative_model_present"": ""yes"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC2_no_text_component"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""genomic large language models (LLM) to impute epigenomic signals"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""genomic large language models (LLM) to impute epigenomic signals"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""impute epigenomic signals"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes a genomic LLM used to predict epigenomic signals from DNA, with no natural‑language text input or output, so there is no text component or text‑bio bridge; thus the paper is excluded under EC2_no_text_component.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,yes,yes,EC2_no_text_component,none,genomic large language models (LLM) to impute epigenomic signals,genomic large language models (LLM) to impute epigenomic signals,impute epigenomic signals,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a genomic LLM used to predict epigenomic signals from DNA, with no natural‑language text input or output, so there is no text component or text‑bio bridge; thus the paper is excluded under EC2_no_text_component."
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.csv:2257:1565,MetagenBERT: a Transformer-based Architecture using Foundational genomic Large Language Models for novel Metagenome Representation,metagenbert a transformerbased architecture using foundational genomic large language models for novel metagenome representation,,,,2601.03295v1,"Metagenomic disease prediction commonly relies on species abundance tables derived from large, incomplete reference catalogs, constraining resolution and discarding valuable information contained in DNA reads. To overcome these limitations, we introduce MetagenBERT, a Transformer based framework that produces end to end metagenome embeddings directly from raw DNA sequences, without taxonomic or functional annotations. Reads are embedded using foundational genomic language models (DNABERT2 and the microbiome specialized DNABERTMS), then aggregated through a scalable clustering strategy based on FAISS accelerated KMeans. Each metagenome is represented as a cluster abundance vector summarizing the distribution of its embedded reads. We evaluate this approach on five benchmark gut microbiome datasets (Cirrhosis, T2D, Obesity, IBD, CRC). MetagenBERT achieves competitive or superior AUC performance relative to species abundance baselines across most tasks. Concatenating both representations further improves prediction, demonstrating complementarity between taxonomic and embedding derived signals. Clustering remains robust when applied to as little as 10% of reads, highlighting substantial redundancy in metagenomes and enabling major computational gains. We additionally introduce MetagenBERT Glob Mcardis, a cross cohort variant trained on the large, phenotypically diverse MetaCardis cohort and transferred to other datasets, retaining predictive signal including for unseen phenotypes, indicating the feasibility of a foundation model for metagenome representation. Robustness analyses (PERMANOVA, PERMDISP, entropy) show consistent separation of different states across subsamples. Overall, MetagenBERT provides a scalable, annotation free representation of metagenomes pointing toward future phenotype aware generalization across heterogeneous cohorts and sequencing technologies.",Gaspar Roy; Eugeni Belda; Baptiste Hennecart; Y. Chevaleyre; E. Prifti; Jean-Daniel Zucker,2026.0,,2026-01-05,,"['arxiv', 'semantic_scholar']",2,[],2,,,,,1444,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents MetagenBERT, a new transformer architecture that processes raw DNA reads via genomic language models, with no mention of natural-language text, prompts, or captions, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,none,none,none,none,not_assessed,none,"The abstract presents MetagenBERT, a new transformer architecture that processes raw DNA reads via genomic language models, with no mention of natural-language text, prompts, or captions, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents MetagenBERT, a Transformer that encodes raw DNA reads using existing genomic LLMs; it does not describe a generative decoder, but it leverages large pretrained models and shows transferability, indicating foundation-model characteristics.""}",primary_model_paper,no,yes,none,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,none,"The abstract presents MetagenBERT, a Transformer that encodes raw DNA reads using existing genomic LLMs; it does not describe a generative decoder, but it leverages large pretrained models and shows transferability, indicating foundation-model characteristics.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.csv:2404:1608,Universal Single-Cell Transcriptomic Aging Clock powered by LLMs reveals targets to slow cellular aging,universal singlecell transcriptomic aging clock powered by llms reveals targets to slow cellular aging,10.1093/geroni/igaf122.4305,,,,"Abstract Large language models such as GPT have shown impressive performance in capturing structure and meaning from natural language. We adapt this capability to biology by representing single-cell transcriptomes as ordered sequences of gene names, analogous to sentences in text. Each cell’s expression profile is converted into a “cell sentence,” enabling pretrained language models to be fine-tuned directly on single-cell data for age prediction. Trained on millions of cells spanning diverse tissues and life stages, the model learns a universal latent representation of aging. It captures both cell-type–specific trajectories and conserved molecular signatures, generalizing across tissues and species. Unlike other foundational models that must be trained from scratch on omics data, our framework leverages already well-validated LLMs, taking advantage of their robust priors while avoiding the cost and instability of building new architectures. The resulting clock achieves state-of-the-art accuracy in predicting biological age at single-cell resolution. Embeddings highlight aging-associated processes such as immune activation, proteostasis decline, and metabolic shifts, aligning with known hallmarks of aging. Importantly, the model can also identify genes whose modulation decreases predicted transcriptional age, nominating candidate targets for therapeutic intervention. These features make the approach valuable both for measuring aging and for prioritizing interventions that may slow or reverse it. This work establishes the first universal, multi-tissue aging clock at single-cell resolution, demonstrating that pretrained language models can be directly adapted to biological data to map, measure, and potentially modify cellular aging.",Raghav Sehgal,2025.0,Innovation in aging,2025-12-01,,['semantic_scholar'],1,['10.1093/geroni/igaf122.4305'],1,,,,,1486,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""representing single-cell transcriptomes as ordered sequences of gene names, analogous to sentences in text"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""representing single-cell transcriptomes as ordered sequences of gene names... fine‑tuned directly on single-cell data"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The work presents a new model (primary_model_paper) that uses single‑cell transcriptomic data (bio modality). The only text‑like element is gene‑name sequencing, not natural language, so there is no substantive text component nor a natural‑language‑bio bridge.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,none,none,"representing single-cell transcriptomes as ordered sequences of gene names, analogous to sentences in text",representing single-cell transcriptomes as ordered sequences of gene names... fine‑tuned directly on single-cell data,not_assessed,none,"The work presents a new model (primary_model_paper) that uses single‑cell transcriptomic data (bio modality). The only text‑like element is gene‑name sequencing, not natural language, so there is no substantive text component nor a natural‑language‑bio bridge.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes fine‑tuning a pretrained LLM to predict cellular age, with no mention of a generative mechanism; it cites leveraging a large language model, indicating foundation‑model characteristics.""}",primary_model_paper,no,yes,none,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes fine‑tuning a pretrained LLM to predict cellular age, with no mention of a generative mechanism; it cites leveraging a large language model, indicating foundation‑model characteristics.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.csv:2595:1693,TridentTCR: Predicting T Cell Receptor Specificity for Antigens and Autoimmune-Related Targets via a Topology-Aware Graph and Large Language Model,tridenttcr predicting t cell receptor specificity for antigens and autoimmunerelated targets via a topologyaware graph and large language model,10.1101/2025.06.27.661632,,,,"T cells recognize and eliminate diseased cells by binding their T cell receptors (TCRs) to short endogenous peptides presented on the cell surface, commonly referred to as antigens. Accurate prediction of antigen–TCR specificity is essential for advancing T cell-based immunotherapies while minimizing autoimmune toxicity. However, current computational methods struggle to generalize across diverse TCR repertoires and reliably differentiate therapeutic antigens from autoimmune-related antigens (arAgs). Here, we introduce TridentTCR, a computational framework integrating a pretrained large language model (LLM) for high-fidelity sequence embedding with a topology-aware graph neural network to capture structural insights from the antigen–TCR interaction network. TridentTCR significantly advances TCR specificity prediction by enabling robust trinary classification, distinguishing TCR interactions involving general antigens, arAgs, and non-binding. Additionally, TridentTCR outperforms existing state-of-the-art models in binary classification (binding vs. non-binding). Validation on independent clinical datasets demonstrated strong generalization capability for unseen TCRs and antigens. In a disease-specific context, TridentTCR identifies notable cross-reactivity between ZnT8 186-194 -specific TCRs and a mimotope from Bacteroides stercoris , suggesting that T cell cross-reactivity may contribute to the initiation of type 1 diabetes. Furthermore, we introduced a quantitative metric, antigenic immune response entropy (AIRE), which leverages TridentTCR predictions alongside clonotype frequencies and repertoire diversity to precisely quantify antigen-specific immune responses from single-cell profiling data. Collectively, TridentTCR provides an interpretable and clinically relevant tool, enabling improved understanding of TCR specificity, cross-reactivity, and off-target autoimmune risks in clinical immunotherapy.",Rui Niu; Xiaoying Kong; Jiren Zhou; Yanli Li; Xuequn Shang,2025.0,bioRxiv,2025-06-27,,"['biorxiv_medrxiv', 'semantic_scholar']",2,['10.1101/2025.06.27.661632'],2,,,,,1566,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract introduces a new computational framework (TridentTCR) for TCR‑antigen specificity prediction, indicating a primary model paper. It centers on biological sequences (TCRs, antigens) but mentions only a pretrained LLM used for sequence embedding, without natural‑language text, prompts, or documents, so no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge is evident.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,none,none,none,none,not_assessed,none,"The abstract introduces a new computational framework (TridentTCR) for TCR‑antigen specificity prediction, indicating a primary model paper. It centers on biological sequences (TCRs, antigens) but mentions only a pretrained LLM used for sequence embedding, without natural‑language text, prompts, or documents, so no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge is evident.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC3_not_generative"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes TridentTCR as a prediction (trinary/binary classification) framework and mentions no decoder, autoregressive, or other generative mechanism; it only uses a pretrained LLM for embeddings.""}",primary_model_paper,no,yes,EC3_not_generative,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes TridentTCR as a prediction (trinary/binary classification) framework and mentions no decoder, autoregressive, or other generative mechanism; it only uses a pretrained LLM for embeddings.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.csv:3132:1969,Cell2Text: Multimodal LLM for Generating Single-Cell Descriptions from RNA-Seq Data,cell2text multimodal llm for generating singlecell descriptions from rnaseq data,10.48550/arXiv.2509.24840,,,2509.24840,"Single-cell RNA sequencing has transformed biology by enabling the measurement of gene expression at cellular resolution, providing information for cell types, states, and disease contexts. Recently, single-cell foundation models have emerged as powerful tools for learning transferable representations directly from expression profiles, improving performance on classification and clustering tasks. However, these models are limited to discrete prediction heads, which collapse cellular complexity into predefined labels that fail to capture the richer, contextual explanations biologists need. We introduce Cell2Text, a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA-seq profiles into structured natural language descriptions. By integrating gene-level embeddings from single-cell foundation models with pretrained large language models, Cell2Text generates coherent summaries that capture cellular identity, tissue origin, disease associations, and pathway activity, generalizing to unseen cells. Empirically, Cell2Text outperforms baselines on classification accuracy, demonstrates strong ontological consistency using PageRank-based similarity metrics, and achieves high semantic fidelity in text generation. These results demonstrate that coupling expression data with natural language offers both stronger predictive performance and inherently interpretable outputs, pointing to a scalable path for label-efficient characterization of unseen cells.",Oussama Kharouiche; Aris Markogiannakis; Xiao Fei; Michail Chatzianastasis; M. Vazirgiannis,2025.0,arXiv.org,2025-09-29,,"['arxiv', 'google_scholar', 'semantic_scholar']",3,['10.48550/arXiv.2509.24840'],3,,,,,1837,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""yes"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""generates coherent summaries ... structured natural language descriptions"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""translates scRNA-seq profiles into structured natural language descriptions"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents Cell2Text as a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA‑seq data into natural‑language descriptions, showing a new primary model with both a biological modality and a substantive text component forming a clear text‑bio bridge.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,none,none,generates coherent summaries ... structured natural language descriptions,translates scRNA-seq profiles into structured natural language descriptions,not_assessed,none,"The abstract presents Cell2Text as a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA‑seq data into natural‑language descriptions, showing a new primary model with both a biological modality and a substantive text component forming a clear text‑bio bridge.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""yes"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA‑seq profiles into structured natural language descriptions"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_include"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents Cell2Text as a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA‑seq data into natural language summaries, showing a generative decoder mechanism, and it leverages gene‑level embeddings from single‑cell foundation models and pretrained LLMs, indicating foundation‑model characteristics.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,yes,none,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA‑seq profiles into structured natural language descriptions,clear_include,"The abstract presents Cell2Text as a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA‑seq data into natural language summaries, showing a generative decoder mechanism, and it leverages gene‑level embeddings from single‑cell foundation models and pretrained LLMs, indicating foundation‑model characteristics.",PASS,PASS,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.csv:3184:1988,Sequence-to-Temperature: RAG-Enhanced and XAI-Supported OGT Estimation from Archaea tRNA Sequences Using Transformer-Based Embeddings,sequencetotemperature ragenhanced and xaisupported ogt estimation from archaea trna sequences using transformerbased embeddings,10.1109/BIBE66822.2025.00068,,,,"The growth conditions of an organism play a critical role in its biological characterization. However, information regarding these conditions is often unavailable. Predicting the optimal growth temperature based on the physical properties of the genome allows for estimation without the need to culture newly discovered species. In this context, developing methods that infer an organism's ideal growth temperature from genomic sequence data represents an important approach for improving efficiency in both time and resource management. In this study, we propose a novel methodology that leverages the structural parallels between tRNA sequences and natural language through transformerbased models. The approach comprises three main components: (i) generating embedding vectors from tRNA sequences using transformer-based encoders, (ii) storing and retrieving these embeddings within a vector-based database via a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) architecture, and (iii) performing a regression task using a large language model (LLM) based on the retrieved sequences, enhanced with explainable AI techniques to provide interpretable predictions. This framework introduces an alternative and interpretable solution for optimal growth temperature (OGT) estimation, highlighting the utility of RAG architectures in genomic applications.",Ahmet Telçeken; Eyup Cinar,2025.0,International Conferences on Biological Information and Biomedical Engineering,2025-11-06,,['semantic_scholar'],1,['10.1109/BIBE66822.2025.00068'],1,,,,,1856,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract proposes a novel transformer‑based embedding and RAG pipeline to predict optimal growth temperature from tRNA sequences, without describing natural‑language text inputs, prompts, or generation, thus lacking a substantive text component or text‑bio bridge.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,none,none,none,none,not_assessed,none,"The abstract proposes a novel transformer‑based embedding and RAG pipeline to predict optimal growth temperature from tRNA sequences, without describing natural‑language text inputs, prompts, or generation, thus lacking a substantive text component or text‑bio bridge.","{""paper_type"": ""application_wrapper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""application_wrapper"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""wrapper_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The study embeds tRNA sequences with transformer encoders and uses a pretrained LLM for regression via a RAG pipeline, without introducing a new generative architecture; it functions as a wrapper around existing models.""}",application_wrapper,no,yes,application_wrapper,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,wrapper_boundary,"The study embeds tRNA sequences with transformer encoders and uses a pretrained LLM for regression via a RAG pipeline, without introducing a new generative architecture; it functions as a wrapper around existing models.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,True,"{""paper_type"": ""application_wrapper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""application_wrapper"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""no mention of natural language text"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""no natural-language bridge described"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""LLM used for regression, not generation"", ""boundary_case"": ""wrapper_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract proposes a pipeline that uses existing transformer encoders and a large language model for regression on tRNA embeddings, without introducing a new multimodal generative model or natural-language text component, thus it is an application‑wrapper study lacking a text‑bio bridge.""}",application_wrapper,yes,no,no,no,yes,application_wrapper,none,no mention of natural language text,no natural-language bridge described,"LLM used for regression, not generation",wrapper_boundary,"The abstract proposes a pipeline that uses existing transformer encoders and a large language model for regression on tRNA embeddings, without introducing a new multimodal generative model or natural-language text component, thus it is an application‑wrapper study lacking a text‑bio bridge."
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.csv:3277:                RNAs play critical roles in gene expression regulation, catalysis, genetic information transmission, and immune response. However, current RNA engineering approaches face limitations in designing RNA for efficient in vivo folding and functional execution. Here, we present a novel, zero-shot strategy that integrates genomic large language models (LLMs) with inverse folding models (IFMs) to generate RNA sequences that mirror natural evolutionary patterns while preserving the structural integrity of critical functional regions. Benchmarking 9 state-of-the-art genomic foundation models, we show that their unsupervised log-likelihood scores correlate strongly with RNA fitness, enabling zero-shot sequence design. Applied to fluorescent RNA aptamers, our approach yielded 20 Broccoli variants, over half of which achieved up to 155% in fluorescence and a twofold increase in binding affinity, with robust performance both in vitro and in vivo. For Pepper, two rounds of optimization produced 40 variants, 16 of which demonstrated up to 2.6 fold in fluorescence and a threefold boost in binding affinity, including five with superior in vivo brightness.This approach offers a scalable, efficient, and resource-light method for RNA design, with significant implications for RNA-based therapeutics and diagnostics. By leveraging general foundation models to facilitate RNA evolution in a zero-shot fashion, we believe our work represents a major advancement in the field of RNA engineering.",Yanjie Huang; Bibi Zhang; Jack X. Chen; Runze Ma; Zhongyue Zhang; Xianjun Chen; Yi Yang; Shuangjia Zheng,2025.0,bioRxiv,2025-04-09,,['semantic_scholar'],1,['10.1101/2025.04.08.647793'],1,crossref,,,,1909,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The paper introduces a new zero‑shot RNA design method using genomic LLMs and inverse folding models (primary model). RNA sequences constitute a biological modality, but the abstract contains no mention of natural‑language text, prompts, or documents, so no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge is evident.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,none,none,none,none,not_assessed,none,"The paper introduces a new zero‑shot RNA design method using genomic LLMs and inverse folding models (primary model). RNA sequences constitute a biological modality, but the abstract contains no mention of natural‑language text, prompts, or documents, so no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge is evident.","{""paper_type"": ""application_wrapper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""yes"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""application_wrapper"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""integrates genomic large language models (LLMs) ... to generate RNA sequences"", ""boundary_case"": ""wrapper_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes a zero‑shot strategy that uses existing genomic LLMs to generate RNA sequences, without introducing a new primary generative model, thus it is an application wrapper.""}",application_wrapper,yes,yes,application_wrapper,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,integrates genomic large language models (LLMs) ... to generate RNA sequences,wrapper_boundary,"The abstract describes a zero‑shot strategy that uses existing genomic LLMs to generate RNA sequences, without introducing a new primary generative model, thus it is an application wrapper.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,True,"{""paper_type"": ""application_wrapper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""generative_model_present"": ""yes"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""application_wrapper"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""generate RNA sequences that mirror natural evolutionary patterns"", ""boundary_case"": ""wrapper_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents a zero‑shot strategy that integrates existing genomic LLMs with inverse‑folding models to generate RNA sequences, with no mention of natural‑language text or a multimodal text‑bio model, fitting the definition of a wrapper‑style use of foundation models.""}",application_wrapper,yes,no,no,yes,yes,application_wrapper,none,none,none,generate RNA sequences that mirror natural evolutionary patterns,wrapper_boundary,"The abstract presents a zero‑shot strategy that integrates existing genomic LLMs with inverse‑folding models to generate RNA sequences, with no mention of natural‑language text or a multimodal text‑bio model, fitting the definition of a wrapper‑style use of foundation models."
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.csv:3640:2246,CellTok: Early-Fusion Multimodal Large Language Model for Single-Cell Transcriptomics via Tokenization,celltok earlyfusion multimodal large language model for singlecell transcriptomics via tokenization,10.1101/2025.10.22.684047,,,,"Single-cell transcriptomic data provide a high-resolution view of cellular states and functions, offering critical insights into development, disease, and tissue heterogeneity. Existing foundation models for single-cell analysis typically embed cells as continuous vectors, limiting their generative flexibility and hindering integration with the knowledge accumulated in large language models (LLMs). In this work, we present CellTok, a multimodal LLM framework for unified analysis of scRNA-seq data and biological text. Each cell is tokenized into discrete codebook tokens via VQ-VAE and integrated into the LLM’s vocabulary using early fusion. This allows CellTok to process biological and textual inputs autoregressively, leveraging pretrained knowledge within LLMs to analyze cellular states and interactions. CellTok demonstrates strong performance in cell-level tasks such as annotation and generation, and enables previously unattainable large population-level tasks, including accurate prediction of intercellular communication networks, which is a key mechanism in tissue organization and disease. Furthermore, it retains interactive Q&A capabilities grounded in biomedical knowledge, offering a new paradigm for analyzing complex cellular systems. Our results suggest that the CellTok framework can extend the capabilities of LLMs to single-cell data, and suggests a direction for building more general mixed-modal models.",Chuxi Xiao; Haiyang Bian; Yixin Chen; Lei Wei; Xuegong Zhang,2025.0,bioRxiv,2025-10-24,,"['biorxiv_medrxiv', 'google_scholar', 'semantic_scholar']",3,['10.1101/2025.10.22.684047'],3,,,,,2101,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""yes"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""process biological and textual inputs autoregressively; interactive Q&A capabilities grounded in biomedical knowledge"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""Each cell is tokenized ... integrated into the LLM’s vocabulary using early fusion; process biological and textual inputs autoregressively"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract introduces CellTok as a multimodal LLM that tokenizes scRNA‑seq data and jointly processes it with natural‑language text, showing a primary model with both biological and text components and a substantive text‑bio bridge.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,none,none,process biological and textual inputs autoregressively; interactive Q&A capabilities grounded in biomedical knowledge,Each cell is tokenized ... integrated into the LLM’s vocabulary using early fusion; process biological and textual inputs autoregressively,not_assessed,none,"The abstract introduces CellTok as a multimodal LLM that tokenizes scRNA‑seq data and jointly processes it with natural‑language text, showing a primary model with both biological and text components and a substantive text‑bio bridge.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""yes"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""processes biological and textual inputs autoregressively"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_include"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract states that CellTok processes inputs autoregressively and enables generation, and it leverages pretrained LLM knowledge, indicating a primary generative foundation model.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,yes,none,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,processes biological and textual inputs autoregressively,clear_include,"The abstract states that CellTok processes inputs autoregressively and enables generation, and it leverages pretrained LLM knowledge, indicating a primary generative foundation model.",PASS,PASS,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.csv:3642:2248,Long-range gene expression prediction with token alignment of large language model,longrange gene expression prediction with token alignment of large language model,10.48550/arXiv.2410.01858,,,2410.01858,"Gene expression is a cellular process that plays a fundamental role in human phenotypical variations and diseases. Despite advances of deep learning models for gene expression prediction, recent benchmarks have revealed their inability to learn distal regulatory grammar. Here, we address this challenge by leveraging a pretrained large language model to enhance gene expression prediction. We introduce Genetic sequence Token Alignment (GTA), which aligns genetic sequence features with natural language tokens, allowing for symbolic reasoning of genomic sequence features via the frozen language model. This cross-modal adaptation learns the regulatory grammar and allows us to further incorporate gene-specific human annotations as prompts, enabling in-context learning that is not possible with existing models. Trained on lymphoblastoid cells, GTA was evaluated on cells from the Geuvadis consortium and outperforms state-of-the-art models such as Enformer, achieving a Spearman correlation of 0.65, a 10\% improvement. Additionally, GTA offers improved interpretation of long-range interactions through the identification of the most meaningful sections of the input genetic context. GTA represents a powerful and novel cross-modal approach to gene expression prediction by utilizing a pretrained language model, in a paradigm shift from conventional gene expression models trained only on sequence data.",Edouardo Honig; Huixin Zhan; Ying Nian Wu; Zijun Zhang,2024.0,arXiv.org,2024-10-02,,"['arxiv', 'semantic_scholar']",2,['10.48550/arXiv.2410.01858'],2,,,,,2103,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""yes"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""aligns genetic sequence features with natural language tokens"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""cross‑modal adaptation learns the regulatory grammar"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents GTA as a new model that aligns genetic sequences with natural‑language tokens and uses prompts, demonstrating a primary multimodal model with both biological and text components and a substantive text‑bio bridge.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,none,none,aligns genetic sequence features with natural language tokens,cross‑modal adaptation learns the regulatory grammar,not_assessed,none,"The abstract presents GTA as a new model that aligns genetic sequences with natural‑language tokens and uses prompts, demonstrating a primary multimodal model with both biological and text components and a substantive text‑bio bridge.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract introduces GTA, a model that predicts gene expression using a frozen large language model but does not describe any generative mechanism (e.g., decoder, autoregressive generation). It does leverage a pretrained LLM, providing foundation‑model evidence.""}",primary_model_paper,no,yes,none,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract introduces GTA, a model that predicts gene expression using a frozen large language model but does not describe any generative mechanism (e.g., decoder, autoregressive generation). It does leverage a pretrained LLM, providing foundation‑model evidence.",PASS,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.csv:3915:2381,Advances in Human Genome Resolution: The Role of Pan-Genomic Strategies and Fine-Tuning Pre-trained Genomic Models,advances in human genome resolution the role of pangenomic strategies and finetuning pretrained genomic models,10.18103/mra.v12i7.5571,,,,"The groundbreaking theory of DNA double helix structure has greatly promoted the development of molecular genetics, shaping and refining the genetic central dogma, thus enabling researchers to explore genotype-phenotype regulation at different levels. In particular, with the continued advancement of third-generation sequencing technology, an increasing number of highly accurate human genomes have been assembled, such as T2T-CHM13 and HG002. These high-quality genome sequences not only provide a more comprehensive human reference sequence, but also enable functional genomics studies within a unified coordinate system. To better explore and resolve the complex genetic information encompassed within human genome sequences, scientists have proposed novel research strategies, involving graphical pan-genome and pre-trained genomic models. The graphical pan-genomes provide population- level high-quality references, revealing the genomic diversity within populations and exploring the sequence complexity of specific regions, such as the KIR immune region. Concurrently, related studies of pre-trained models within the human genome offer new perspectives for interpreting sequence functions and delving into the hidden genetic codes, potentially leading to complete DNA decoding. Overall, graphical pan-genome and pre-trained genomic models represent two crucial strategies in genomics research, which will provide more new insights and make greater breakthroughs in the human genome. Together, these approaches have deepened our understanding of the human genome, fostered the development of bioinformatics ecosystems, and will contribute to the establishment and improvement of the entire field. Therefore, this review focuses on DNA sequencing, human genome assembly, high-quality pan-genome and pre-trained genomic large language models (LLMs), highlighting and summarizing the latest achievements and progress in human genome research, discussing existing challenges and providing future perspectives.",Lei Liu,2024.0,Medical Research Archives,,,['semantic_scholar'],1,['10.18103/mra.v12i7.5571'],1,,,,,2230,"{""paper_type"": ""review_editorial"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""review_editorial"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract explicitly states it is a review and summarizes advances in pan‑genome and pre‑trained genomic LLMs, with no mention of natural‑language text or a text‑bio mapping.""}",review_editorial,yes,no,no,review_editorial,none,none,none,not_assessed,clear_exclude,"The abstract explicitly states it is a review and summarizes advances in pan‑genome and pre‑trained genomic LLMs, with no mention of natural‑language text or a text‑bio mapping.","{""paper_type"": ""review_editorial"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""review_editorial"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract is a review summarizing pan‑genome and pre‑trained genomic LLMs, not presenting a new generative architecture.""}",review_editorial,no,yes,review_editorial,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract is a review summarizing pan‑genome and pre‑trained genomic LLMs, not presenting a new generative architecture.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.csv:4130:2500,GenoCGC: A Hybrid CNN and Graph-based Transformer Model for Genomic Sequence Classification,genocgc a hybrid cnn and graphbased transformer model for genomic sequence classification,10.1109/ICIT64950.2025.11049132,,,,"Advancements in sequencing technologies have resulted in rapid growth in genomic data. This revolutionary technology has enabled conducting large-scale studies in biology and medicine. In bioinformatics, accurately classifying genomic sequences is a necessary step for understanding genetic variations, identifying disease mutations, and advancing precision medicine. However, it remains one of the key challenges in the field. Traditional machine learning approaches often struggle with the complexity of genomic data. However, deep learning approaches, such as transformer-based models, improve analysis by capturing long-range dependencies. We introduce GenoCGC, a hybrid model combining Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) and a Graph-based Transformer (Graphormer) for genomic sequence classification. GenoCGC uses CNNs for capturing local features while utilizing Graphormer to model complex dependencies. We compared GenoCGC with traditional classifiers and genomic large language models across multiple genomic benchmark tasks. Experimental results show that GenoCGC achieves promising performance, providing an accurate and practical solution for genomic sequence analysis.",Anas Abu-Doleh,2025.0,2025 12th International Conference on Information Technology (ICIT),2025-05-27,,['semantic_scholar'],1,['10.1109/ICIT64950.2025.11049132'],1,,,,,2340,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The paper introduces a new hybrid CNN‑Graph Transformer model for genomic sequence classification, with no mention of natural‑language text, prompts, or textual descriptions; therefore it lacks a substantive text component and bridge.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,none,none,none,none,not_assessed,none,"The paper introduces a new hybrid CNN‑Graph Transformer model for genomic sequence classification, with no mention of natural‑language text, prompts, or textual descriptions; therefore it lacks a substantive text component and bridge.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC3_not_generative"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract introduces a hybrid CNN‑Graph Transformer for genomic sequence classification and reports performance comparisons, with no mention of generation or generative mechanisms.""}",primary_model_paper,no,no,EC3_not_generative,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract introduces a hybrid CNN‑Graph Transformer for genomic sequence classification and reports performance comparisons, with no mention of generation or generative mechanisms.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.csv:4706:2769,AI-discovered tuning laws explain neuronal population code geometry,aidiscovered tuning laws explain neuronal population code geometry,10.1101/2025.11.12.688086,,,,"The activity of visual cortical neurons forms a population code representing image stimuli. There is, however, a discrepancy between our understanding of this code at the single-cell and population levels: direct measurements indicate the population code is high-dimensional, but established models of single-cell tuning give rise to low-dimensional codes. We reconciled this discrepancy by developing an AI science system to find a new parsimonious, interpretable equation for visual cortical orientation tuning. Candidate equations were expressed as short computer programs and evolved by Large Language Models (LLMs) using graphical diagnostics. The resulting equation not only improved single-cell fits, but also accurately modelled the population code’s high-dimensional geometry. A novel parameter of the AI-discovered equation, which controls single-cell tuning smoothness, gives rise to high-dimensional population codes. The same parameter drives high-dimensional coding in head-direction cells, suggesting a common coding strategy across brain regions. We used this equation to hypothesize a circuit mechanism generating high-dimensional population codes, and to demonstrate the advantages of these codes in a simulated hyperacuity task. These results show that tuning smoothness has a key role in controlling population code geometry, and demonstrate how AI equation discovery can deliver interpretable models accelerating scientific understanding in neuroscience and beyond.","Tilbury R, Kwon D, Haydaroğlu A, Ratliff J, Schmutz V, Carandini M, Miller K, Stachenfeld K, Harris KD.",2025.0,,2025-11-13,,['biorxiv_medrxiv'],1,['10.1101/2025.11.12.688086'],1,,,,,2597,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC2_no_text_component"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""evolved by Large Language Models (LLMs) using graphical diagnostics"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The paper introduces a new AI system that uses LLMs to evolve equations for neuronal population coding, showing a clear biological modality, but the abstract describes only program generation, not natural‑language text or a text‑bio mapping.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,EC2_no_text_component,none,evolved by Large Language Models (LLMs) using graphical diagnostics,none,not_assessed,none,"The paper introduces a new AI system that uses LLMs to evolve equations for neuronal population coding, showing a clear biological modality, but the abstract describes only program generation, not natural‑language text or a text‑bio mapping.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""yes"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""evolved by Large Language Models (LLMs)"", ""boundary_case"": ""generative_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract states that candidate equations are evolved by LLMs, showing a generative mechanism, and that pretrained LLMs are used, indicating foundation‑model characteristics.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,yes,none,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,evolved by Large Language Models (LLMs),generative_boundary,"The abstract states that candidate equations are evolved by LLMs, showing a generative mechanism, and that pretrained LLMs are used, indicating foundation‑model characteristics.",EXCLUDE,PASS,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/nemotron3_super_120b_fp8_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11551/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.jsonl:55:{"cluster_id": 62, "title": "TissueNarrator: Generative Modeling of Spatial Transcriptomics with Large Language Models.", "title_normalized": "tissuenarrator generative modeling of spatial transcriptomics with large language models", "doi": "10.1101/2025.11.24.690325", "preprint_doi": NaN, "pmid": 41394628.0, "arxiv_id": NaN, "abstract": "The intricate spatial organization and molecular communication among cells are fundamental to multicellular systems. Spatial transcriptomics (ST) enables gene expression profiling while preserving spatial context, providing rich data for studying cellular interactions and tissue dynamics. However, most existing computational approaches focus on embedding-based tasks and provide limited generative capacity for simulating cell behavior  in situ . Moreover, accurately interpreting spatial interactions requires extensive biological knowledge, which current models do not incorporate. Here, we introduce T  issue N  arrator , a framework that reformulates spatial omics analysis as a language modeling problem. By representing tissue sections as spatial sentences – rank-based gene lists augmented with spatial coordinates and metadata – T  issue N  arrator leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs) to learn spatially conditioned gene expression patterns. The model generates realistic, context-aware cellular profiles, predicts intercellular interactions, and performs  in silico perturbation analyses. Across multiple ST technologies (MERFISH, Perturb-FISH, and CosMx SMI), T  issue N  arrator achieves superior quantitative performance and recovers biologically meaning-ful ligand–receptor and signaling pathways. Furthermore, a conversational inference mode enables natural-language querying of tissue organization. By integrating pretrained biological knowledge with spatial context, T  issue N  arrator establishes a new, scalable generative paradigm for modeling, simulating, and reasoning about tissue systems.", "authors": "Liu Sizhe; Tang Junjie; Ma Jian; Liang Shaoheng", "year": 2025.0, "venue": "bioRxiv : the preprint server for biology", "date": NaN, "url": NaN, "sources": "['biorxiv_medrxiv', 'google_scholar', 'pubmed', 'semantic_scholar']", "n_sources": 4, "all_dois": "['10.1101/2025.11.24.690325']", "duplicate_count": 4, "abstract_source": NaN, "abstract_enriched_from": NaN, "abstract_old_len": NaN, "update_batch": NaN, "_pilot_row_id": 62, "round-A_scope_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "yes", "text_bio_bridge_present": "yes", "primary_exclusion_code": "none", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "a conversational inference mode enables natural-language querying of tissue organization", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "a conversational inference mode enables natural-language querying of tissue organization", "evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "boundary_case": "clear_include", "decision_rationale": "The abstract presents TissueNarrator as a framework leveraging pretrained LLMs for spatial transcriptomics and includes a natural-language conversational mode, showing a primary model with substantive text and bio components."}, "round-A_scope_reviewer_paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "round-A_scope_reviewer_bio_modality_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_component_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_bio_bridge_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "a conversational inference mode enables natural-language querying of tissue organization", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "a conversational inference mode enables natural-language querying of tissue organization", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "round-A_scope_reviewer_boundary_case": "clear_include", "round-A_scope_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The abstract presents TissueNarrator as a framework leveraging pretrained LLMs for spatial transcriptomics and includes a natural-language conversational mode, showing a primary model with substantive text and bio components.", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "generative_model_present": "yes", "foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "primary_exclusion_code": "none", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_generative_model": "generates realistic, context-aware cellular profiles", "boundary_case": "clear_include", "decision_rationale": "The abstract describes TissueNarrator as generating realistic, context-aware cellular profiles via a language modeling approach using pretrained LLMs, indicating a generative architecture with foundation-model characteristics."}, "round-A_architecture_reviewer_paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_generative_model_present": "yes", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "generates realistic, context-aware cellular profiles", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_boundary_case": "clear_include", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The abstract describes TissueNarrator as generating realistic, context-aware cellular profiles via a language modeling approach using pretrained LLMs, indicating a generative architecture with foundation-model characteristics.", "pilot_roundA_scope_gate": "PASS", "pilot_roundA_architecture_gate": "PASS", "pilot_needs_adjudication": false}
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/nemotron3_super_120b_fp8_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11551/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.jsonl:728:{"cluster_id": 719, "title": "Improving polygenic prediction from whole-genome sequencing data by leveraging predicted epigenomic features", "title_normalized": "improving polygenic prediction from wholegenome sequencing data by leveraging predicted epigenomic features", "doi": "10.1073/pnas.2419202122", "preprint_doi": NaN, "pmid": NaN, "arxiv_id": NaN, "abstract": "Significance Epi-PRS improves polygenic risk scoring by integrating genomic large language models (LLMs) to impute epigenomic signals as intermediaries between genotype and phenotype. This approach enables a more comprehensive assessment of personal variant impacts by incorporating nonlinear models, rare variants, and regulatory mechanisms. By leveraging the power of genomic LLM trained on massive amount of reference epigenomics data, Epi-PRS has demonstrated superior performance over existing PRS methods in predicting genetic risk for breast cancer and diabetes in UK Biobank data. These results highlight the potential of Epi-PRS to improve disease risk modeling and advance the field of precision medicine.", "authors": "Zeng W.", "year": 2025.0, "venue": "Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America", "date": "2025-06-17", "url": NaN, "sources": "['scopus']", "n_sources": 1, "all_dois": "['10.1073/pnas.2419202122']", "duplicate_count": 1, "abstract_source": "s2_doi", "abstract_enriched_from": NaN, "abstract_old_len": NaN, "update_batch": NaN, "_pilot_row_id": 697, "round-A_scope_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "no", "text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "primary_exclusion_code": "none", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "none", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "boundary_case": "clear_exclude", "decision_rationale": "The abstract describes a genomic LLM trained on epigenomic data, which is a biological-token model without natural-language text. Hence there is no substantive text-bio bridge as required for a generative text-bio foundational model."}, "round-A_scope_reviewer_paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "round-A_scope_reviewer_bio_modality_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_component_present": "no", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "round-A_scope_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "round-A_scope_reviewer_boundary_case": "clear_exclude", "round-A_scope_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The abstract describes a genomic LLM trained on epigenomic data, which is a biological-token model without natural-language text. Hence there is no substantive text-bio bridge as required for a generative text-bio foundational model.", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "application_wrapper", "generative_model_present": "unclear", "foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "uncertainty_reason": "generative_status_unclear", "evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "boundary_case": "wrapper_boundary", "decision_rationale": "The abstract describes using a genomic LLM to impute epigenomic signals but does not specify a generative mechanism such as decoder or autoregressive generation, leaving generative status unclear. It does note the LLM is pretrained on massive epigenomics data, providing foundation‑model evidence."}, "round-A_architecture_reviewer_paper_type": "application_wrapper", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_generative_model_present": "unclear", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "generative_status_unclear", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_boundary_case": "wrapper_boundary", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The abstract describes using a genomic LLM to impute epigenomic signals but does not specify a generative mechanism such as decoder or autoregressive generation, leaving generative status unclear. It does note the LLM is pretrained on massive epigenomics data, providing foundation‑model evidence.", "pilot_roundA_scope_gate": "EXCLUDE", "pilot_roundA_architecture_gate": "EXCLUDE", "pilot_needs_adjudication": true, "round-B_adjudicator_output": {"paper_type": "application_wrapper", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "no", "text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "generative_model_present": "no", "foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "genomic large language models (LLMs) to impute epigenomic signals", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "genomic large language models (LLMs) to impute epigenomic signals", "evidence_for_generative_model": "genomic large language models (LLMs) to impute epigenomic signals", "boundary_case": "wrapper_boundary", "decision_rationale": "The abstract describes using a genomic LLM to impute epigenomic signals for polygenic risk scoring, with no explicit natural language text or generative mechanism, indicating a wrapper-style application of an existing LLM."}, "round-B_adjudicator_paper_type": "application_wrapper", "round-B_adjudicator_bio_modality_present": "yes", "round-B_adjudicator_text_component_present": "no", "round-B_adjudicator_text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "round-B_adjudicator_generative_model_present": "no", "round-B_adjudicator_foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "round-B_adjudicator_primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "round-B_adjudicator_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-B_adjudicator_evidence_for_text_component": "genomic large language models (LLMs) to impute epigenomic signals", "round-B_adjudicator_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "genomic large language models (LLMs) to impute epigenomic signals", "round-B_adjudicator_evidence_for_generative_model": "genomic large language models (LLMs) to impute epigenomic signals", "round-B_adjudicator_boundary_case": "wrapper_boundary", "round-B_adjudicator_decision_rationale": "The abstract describes using a genomic LLM to impute epigenomic signals for polygenic risk scoring, with no explicit natural language text or generative mechanism, indicating a wrapper-style application of an existing LLM."}
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/nemotron3_super_120b_fp8_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11551/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.jsonl:1082:{"cluster_id": 1207, "title": "GeneGPT: Augmenting Large Language Models with Domain Tools for Improved Access to Biomedical Information", "title_normalized": "genegpt augmenting large language models with domain tools for improved access to biomedical information", "doi": "10.1093/bioinformatics/btae075", "preprint_doi": NaN, "pmid": 38341654.0, "arxiv_id": "2304.09667v3", "abstract": "While large language models (LLMs) have been successfully applied to various tasks, they still face challenges with hallucinations. Augmenting LLMs with domain-specific tools such as database utilities can facilitate easier and more precise access to specialized knowledge. In this paper, we present GeneGPT, a novel method for teaching LLMs to use the Web APIs of the National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI) for answering genomics questions. Specifically, we prompt Codex to solve the GeneTuring tests with NCBI Web APIs by in-context learning and an augmented decoding algorithm that can detect and execute API calls. Experimental results show that GeneGPT achieves state-of-the-art performance on eight tasks in the GeneTuring benchmark with an average score of 0.83, largely surpassing retrieval-augmented LLMs such as the new Bing (0.44), biomedical LLMs such as BioMedLM (0.08) and BioGPT (0.04), as well as GPT-3 (0.16) and ChatGPT (0.12). Our further analyses suggest that: (1) API demonstrations have good cross-task generalizability and are more useful than documentations for in-context learning; (2) GeneGPT can generalize to longer chains of API calls and answer multi-hop questions in GeneHop, a novel dataset introduced in this work; (3) Different types of errors are enriched in different tasks, providing valuable insights for future improvements.", "authors": "Qiao Jin; Yifan Yang; Qingyu Chen; Zhiyong Lu", "year": 2023.0, "venue": "Bioinform.", "date": "2023-04-19", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/bioinformatics/advance-article-pdf/doi/10.1093/bioinformatics/btae075/56659521/btae075.pdf", "sources": "['arxiv', 'google_scholar', 'semantic_scholar']", "n_sources": 3, "all_dois": "['10.1093/bioinformatics/btae075', 'https://doi.org/10.1093/bioinformatics/btae075']", "duplicate_count": 3, "abstract_source": NaN, "abstract_enriched_from": NaN, "abstract_old_len": NaN, "update_batch": NaN, "_pilot_row_id": 1096, "round-A_scope_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "application_wrapper", "bio_modality_present": "unclear", "text_component_present": "yes", "text_bio_bridge_present": "yes", "primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "uncertainty_reason": "bio_modality_unclear", "evidence_for_text_component": "large language models (LLMs)", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "answering genomics questions", "evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "boundary_case": "wrapper_boundary", "decision_rationale": "GeneGPT augments an existing LLM (Codex) with NCBI API tool use to answer genomics questions, making it an application wrapper around a pretrained LLM rather than a new multimodal text‑bio model."}, "round-A_scope_reviewer_paper_type": "application_wrapper", "round-A_scope_reviewer_bio_modality_present": "unclear", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_component_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_bio_bridge_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "round-A_scope_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "bio_modality_unclear", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "large language models (LLMs)", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "answering genomics questions", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "round-A_scope_reviewer_boundary_case": "wrapper_boundary", "round-A_scope_reviewer_decision_rationale": "GeneGPT augments an existing LLM (Codex) with NCBI API tool use to answer genomics questions, making it an application wrapper around a pretrained LLM rather than a new multimodal text‑bio model.", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "application_wrapper", "generative_model_present": "unclear", "foundation_model_evidence": "unclear", "primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "uncertainty_reason": "mixed_signals", "evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "boundary_case": "wrapper_boundary", "decision_rationale": "The paper augments an existing LLM (Codex) with a decoding algorithm to use NCBI APIs, but does not introduce a new generative architecture or provide foundation-model evidence for the candidate model."}, "round-A_architecture_reviewer_paper_type": "application_wrapper", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_generative_model_present": "unclear", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_foundation_model_evidence": "unclear", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "mixed_signals", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "not_assessed", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_boundary_case": "wrapper_boundary", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The paper augments an existing LLM (Codex) with a decoding algorithm to use NCBI APIs, but does not introduce a new generative architecture or provide foundation-model evidence for the candidate model.", "pilot_roundA_scope_gate": "EXCLUDE", "pilot_roundA_architecture_gate": "EXCLUDE", "pilot_needs_adjudication": false}
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/nemotron3_super_120b_fp8_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11551/guideline_pilot_raw_completed.jsonl:1144:{"cluster_id": 1236, "title": "Language may be all omics needs: Harmonizing multimodal data for omics understanding with CellHermes", "title_normalized": "language may be all omics needs harmonizing multimodal data for omics understanding with cellhermes", "doi": "10.1101/2025.11.07.687322", "preprint_doi": NaN, "pmid": NaN, "arxiv_id": NaN, "abstract": "Decoding cellular systems requires integrating diverse omics data, yet most models are trained from scratch on a single modality, restricting generalization. Here we present CellHermes, a biological language model that leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs) to integrate multimodal forms of omics data, such as transcriptomic profile and PPI network, through natural language for better omics understanding. By reformulating these datasets into question-answer pairs, CellHermes emulates multiple self-supervised learning paradigms within a single, universal, language-based framework with LORA fine-tuning on existing natural language-LLM, achieving comparable or even better performance compared to current single-cell foundation models. Within this framework, CellHermes functions as an encoder, a predictor and an explainer for supporting a range of downstream tasks. For encoder, CellHermes encodes biological entities with embeddings that enable accurate network discovery and cross-dataset generalization; For predictor, CellHermes unifies heterogeneous downstream tasks by translating them into natural language question-answers pairs, allowing a single model to perform multi-task prediction by instruction fine-tuning; For explainer, CellHermes explains molecular mechanisms by combining attention analysis with text-based reasoning, leveraging the interpretability and reasoning capabilities of LLMs. We evaluated the ability of CellHermes as an  encoder on representing genes and cells using 5 gene-level downstream tasks and 5 diverse single-cell datasets across different tissues compared with other single cell foundation models. We also evaluated CellHermes as a  predictor on BioUniBench, a new benchmark of 10 tasks across 7 databases for LLMs, including perturbation response, cell fitness estimation, gene-disease association, and cell type identification. All benchmarks suggest CellHermes can unify multiple biological tasks within one model without compromising performance. By applying CellHermes as an  explainer for a melanoma patients dataset, it can uncover potential key genes on tumor-reactive T cells. Together, CellHermes establishes natural language as a unifying medium for omics, offering a foundation that, in the future, potentially enables a more integrated and interpretable research loop of biological representation, prediction, and interpretability.", "authors": "Yicheng Gao; Weixu Wang; Yuheng Zhao; Kejing Dong; Caihua Shan; Weizhong Zheng; Till Richter; Zekai Li; Siming Chen; Fabian J. Theis; Qi Liu", "year": 2025.0, "venue": "bioRxiv", "date": "2025-11-28", "url": NaN, "sources": "['biorxiv_medrxiv', 'google_scholar', 'semantic_scholar']", "n_sources": 3, "all_dois": "['10.1101/2025.11.07.687322']", "duplicate_count": 3, "abstract_source": NaN, "abstract_enriched_from": NaN, "abstract_old_len": NaN, "update_batch": NaN, "_pilot_row_id": 1125, "round-A_scope_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "yes", "text_bio_bridge_present": "yes", "primary_exclusion_code": "none", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "evidence_for_text_component": "biological language model that leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs)", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "integrate multimodal forms of omics data, such as transcriptomic profile and PPI network, through natural language", "evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "boundary_case": "clear_include", "decision_rationale": "Abstract describes CellHermes as a biological language model that uses pretrained LLMs and reformulates omics data into natural language question-answer pairs, showing a substantive text component and text-bio bridge."}, "round-A_scope_reviewer_paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "round-A_scope_reviewer_bio_modality_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_component_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_text_bio_bridge_present": "yes", "round-A_scope_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "none", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "biological language model that leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs)", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "integrate multimodal forms of omics data, such as transcriptomic profile and PPI network, through natural language", "round-A_scope_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "not_assessed", "round-A_scope_reviewer_boundary_case": "clear_include", "round-A_scope_reviewer_decision_rationale": "Abstract describes CellHermes as a biological language model that uses pretrained LLMs and reformulates omics data into natural language question-answer pairs, showing a substantive text component and text-bio bridge.", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_output": {"paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "generative_model_present": "unclear", "foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "primary_exclusion_code": "none", "uncertainty_reason": "generative_status_unclear", "evidence_for_text_component": "leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs)", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "integrating diverse omics data through natural language", "evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "boundary_case": "none", "decision_rationale": "The abstract describes CellHermes as a biological language model that leverages pretrained LLMs and LoRA fine‑tuning for encoding, predicting and explaining omics data, but does not specify a decoder, autoregressive or other explicit generative mechanism, leaving its generative nature uncertain. It does provide foundation‑model evidence via broad pretraining and multi‑task applicability."}, "round-A_architecture_reviewer_paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_generative_model_present": "unclear", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_primary_exclusion_code": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_uncertainty_reason": "generative_status_unclear", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_component": "leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs)", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "integrating diverse omics data through natural language", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_boundary_case": "none", "round-A_architecture_reviewer_decision_rationale": "The abstract describes CellHermes as a biological language model that leverages pretrained LLMs and LoRA fine‑tuning for encoding, predicting and explaining omics data, but does not specify a decoder, autoregressive or other explicit generative mechanism, leaving its generative nature uncertain. It does provide foundation‑model evidence via broad pretraining and multi‑task applicability.", "pilot_roundA_scope_gate": "PASS", "pilot_roundA_architecture_gate": "UNCERTAIN", "pilot_needs_adjudication": true, "round-B_adjudicator_output": {"paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "yes", "text_bio_bridge_present": "yes", "generative_model_present": "unclear", "foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "primary_exclusion_code": "none", "uncertainty_reason": "generative_status_unclear", "evidence_for_text_component": "leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs) to integrate multimodal forms of omics data through natural language", "evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "translating them into natural language question-answers pairs", "evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "boundary_case": "thin_abstract_boundary", "decision_rationale": "Abstract describes CellHermes as a biological language model using natural language Q&A pairs to integrate omics data, establishing text component and bio bridge; however, it does not explicitly state a generative mechanism (e.g., decoder, autoregressive), leaving generative status unclear."}, "round-B_adjudicator_paper_type": "primary_model_paper", "round-B_adjudicator_bio_modality_present": "yes", "round-B_adjudicator_text_component_present": "yes", "round-B_adjudicator_text_bio_bridge_present": "yes", "round-B_adjudicator_generative_model_present": "unclear", "round-B_adjudicator_foundation_model_evidence": "yes", "round-B_adjudicator_primary_exclusion_code": "none", "round-B_adjudicator_uncertainty_reason": "generative_status_unclear", "round-B_adjudicator_evidence_for_text_component": "leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs) to integrate multimodal forms of omics data through natural language", "round-B_adjudicator_evidence_for_text_bio_bridge": "translating them into natural language question-answers pairs", "round-B_adjudicator_evidence_for_generative_model": "none", "round-B_adjudicator_boundary_case": "thin_abstract_boundary", "round-B_adjudicator_decision_rationale": "Abstract describes CellHermes as a biological language model using natural language Q&A pairs to integrate omics data, establishing text component and bio bridge; however, it does not explicitly state a generative mechanism (e.g., decoder, autoregressive), leaving generative status unclear."}
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:64:62,TissueNarrator: Generative Modeling of Spatial Transcriptomics with Large Language Models.,tissuenarrator generative modeling of spatial transcriptomics with large language models,10.1101/2025.11.24.690325,,41394628.0,,"The intricate spatial organization and molecular communication among cells are fundamental to multicellular systems. Spatial transcriptomics (ST) enables gene expression profiling while preserving spatial context, providing rich data for studying cellular interactions and tissue dynamics. However, most existing computational approaches focus on embedding-based tasks and provide limited generative capacity for simulating cell behavior  in situ . Moreover, accurately interpreting spatial interactions requires extensive biological knowledge, which current models do not incorporate. Here, we introduce T  issue N  arrator , a framework that reformulates spatial omics analysis as a language modeling problem. By representing tissue sections as spatial sentences – rank-based gene lists augmented with spatial coordinates and metadata – T  issue N  arrator leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs) to learn spatially conditioned gene expression patterns. The model generates realistic, context-aware cellular profiles, predicts intercellular interactions, and performs  in silico perturbation analyses. Across multiple ST technologies (MERFISH, Perturb-FISH, and CosMx SMI), T  issue N  arrator achieves superior quantitative performance and recovers biologically meaning-ful ligand–receptor and signaling pathways. Furthermore, a conversational inference mode enables natural-language querying of tissue organization. By integrating pretrained biological knowledge with spatial context, T  issue N  arrator establishes a new, scalable generative paradigm for modeling, simulating, and reasoning about tissue systems.",Liu Sizhe; Tang Junjie; Ma Jian; Liang Shaoheng,2025.0,bioRxiv : the preprint server for biology,,,"['biorxiv_medrxiv', 'google_scholar', 'pubmed', 'semantic_scholar']",4,['10.1101/2025.11.24.690325'],4,,,,,62,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""yes"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""conversational inference mode enables natural-language querying of tissue organization"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""reformulates spatial omics analysis as a language modeling problem"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents TissueNarrator as a new framework that treats spatial transcriptomics as language, uses pretrained LLMs, and provides natural-language querying, satisfying primary model, biological modality, text component, and text‑bio bridge criteria.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,none,none,conversational inference mode enables natural-language querying of tissue organization,reformulates spatial omics analysis as a language modeling problem,not_assessed,none,"The abstract presents TissueNarrator as a new framework that treats spatial transcriptomics as language, uses pretrained LLMs, and provides natural-language querying, satisfying primary model, biological modality, text component, and text‑bio bridge criteria.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""yes"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""model generates realistic, context-aware cellular profiles"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_include"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents TissueNarrator, a new framework that leverages pretrained LLMs to generate realistic cellular profiles and simulate tissue dynamics, indicating a generative architecture with foundation-model pretraining.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,yes,none,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,"model generates realistic, context-aware cellular profiles",clear_include,"The abstract presents TissueNarrator, a new framework that leverages pretrained LLMs to generate realistic cellular profiles and simulate tissue dynamics, indicating a generative architecture with foundation-model pretraining.",PASS,PASS,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,PASS|PASS,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,,,"The abstract presents TissueNarrator as a new framework that treats spatial transcriptomics as language, uses pretrained LLMs, and provides natural-language querying, satisfying primary model, biological modality, text component, and text‑bio bridge criteria. | The abstract presents TissueNarrator, a new framework that leverages pretrained LLMs to generate realistic cellular profiles and simulate tissue dynamics, indicating a generative architecture with foundation-model pretraining.",INCLUDE,,,INCLUDE,,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:145:143,Interpretable Transfer Learning for Cancer Drug Resistance: Candidate Target Identification.,interpretable transfer learning for cancer drug resistance candidate target identification,10.3390/cimb47090753,,41020875.0,,"Tumor drug resistance exhibits substantial heterogeneity across cancer types, reflecting distinct molecular mechanisms in each malignancy. To characterize this complexity, we developed a pan-cancer transfer learning framework that integrates bulk RNA-seq data with a residual variational autoencoder (Res VAE) backbone. Five models were trained on the Genomics of Drug Sensitivity in Cancer (GDSC) dataset, which includes drug response profiles for 72 chemotherapeutic agents. Among them, three models are specially designed by incorporating variational autoencoders and large pretrained models (LLMs): the LLM large VAE (VAE_LL), the LLM small VAE (VAE_LS), and the LLM distillation VAE (VAE_LD). Random Forest (RF) and eXtreme Gradient Boosting (XGB) were included as ensemble learning baselines. After internal cross-validation, the top four models (VAE_LL, VAE_LD, XGB, and RF) were applied to five representative TCGA cohorts comprising 1,836 patients. For each cancer type, resistance to nine clinically relevant first-line drugs was modeled, resulting in 180 drug–cancer prediction tasks. Among all models, VAE_LD achieved the best overall performance, with a mean AUC of 0.81 and an F1 score of 0.92 on the GDSC benchmark, and maintained strong predictive power in the clinical validation phase. Interpretation analyses identified tumor-specific resistance biomarkers with clinical significance. In lung adenocarcinoma, elevated expression of TFF1 was repeatedly associated with resistance to Gefitinib and correlated with poor patient prognosis, indicating its potential as a therapeutic target. In glioblastoma, OPALIN, LTF, IL2RA, and SLC17A7 were implicated in Temozolomide resistance through pathways related to epithelial differentiation and angiogenesis. In conclusion, the VAE_LD model offers a high-performing and interpretable approach for predicting drug resistance across multiple tumor types. It supports the identification of clinically actionable biomarkers and provides a robust framework for precision oncology applications.",Zhang Wenjie; Wu Xisong; Chen Liang; Wan Xinyue,2025.0,Current issues in molecular biology,,,"['pubmed', 'scopus', 'semantic_scholar']",3,['10.3390/cimb47090753'],3,,,,,143,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""unclear"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""unclear"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""text_component_unclear"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""large pretrained models (LLMs)"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The work presents new VAE models (primary model) using bulk RNA‑seq data (bio modality), but the abstract only mentions incorporating LLMs without specifying natural‑language text input or output, leaving the text component and text‑bio bridge unclear.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,unclear,unclear,none,text_component_unclear,large pretrained models (LLMs),none,not_assessed,none,"The work presents new VAE models (primary model) using bulk RNA‑seq data (bio modality), but the abstract only mentions incorporating LLMs without specifying natural‑language text input or output, leaving the text component and text‑bio bridge unclear.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""yes"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""variational autoencoder (Res VAE) backbone"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract introduces VAE-based models (a generative architecture) and explicitly incorporates large pretrained LLMs, fitting the criteria for a primary generative text‑bio foundational model.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,yes,none,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,variational autoencoder (Res VAE) backbone,none,"The abstract introduces VAE-based models (a generative architecture) and explicitly incorporates large pretrained LLMs, fitting the criteria for a primary generative text‑bio foundational model.",UNCERTAIN,PASS,True,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""generative_model_present"": ""yes"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC2_no_text_component"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""variational autoencoder (Res VAE) backbone"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents a VAE‑based model for drug‑resistance prediction using RNA‑seq data and mentions large pretrained LLMs, but it contains no natural‑language text input, output, or alignment, so there is no text‑bio bridge; therefore the paper is excluded under EC2_no_text_component.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,yes,yes,EC2_no_text_component,none,none,none,variational autoencoder (Res VAE) backbone,clear_exclude,"The abstract presents a VAE‑based model for drug‑resistance prediction using RNA‑seq data and mentions large pretrained LLMs, but it contains no natural‑language text input, output, or alignment, so there is no text‑bio bridge; therefore the paper is excluded under EC2_no_text_component.",UNCERTAIN|PASS,primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,yes,yes,EC2_no_text_component,,none,none,variational autoencoder (Res VAE) backbone,clear_exclude,"The abstract presents a VAE‑based model for drug‑resistance prediction using RNA‑seq data and mentions large pretrained LLMs, but it contains no natural‑language text input, output, or alignment, so there is no text‑bio bridge; therefore the paper is excluded under EC2_no_text_component.",EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:737:719,Improving polygenic prediction from whole-genome sequencing data by leveraging predicted epigenomic features,improving polygenic prediction from wholegenome sequencing data by leveraging predicted epigenomic features,10.1073/pnas.2419202122,,,,"Significance Epi-PRS improves polygenic risk scoring by integrating genomic large language models (LLMs) to impute epigenomic signals as intermediaries between genotype and phenotype. This approach enables a more comprehensive assessment of personal variant impacts by incorporating nonlinear models, rare variants, and regulatory mechanisms. By leveraging the power of genomic LLM trained on massive amount of reference epigenomics data, Epi-PRS has demonstrated superior performance over existing PRS methods in predicting genetic risk for breast cancer and diabetes in UK Biobank data. These results highlight the potential of Epi-PRS to improve disease risk modeling and advance the field of precision medicine.",Zeng W.,2025.0,Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America,2025-06-17,,['scopus'],1,['10.1073/pnas.2419202122'],1,s2_doi,,,,697,"{""paper_type"": ""application_wrapper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""application_wrapper"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents a polygenic risk scoring method that leverages a genomic LLM to impute epigenomic signals, with no mention of natural‑language text or a text‑bio mapping; thus it functions as an application wrapper rather than a primary text‑bio generative model.""}",application_wrapper,yes,no,no,application_wrapper,none,none,none,not_assessed,none,"The abstract presents a polygenic risk scoring method that leverages a genomic LLM to impute epigenomic signals, with no mention of natural‑language text or a text‑bio mapping; thus it functions as an application wrapper rather than a primary text‑bio generative model.","{""paper_type"": ""application_wrapper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""application_wrapper"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""wrapper_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes Epi-PRS that uses a pretrained genomic LLM to impute epigenomic signals for polygenic risk scoring, without presenting a new generative architecture; thus it is an application wrapper rather than a primary generative model.""}",application_wrapper,no,yes,application_wrapper,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,wrapper_boundary,"The abstract describes Epi-PRS that uses a pretrained genomic LLM to impute epigenomic signals for polygenic risk scoring, without presenting a new generative architecture; thus it is an application wrapper rather than a primary generative model.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,EXCLUDE|EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,yes,no,no,no,yes,application_wrapper,,,,,,"The abstract presents a polygenic risk scoring method that leverages a genomic LLM to impute epigenomic signals, with no mention of natural‑language text or a text‑bio mapping; thus it functions as an application wrapper rather than a primary text‑bio generative model. | The abstract describes Epi-PRS that uses a pretrained genomic LLM to impute epigenomic signals for polygenic risk scoring, without presenting a new generative architecture; thus it is an application wrapper rather than a primary generative model.",EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:998:952,A BERT-GNN Approach for Metastatic Breast Cancer Prediction Using Histopathology Reports,a bertgnn approach for metastatic breast cancer prediction using histopathology reports,10.3390/diagnostics14131365,,,,"Metastatic breast cancer (MBC) continues to be a leading cause of cancer-related deaths among women. This work introduces an innovative non-invasive breast cancer classification model designed to improve the identification of cancer metastases. While this study marks the initial exploration into predicting MBC, additional investigations are essential to validate the occurrence of MBC. Our approach combines the strengths of large language models (LLMs), specifically the bidirectional encoder representations from transformers (BERT) model, with the powerful capabilities of graph neural networks (GNNs) to predict MBC patients based on their histopathology reports. This paper introduces a BERT-GNN approach for metastatic breast cancer prediction (BG-MBC) that integrates graph information derived from the BERT model. In this model, nodes are constructed from patient medical records, while BERT embeddings are employed to vectorise representations of the words in histopathology reports, thereby capturing semantic information crucial for classification by employing three distinct approaches (namely univariate selection, extra trees classifier for feature importance, and Shapley values to identify the features that have the most significant impact). Identifying the most crucial 30 features out of 676 generated as embeddings during model training, our model further enhances its predictive capabilities. The BG-MBC model achieves outstanding accuracy, with a detection rate of 0.98 and an area under curve (AUC) of 0.98, in identifying MBC patients. This remarkable performance is credited to the model’s utilisation of attention scores generated by the LLM from histopathology reports, effectively capturing pertinent features for classification.",Basaad  A.,2024.0,Diagnostics,2024-07-01,,['scopus'],1,['10.3390/diagnostics14131365'],1,s2_doi,,,,880,"{""paper_type"": ""application_wrapper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""no"", ""text_component_present"": ""yes"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""application_wrapper"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""BERT embeddings are employed to vectorise representations of the words in histopathology reports"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""predict MBC patients based on their histopathology reports"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The work introduces a BERT‑GNN classifier that uses BERT embeddings of histopathology report text as features for a downstream graph neural network, i.e., it wraps an existing LLM rather than presenting a novel generative text‑bio foundational model.""}",application_wrapper,no,yes,yes,application_wrapper,none,BERT embeddings are employed to vectorise representations of the words in histopathology reports,predict MBC patients based on their histopathology reports,not_assessed,none,"The work introduces a BERT‑GNN classifier that uses BERT embeddings of histopathology report text as features for a downstream graph neural network, i.e., it wraps an existing LLM rather than presenting a novel generative text‑bio foundational model.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC3_not_generative"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract proposes a BERT‑GNN classification model for metastatic breast cancer prediction and does not mention any generative mechanism (e.g., decoder, autoregressive generation). It uses BERT embeddings, indicating use of a pretrained LLM, but the work is purely predictive.""}",primary_model_paper,no,yes,EC3_not_generative,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract proposes a BERT‑GNN classification model for metastatic breast cancer prediction and does not mention any generative mechanism (e.g., decoder, autoregressive generation). It uses BERT embeddings, indicating use of a pretrained LLM, but the work is purely predictive.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,True,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""yes"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""yes"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC3_not_generative"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""BERT embeddings are employed to vectorise representations of the words in histopathology reports"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""nodes are constructed from patient medical records, while BERT embeddings ... capture semantic information ... for classification"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes a BERT‑GNN classification model using text embeddings and patient record graphs, with no indication of generative capabilities; therefore it is excluded under EC3_not_generative.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,no,yes,EC3_not_generative,none,BERT embeddings are employed to vectorise representations of the words in histopathology reports,"nodes are constructed from patient medical records, while BERT embeddings ... capture semantic information ... for classification",none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a BERT‑GNN classification model using text embeddings and patient record graphs, with no indication of generative capabilities; therefore it is excluded under EC3_not_generative.",EXCLUDE|EXCLUDE,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,no,yes,EC3_not_generative,,BERT embeddings are employed to vectorise representations of the words in histopathology reports,"nodes are constructed from patient medical records, while BERT embeddings ... capture semantic information ... for classification",none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a BERT‑GNN classification model using text embeddings and patient record graphs, with no indication of generative capabilities; therefore it is excluded under EC3_not_generative.",EXCLUDE,EC3_not_generative,,EXCLUDE,EC3_not_generative,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:1402:1236,Language may be all omics needs: Harmonizing multimodal data for omics understanding with CellHermes,language may be all omics needs harmonizing multimodal data for omics understanding with cellhermes,10.1101/2025.11.07.687322,,,,"Decoding cellular systems requires integrating diverse omics data, yet most models are trained from scratch on a single modality, restricting generalization. Here we present CellHermes, a biological language model that leverages pretrained large language models (LLMs) to integrate multimodal forms of omics data, such as transcriptomic profile and PPI network, through natural language for better omics understanding. By reformulating these datasets into question-answer pairs, CellHermes emulates multiple self-supervised learning paradigms within a single, universal, language-based framework with LORA fine-tuning on existing natural language-LLM, achieving comparable or even better performance compared to current single-cell foundation models. Within this framework, CellHermes functions as an encoder, a predictor and an explainer for supporting a range of downstream tasks. For encoder, CellHermes encodes biological entities with embeddings that enable accurate network discovery and cross-dataset generalization; For predictor, CellHermes unifies heterogeneous downstream tasks by translating them into natural language question-answers pairs, allowing a single model to perform multi-task prediction by instruction fine-tuning; For explainer, CellHermes explains molecular mechanisms by combining attention analysis with text-based reasoning, leveraging the interpretability and reasoning capabilities of LLMs. We evaluated the ability of CellHermes as an  encoder on representing genes and cells using 5 gene-level downstream tasks and 5 diverse single-cell datasets across different tissues compared with other single cell foundation models. We also evaluated CellHermes as a  predictor on BioUniBench, a new benchmark of 10 tasks across 7 databases for LLMs, including perturbation response, cell fitness estimation, gene-disease association, and cell type identification. All benchmarks suggest CellHermes can unify multiple biological tasks within one model without compromising performance. By applying CellHermes as an  explainer for a melanoma patients dataset, it can uncover potential key genes on tumor-reactive T cells. Together, CellHermes establishes natural language as a unifying medium for omics, offering a foundation that, in the future, potentially enables a more integrated and interpretable research loop of biological representation, prediction, and interpretability.",Yicheng Gao; Weixu Wang; Yuheng Zhao; Kejing Dong; Caihua Shan; Weizhong Zheng; Till Richter; Zekai Li; Siming Chen; Fabian J. Theis; Qi Liu,2025.0,bioRxiv,2025-11-28,,"['biorxiv_medrxiv', 'google_scholar', 'semantic_scholar']",3,['10.1101/2025.11.07.687322'],3,,,,,1125,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""yes"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""reformulating these datasets into question‑answer pairs"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""translating them into natural language question‑answers pairs"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents CellHermes as a new model that uses pretrained LLMs and natural‑language QA reformulation to integrate omics data, indicating a substantive text component and a clear text‑bio bridge.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,none,none,reformulating these datasets into question‑answer pairs,translating them into natural language question‑answers pairs,not_assessed,none,"The abstract presents CellHermes as a new model that uses pretrained LLMs and natural‑language QA reformulation to integrate omics data, indicating a substantive text component and a clear text‑bio bridge.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents CellHermes as a new model built on pretrained LLMs for encoding, prediction, and explanation, without mentioning any decoder or generative mechanism; it clearly leverages a pretrained LLM, providing foundation‑model evidence.""}",primary_model_paper,no,yes,none,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,none,"The abstract presents CellHermes as a new model built on pretrained LLMs for encoding, prediction, and explanation, without mentioning any decoder or generative mechanism; it clearly leverages a pretrained LLM, providing foundation‑model evidence.",PASS,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,PASS|EXCLUDE,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,no,yes,,,,,,,"The abstract presents CellHermes as a new model that uses pretrained LLMs and natural‑language QA reformulation to integrate omics data, indicating a substantive text component and a clear text‑bio bridge. | The abstract presents CellHermes as a new model built on pretrained LLMs for encoding, prediction, and explanation, without mentioning any decoder or generative mechanism; it clearly leverages a pretrained LLM, providing foundation‑model evidence.",EXCLUDE,EC3_not_generative,,EXCLUDE,EC3_not_generative,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:1845:1403,Leveraging genomic large language models to enhance causal genotype-brain-clinical pathways in Alzheimer's disease,leveraging genomic large language models to enhance causal genotypebrainclinical pathways in alzheimers disease,10.1101/2024.10.03.24314824,,,,"Abstract
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:1846:                Genome-wide association studies (GWAS) have identified numerous Alzheimer’s disease (AD)- associated variants. However, how these variants contribute to the etiology of AD remains largely elusive. Recent advances in genomic large language models (LLMs) offer new opportunities to interpret the genetic variation observed in personal genome. In this study, we propose epiBrainLLM, a novel computational framework that leverages genomic LLM to enhance our understanding of the causal pathways from genotypes to brain measures to AD-related clinical phenotypes. epiBrainLLM will first convert the personal DNA sequence into a diverse set of genomic and epigenomic features using a pretrained genomic LLM and then use these features to further predict phenotypes. Across various experimental settings, epiBrainLLM significantly improves causal analysis compared to traditional genotype association approach. We conclude that epiBrainLLM provides a novel perspective for understanding the regulatory mechanisms underlying the AD disease etiology, potentially offering insights into complex disease mechanisms beyond AD.",Q. Liu; W. Zeng; H. Zhu; L. Li; W. Wong,2024.0,medRxiv,2024-10-04,,['semantic_scholar'],1,['10.1101/2024.10.03.24314824'],1,crossref,,,,1284,"{""paper_type"": ""application_wrapper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""application_wrapper"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents epiBrainLLM, which uses a pretrained genomic LLM to extract features from DNA and predict phenotypes, without describing a new model or natural-language text component, thus it is an application wrapper lacking a substantive text-bio bridge.""}",application_wrapper,yes,no,no,application_wrapper,none,none,none,not_assessed,none,"The abstract presents epiBrainLLM, which uses a pretrained genomic LLM to extract features from DNA and predict phenotypes, without describing a new model or natural-language text component, thus it is an application wrapper lacking a substantive text-bio bridge.","{""paper_type"": ""application_wrapper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""application_wrapper"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""wrapper_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents epiBrainLLM as a framework that leverages a pretrained genomic LLM to generate features and predict phenotypes, without describing its own generative mechanism; therefore it is an application wrapper rather than a primary generative model.""}",application_wrapper,no,yes,application_wrapper,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,wrapper_boundary,"The abstract presents epiBrainLLM as a framework that leverages a pretrained genomic LLM to generate features and predict phenotypes, without describing its own generative mechanism; therefore it is an application wrapper rather than a primary generative model.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,EXCLUDE|EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,yes,no,no,no,yes,application_wrapper,,,,,,"The abstract presents epiBrainLLM, which uses a pretrained genomic LLM to extract features from DNA and predict phenotypes, without describing a new model or natural-language text component, thus it is an application wrapper lacking a substantive text-bio bridge. | The abstract presents epiBrainLLM as a framework that leverages a pretrained genomic LLM to generate features and predict phenotypes, without describing its own generative mechanism; therefore it is an application wrapper rather than a primary generative model.",EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:1888:1421,How to improve polygenic prediction from whole-genome sequencing data by leveraging predicted epigenomic features?,how to improve polygenic prediction from wholegenome sequencing data by leveraging predicted epigenomic features,10.1101/2024.10.04.24314860,,,,"Polygenic risk scores (PRS) are crucial in genetics for predicting individual susceptibility to complex diseases by aggregating the effects of numerous genetic variants. Whole-genome sequencing (WGS) has revolutionized our ability to detect rare and even de novo variants, creating an exciting opportunity for developing new PRS methods that can effectively leverage rare variants and capture the complex relationships among different variants. Furthermore, regulatory mechanisms play a crucial role in gene expression and disease manifestation, offering avenues to further enhance the performance and interpretation of PRS predictions. Through simulation studies, we highlighted aspects where current PRS methods face challenges when applied to WGS data, aiming to shed light on potential opportunities for further improvement. To address these challenges, we developed Epi-PRS, an approach that leverages the power of genomic large language models (LLM) to impute epigenomic signals across diverse cellular contexts, for use as intermediate variables between genotype and phenotype. A pretrained LLM is employed to transform genotypes into epigenomic signals using personal diploid sequences as inputs, and the genetic risk is then estimated based on the imputed personal epigenomic signals. Epi-PRS enhances the assessment of personal variant impacts, enabling a comprehensive and holistic consideration of genotypic and regulatory information within large genomic regions. Our simulation results demonstrated that incorporating the nuanced effects of non-linear models, rare variants, and regulatory information can provide more precise PRS prediction and better understanding of genetic risk. Applying Epi-PRS to real data from the UK Biobank, our results further showed that Epi-PRS significantly outperforms existing PRS methods in two major diseases: breast cancer and diabetes. This study suggests that PRS methods can benefit from incorporating non-linear models, rare variants, and regulatory information, highlighting the potential for significant advancements in disease risk modeling and enhancing the understanding of precision medicine. Significance Statement Epi-PRS improves polygenic risk scoring by integrating genomic large language models (LLMs) to impute epigenomic signals as intermediaries between genotype and phenotype. This approach enables a more comprehensive assessment of personal variant impacts by incorporating non-linear models, rare variants, and regulatory mechanisms. By leveraging the power of genomic LLM trained on massive amount of reference epigenomics data, Epi-PRS has demonstrated superior performance over existing PRS methods in predicting genetic risk for breast cancer and diabetes in UK Biobank data. These results highlight the potential of Epi-PRS to improve disease risk modeling and advance the field of precision medicine.",W. Zeng; H. Guo; Q. Liu; W. Wong,2024.0,medRxiv,2024-10-06,https://www.medrxiv.org/content/medrxiv/early/2024/10/06/2024.10.04.24314860.full.pdf,"['biorxiv_medrxiv', 'semantic_scholar']",2,['10.1101/2024.10.04.24314860'],2,,,,,1301,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC2_no_text_component"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents a new PRS method using a genomic LLM to impute epigenomic signals, but it contains no mention of natural-language text or a text‑bio mapping, so it lacks a substantive text component and bridge.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,EC2_no_text_component,none,none,none,not_assessed,clear_exclude,"The abstract presents a new PRS method using a genomic LLM to impute epigenomic signals, but it contains no mention of natural-language text or a text‑bio mapping, so it lacks a substantive text component and bridge.","{""paper_type"": ""application_wrapper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""yes"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""application_wrapper"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""A pretrained LLM is employed to transform genotypes into epigenomic signals"", ""boundary_case"": ""wrapper_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""Epi-PRS uses an existing pretrained genomic LLM to generate epigenomic signals for PRS, representing a wrapper around a generative model rather than a novel primary model.""}",application_wrapper,yes,yes,application_wrapper,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,A pretrained LLM is employed to transform genotypes into epigenomic signals,wrapper_boundary,"Epi-PRS uses an existing pretrained genomic LLM to generate epigenomic signals for PRS, representing a wrapper around a generative model rather than a novel primary model.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,True,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""generative_model_present"": ""yes"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC2_no_text_component"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""genomic large language models (LLM) to impute epigenomic signals"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""genomic large language models (LLM) to impute epigenomic signals"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""impute epigenomic signals"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes a genomic LLM used to predict epigenomic signals from DNA, with no natural‑language text input or output, so there is no text component or text‑bio bridge; thus the paper is excluded under EC2_no_text_component.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,yes,yes,EC2_no_text_component,none,genomic large language models (LLM) to impute epigenomic signals,genomic large language models (LLM) to impute epigenomic signals,impute epigenomic signals,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a genomic LLM used to predict epigenomic signals from DNA, with no natural‑language text input or output, so there is no text component or text‑bio bridge; thus the paper is excluded under EC2_no_text_component.",EXCLUDE|EXCLUDE,primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,yes,yes,EC2_no_text_component,,genomic large language models (LLM) to impute epigenomic signals,genomic large language models (LLM) to impute epigenomic signals,impute epigenomic signals,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a genomic LLM used to predict epigenomic signals from DNA, with no natural‑language text input or output, so there is no text component or text‑bio bridge; thus the paper is excluded under EC2_no_text_component.",EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:2257:1565,MetagenBERT: a Transformer-based Architecture using Foundational genomic Large Language Models for novel Metagenome Representation,metagenbert a transformerbased architecture using foundational genomic large language models for novel metagenome representation,,,,2601.03295v1,"Metagenomic disease prediction commonly relies on species abundance tables derived from large, incomplete reference catalogs, constraining resolution and discarding valuable information contained in DNA reads. To overcome these limitations, we introduce MetagenBERT, a Transformer based framework that produces end to end metagenome embeddings directly from raw DNA sequences, without taxonomic or functional annotations. Reads are embedded using foundational genomic language models (DNABERT2 and the microbiome specialized DNABERTMS), then aggregated through a scalable clustering strategy based on FAISS accelerated KMeans. Each metagenome is represented as a cluster abundance vector summarizing the distribution of its embedded reads. We evaluate this approach on five benchmark gut microbiome datasets (Cirrhosis, T2D, Obesity, IBD, CRC). MetagenBERT achieves competitive or superior AUC performance relative to species abundance baselines across most tasks. Concatenating both representations further improves prediction, demonstrating complementarity between taxonomic and embedding derived signals. Clustering remains robust when applied to as little as 10% of reads, highlighting substantial redundancy in metagenomes and enabling major computational gains. We additionally introduce MetagenBERT Glob Mcardis, a cross cohort variant trained on the large, phenotypically diverse MetaCardis cohort and transferred to other datasets, retaining predictive signal including for unseen phenotypes, indicating the feasibility of a foundation model for metagenome representation. Robustness analyses (PERMANOVA, PERMDISP, entropy) show consistent separation of different states across subsamples. Overall, MetagenBERT provides a scalable, annotation free representation of metagenomes pointing toward future phenotype aware generalization across heterogeneous cohorts and sequencing technologies.",Gaspar Roy; Eugeni Belda; Baptiste Hennecart; Y. Chevaleyre; E. Prifti; Jean-Daniel Zucker,2026.0,,2026-01-05,,"['arxiv', 'semantic_scholar']",2,[],2,,,,,1444,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents MetagenBERT, a new transformer architecture that processes raw DNA reads via genomic language models, with no mention of natural-language text, prompts, or captions, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,none,none,none,none,not_assessed,none,"The abstract presents MetagenBERT, a new transformer architecture that processes raw DNA reads via genomic language models, with no mention of natural-language text, prompts, or captions, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents MetagenBERT, a Transformer that encodes raw DNA reads using existing genomic LLMs; it does not describe a generative decoder, but it leverages large pretrained models and shows transferability, indicating foundation-model characteristics.""}",primary_model_paper,no,yes,none,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,none,"The abstract presents MetagenBERT, a Transformer that encodes raw DNA reads using existing genomic LLMs; it does not describe a generative decoder, but it leverages large pretrained models and shows transferability, indicating foundation-model characteristics.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,EXCLUDE|EXCLUDE,primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,no,yes,,,,,,,"The abstract presents MetagenBERT, a new transformer architecture that processes raw DNA reads via genomic language models, with no mention of natural-language text, prompts, or captions, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge. | The abstract presents MetagenBERT, a Transformer that encodes raw DNA reads using existing genomic LLMs; it does not describe a generative decoder, but it leverages large pretrained models and shows transferability, indicating foundation-model characteristics.",EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:2404:1608,Universal Single-Cell Transcriptomic Aging Clock powered by LLMs reveals targets to slow cellular aging,universal singlecell transcriptomic aging clock powered by llms reveals targets to slow cellular aging,10.1093/geroni/igaf122.4305,,,,"Abstract Large language models such as GPT have shown impressive performance in capturing structure and meaning from natural language. We adapt this capability to biology by representing single-cell transcriptomes as ordered sequences of gene names, analogous to sentences in text. Each cell’s expression profile is converted into a “cell sentence,” enabling pretrained language models to be fine-tuned directly on single-cell data for age prediction. Trained on millions of cells spanning diverse tissues and life stages, the model learns a universal latent representation of aging. It captures both cell-type–specific trajectories and conserved molecular signatures, generalizing across tissues and species. Unlike other foundational models that must be trained from scratch on omics data, our framework leverages already well-validated LLMs, taking advantage of their robust priors while avoiding the cost and instability of building new architectures. The resulting clock achieves state-of-the-art accuracy in predicting biological age at single-cell resolution. Embeddings highlight aging-associated processes such as immune activation, proteostasis decline, and metabolic shifts, aligning with known hallmarks of aging. Importantly, the model can also identify genes whose modulation decreases predicted transcriptional age, nominating candidate targets for therapeutic intervention. These features make the approach valuable both for measuring aging and for prioritizing interventions that may slow or reverse it. This work establishes the first universal, multi-tissue aging clock at single-cell resolution, demonstrating that pretrained language models can be directly adapted to biological data to map, measure, and potentially modify cellular aging.",Raghav Sehgal,2025.0,Innovation in aging,2025-12-01,,['semantic_scholar'],1,['10.1093/geroni/igaf122.4305'],1,,,,,1486,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""representing single-cell transcriptomes as ordered sequences of gene names, analogous to sentences in text"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""representing single-cell transcriptomes as ordered sequences of gene names... fine‑tuned directly on single-cell data"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The work presents a new model (primary_model_paper) that uses single‑cell transcriptomic data (bio modality). The only text‑like element is gene‑name sequencing, not natural language, so there is no substantive text component nor a natural‑language‑bio bridge.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,none,none,"representing single-cell transcriptomes as ordered sequences of gene names, analogous to sentences in text",representing single-cell transcriptomes as ordered sequences of gene names... fine‑tuned directly on single-cell data,not_assessed,none,"The work presents a new model (primary_model_paper) that uses single‑cell transcriptomic data (bio modality). The only text‑like element is gene‑name sequencing, not natural language, so there is no substantive text component nor a natural‑language‑bio bridge.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes fine‑tuning a pretrained LLM to predict cellular age, with no mention of a generative mechanism; it cites leveraging a large language model, indicating foundation‑model characteristics.""}",primary_model_paper,no,yes,none,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes fine‑tuning a pretrained LLM to predict cellular age, with no mention of a generative mechanism; it cites leveraging a large language model, indicating foundation‑model characteristics.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,EXCLUDE|EXCLUDE,primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,no,yes,,,,,,,"The work presents a new model (primary_model_paper) that uses single‑cell transcriptomic data (bio modality). The only text‑like element is gene‑name sequencing, not natural language, so there is no substantive text component nor a natural‑language‑bio bridge. | The abstract describes fine‑tuning a pretrained LLM to predict cellular age, with no mention of a generative mechanism; it cites leveraging a large language model, indicating foundation‑model characteristics.",EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:2595:1693,TridentTCR: Predicting T Cell Receptor Specificity for Antigens and Autoimmune-Related Targets via a Topology-Aware Graph and Large Language Model,tridenttcr predicting t cell receptor specificity for antigens and autoimmunerelated targets via a topologyaware graph and large language model,10.1101/2025.06.27.661632,,,,"T cells recognize and eliminate diseased cells by binding their T cell receptors (TCRs) to short endogenous peptides presented on the cell surface, commonly referred to as antigens. Accurate prediction of antigen–TCR specificity is essential for advancing T cell-based immunotherapies while minimizing autoimmune toxicity. However, current computational methods struggle to generalize across diverse TCR repertoires and reliably differentiate therapeutic antigens from autoimmune-related antigens (arAgs). Here, we introduce TridentTCR, a computational framework integrating a pretrained large language model (LLM) for high-fidelity sequence embedding with a topology-aware graph neural network to capture structural insights from the antigen–TCR interaction network. TridentTCR significantly advances TCR specificity prediction by enabling robust trinary classification, distinguishing TCR interactions involving general antigens, arAgs, and non-binding. Additionally, TridentTCR outperforms existing state-of-the-art models in binary classification (binding vs. non-binding). Validation on independent clinical datasets demonstrated strong generalization capability for unseen TCRs and antigens. In a disease-specific context, TridentTCR identifies notable cross-reactivity between ZnT8 186-194 -specific TCRs and a mimotope from Bacteroides stercoris , suggesting that T cell cross-reactivity may contribute to the initiation of type 1 diabetes. Furthermore, we introduced a quantitative metric, antigenic immune response entropy (AIRE), which leverages TridentTCR predictions alongside clonotype frequencies and repertoire diversity to precisely quantify antigen-specific immune responses from single-cell profiling data. Collectively, TridentTCR provides an interpretable and clinically relevant tool, enabling improved understanding of TCR specificity, cross-reactivity, and off-target autoimmune risks in clinical immunotherapy.",Rui Niu; Xiaoying Kong; Jiren Zhou; Yanli Li; Xuequn Shang,2025.0,bioRxiv,2025-06-27,,"['biorxiv_medrxiv', 'semantic_scholar']",2,['10.1101/2025.06.27.661632'],2,,,,,1566,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract introduces a new computational framework (TridentTCR) for TCR‑antigen specificity prediction, indicating a primary model paper. It centers on biological sequences (TCRs, antigens) but mentions only a pretrained LLM used for sequence embedding, without natural‑language text, prompts, or documents, so no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge is evident.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,none,none,none,none,not_assessed,none,"The abstract introduces a new computational framework (TridentTCR) for TCR‑antigen specificity prediction, indicating a primary model paper. It centers on biological sequences (TCRs, antigens) but mentions only a pretrained LLM used for sequence embedding, without natural‑language text, prompts, or documents, so no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge is evident.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC3_not_generative"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes TridentTCR as a prediction (trinary/binary classification) framework and mentions no decoder, autoregressive, or other generative mechanism; it only uses a pretrained LLM for embeddings.""}",primary_model_paper,no,yes,EC3_not_generative,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes TridentTCR as a prediction (trinary/binary classification) framework and mentions no decoder, autoregressive, or other generative mechanism; it only uses a pretrained LLM for embeddings.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,EXCLUDE|EXCLUDE,primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,no,yes,,,,,,,"The abstract introduces a new computational framework (TridentTCR) for TCR‑antigen specificity prediction, indicating a primary model paper. It centers on biological sequences (TCRs, antigens) but mentions only a pretrained LLM used for sequence embedding, without natural‑language text, prompts, or documents, so no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge is evident. | The abstract describes TridentTCR as a prediction (trinary/binary classification) framework and mentions no decoder, autoregressive, or other generative mechanism; it only uses a pretrained LLM for embeddings.",EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:3132:1969,Cell2Text: Multimodal LLM for Generating Single-Cell Descriptions from RNA-Seq Data,cell2text multimodal llm for generating singlecell descriptions from rnaseq data,10.48550/arXiv.2509.24840,,,2509.24840,"Single-cell RNA sequencing has transformed biology by enabling the measurement of gene expression at cellular resolution, providing information for cell types, states, and disease contexts. Recently, single-cell foundation models have emerged as powerful tools for learning transferable representations directly from expression profiles, improving performance on classification and clustering tasks. However, these models are limited to discrete prediction heads, which collapse cellular complexity into predefined labels that fail to capture the richer, contextual explanations biologists need. We introduce Cell2Text, a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA-seq profiles into structured natural language descriptions. By integrating gene-level embeddings from single-cell foundation models with pretrained large language models, Cell2Text generates coherent summaries that capture cellular identity, tissue origin, disease associations, and pathway activity, generalizing to unseen cells. Empirically, Cell2Text outperforms baselines on classification accuracy, demonstrates strong ontological consistency using PageRank-based similarity metrics, and achieves high semantic fidelity in text generation. These results demonstrate that coupling expression data with natural language offers both stronger predictive performance and inherently interpretable outputs, pointing to a scalable path for label-efficient characterization of unseen cells.",Oussama Kharouiche; Aris Markogiannakis; Xiao Fei; Michail Chatzianastasis; M. Vazirgiannis,2025.0,arXiv.org,2025-09-29,,"['arxiv', 'google_scholar', 'semantic_scholar']",3,['10.48550/arXiv.2509.24840'],3,,,,,1837,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""yes"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""generates coherent summaries ... structured natural language descriptions"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""translates scRNA-seq profiles into structured natural language descriptions"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents Cell2Text as a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA‑seq data into natural‑language descriptions, showing a new primary model with both a biological modality and a substantive text component forming a clear text‑bio bridge.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,none,none,generates coherent summaries ... structured natural language descriptions,translates scRNA-seq profiles into structured natural language descriptions,not_assessed,none,"The abstract presents Cell2Text as a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA‑seq data into natural‑language descriptions, showing a new primary model with both a biological modality and a substantive text component forming a clear text‑bio bridge.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""yes"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA‑seq profiles into structured natural language descriptions"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_include"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents Cell2Text as a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA‑seq data into natural language summaries, showing a generative decoder mechanism, and it leverages gene‑level embeddings from single‑cell foundation models and pretrained LLMs, indicating foundation‑model characteristics.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,yes,none,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA‑seq profiles into structured natural language descriptions,clear_include,"The abstract presents Cell2Text as a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA‑seq data into natural language summaries, showing a generative decoder mechanism, and it leverages gene‑level embeddings from single‑cell foundation models and pretrained LLMs, indicating foundation‑model characteristics.",PASS,PASS,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,PASS|PASS,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,,,"The abstract presents Cell2Text as a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA‑seq data into natural‑language descriptions, showing a new primary model with both a biological modality and a substantive text component forming a clear text‑bio bridge. | The abstract presents Cell2Text as a multimodal generative framework that translates scRNA‑seq data into natural language summaries, showing a generative decoder mechanism, and it leverages gene‑level embeddings from single‑cell foundation models and pretrained LLMs, indicating foundation‑model characteristics.",INCLUDE,,,INCLUDE,,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/gpt_oss_120b_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11550/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:3184:1988,Sequence-to-Temperature: RAG-Enhanced and XAI-Supported OGT Estimation from Archaea tRNA Sequences Using Transformer-Based Embeddings,sequencetotemperature ragenhanced and xaisupported ogt estimation from archaea trna sequences using transformerbased embeddings,10.1109/BIBE66822.2025.00068,,,,"The growth conditions of an organism play a critical role in its biological characterization. However, information regarding these conditions is often unavailable. Predicting the optimal growth temperature based on the physical properties of the genome allows for estimation without the need to culture newly discovered species. In this context, developing methods that infer an organism's ideal growth temperature from genomic sequence data represents an important approach for improving efficiency in both time and resource management. In this study, we propose a novel methodology that leverages the structural parallels between tRNA sequences and natural language through transformerbased models. The approach comprises three main components: (i) generating embedding vectors from tRNA sequences using transformer-based encoders, (ii) storing and retrieving these embeddings within a vector-based database via a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) architecture, and (iii) performing a regression task using a large language model (LLM) based on the retrieved sequences, enhanced with explainable AI techniques to provide interpretable predictions. This framework introduces an alternative and interpretable solution for optimal growth temperature (OGT) estimation, highlighting the utility of RAG architectures in genomic applications.",Ahmet Telçeken; Eyup Cinar,2025.0,International Conferences on Biological Information and Biomedical Engineering,2025-11-06,,['semantic_scholar'],1,['10.1109/BIBE66822.2025.00068'],1,,,,,1856,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract proposes a novel transformer‑based embedding and RAG pipeline to predict optimal growth temperature from tRNA sequences, without describing natural‑language text inputs, prompts, or generation, thus lacking a substantive text component or text‑bio bridge.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,none,none,none,none,not_assessed,none,"The abstract proposes a novel transformer‑based embedding and RAG pipeline to predict optimal growth temperature from tRNA sequences, without describing natural‑language text inputs, prompts, or generation, thus lacking a substantive text component or text‑bio bridge.","{""paper_type"": ""application_wrapper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""application_wrapper"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""wrapper_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The study embeds tRNA sequences with transformer encoders and uses a pretrained LLM for regression via a RAG pipeline, without introducing a new generative architecture; it functions as a wrapper around existing models.""}",application_wrapper,no,yes,application_wrapper,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,wrapper_boundary,"The study embeds tRNA sequences with transformer encoders and uses a pretrained LLM for regression via a RAG pipeline, without introducing a new generative architecture; it functions as a wrapper around existing models.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,True,"{""paper_type"": ""application_wrapper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""application_wrapper"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""no mention of natural language text"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""no natural-language bridge described"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""LLM used for regression, not generation"", ""boundary_case"": ""wrapper_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract proposes a pipeline that uses existing transformer encoders and a large language model for _id":"rec_002232","title":"Advancing immunotherapy for esophageal cancer: decoding the roles of PD-L1, TME, and tumor-intrinsic biomarkers","abstract":"Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma (ESCC) remains a global health challenge, with immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs) reshaping therapeutic strategies. However, heterogeneous responses underscore the urgent need for robust predictive biomarkers. While PD-L1 expression remains the most widely used marker, its limitations, including spatial heterogeneity and inducible expression, have prompted exploration of alternative and composite indicators. Recent advances highlight the predictive potential of tumor immune microenvironment (TME) features such as tumor-infiltrating lymphocytes (TILs), tertiary lymphoid structures (TLSs), stromal maturity, and T cell–inflamed gene expression profiles. Concurrently, tumor-intrinsic biomarkers, including microsatellite instability, tumor mutational burden, neoantigen load, and chromosomal alterations—have shown promise in stratifying immunotherapy responders. Multi-omics approaches, liquid biopsies, and integration of host factors such as gut microbiota are emerging to refine patient selection. This review comprehensively examines evolving biomarkers and therapeutic trials, emphasizing the need for integrative precision strategies to optimize immunotherapy efficacy in ESCC.","doi":"10.3389/fimmu.2025.1679365","year":"2025","venue":"Frontiers in Immunology","sources":["semantic_scholar"]}]
