s","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes jointly training language models on scRNA-seq-derived text datasets and biomedical literature. That supports both a text component and a substantive bio-text bridge.","evidence_snippet":"\"jointly train language models on both information sources\" and \"common, literature-enriched representation\""},{"record_id":"rec_002229","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The title and abstract state this is a review of machine learning methods for heart disease prediction. It is not a primary model paper.","evidence_snippet":"\"This review examines various computational models\" for heart disease prediction"},{"record_id":"rec_002230","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly says this is a systematic review of AutoML with interpretation in healthcare. It synthesizes prior work rather than presenting a primary model.","evidence_snippet":"\"We conducted a systematic review\" of AutoML with interpretation systems for healthcare"},{"record_id":"rec_002231","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract says the paper is a review of human genome assembly, pan-genomes, and pre-trained genomic models. This is not a primary candidate model paper.","evidence_snippet":"\"Therefore, this review focuses on ... pre-trained genomic large language models\""},{"record_id":"rec_002232","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly describes a review of biomarkers and immunotherapy trials in esophageal cancer. It is not a primary model paper.","evidence_snippet":"\"This review comprehensively examines evolving biomarkers and therapeutic trials\""}]}
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0110.stderr.log:1953:./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0141.stdout.log:1:{"results":[{"record_id":"rec_001121","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract centers on creating the MSK-CHORD dataset and infrastructure, not introducing a new candidate foundation model. NLP is used to annotate free-text clinical reports for a resource buildout.","evidence_snippet":"\"Systematic generation of a clinicogenomic harmonized oncologic real-world dataset (MSK-CHORD)\"; \"NLP could automatically annotate\"; \"create a Clinicogenomic, Harmonized Oncologic Real-world Dataset\""},{"record_id":"rec_001122","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a self-supervised model on neuronal population recordings with transcriptomic labels used downstream. The abstract does not describe a substantive text/language component in the candidate model.","evidence_snippet":"\"self-supervised learning based method\"; \"population recordings\"; \"molecular (transcriptomic) labels for downstream supervised tasks\""},{"record_id":"rec_001123","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract presents a gene-based language model and a Transformer trained to produce genome representations. It explicitly frames gene content as language and uses that bridge for model learning.","evidence_snippet":"\"gene-based language model\"; \"Transformer model\"; \"generates whole-genome vector representations\""},{"record_id":"rec_001124","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a multimodal foundation model using pathology images, clinical reports, and genomics. The clinical reports provide a clear text component within the model itself.","evidence_snippet":"\"integrates pathology images, clinical reports, and genomics data\"; \"multimodal foundation model\""},{"record_id":"rec_001125","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The model operates on cancer multi-omics data for survival analysis and does not describe a text or language component. The bridge is between Transformer/VAE modules and omics, not text-bio.","evidence_snippet":"\"robust cancer multi-omics survival analysis\"; \"predict patients’ survival risk using cancer multi-omics data\""},{"record_id":"rec_001126","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"CellHermes explicitly reformulates omics data into question-answer pairs and uses pretrained LLMs. The abstract supports a substantive text-bio bridge as part of the model.","evidence_snippet":"\"integrate multimodal forms of omics data ... through natural language\"; \"question-answer pairs\"; \"pretrained large language models\""},{"record_id":"rec_001127","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes a deep learning framework over drug response, genetics, expression, and CRISPR screens. It does not present a substantive text or language component.","evidence_snippet":"\"learns molecular mechanisms of drug sensitivity and resistance\"; \"integrating genetics, gene expression, and CRISPR screens\""},{"record_id":"rec_001128","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a gene expression language model trained self-supervised on single-cell RNA profiles. The language-model framing and gene-context learning support a text-style modeling component tied to biology.","evidence_snippet":"\"single-cell gene expression language model\"; \"trained across a diversity of cell types\"; \"learn context dependencies between genes\""}]}
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0110.stderr.log:2187:data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0088.response.txt:1:{"results":[{"record_id":"rec_000697","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is a review article, not a primary model paper. It mentions generative adversarial networks and large models, but does not describe a candidate generative foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"“we first review the application of deep learning” and “generative adversarial networks, large models”"},{"record_id":"rec_000698","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes Epi-PRS as a risk-scoring method that leverages an existing genomic LLM to impute epigenomic signals. It does not present a new generative architecture or foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"“improves polygenic risk scoring by integrating genomic large language models”"},{"record_id":"rec_000699","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"FLEX is presented as a framework to adapt pathology foundation models, not as a new generative model itself. The abstract emphasizes generalization and fairness rather than broad pretraining of a candidate foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"“employs a task-specific information bottleneck” and “compatibility with various foundation models”"},{"record_id":"rec_000700","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"ATHENA is a hierarchical graph neural network for personalized classification and subtype discovery. The abstract is predictive/explanatory, not generative, and does not indicate foundation-model-like pretraining or broad applicability.","evidence_snippet":"“developing a hierarchical graph neural network framework” and “personalized classification”"},{"record_id":"rec_000701","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly says this is a review and synthesis of miRNA bioinformatics methods. It mentions generative models and LLMs, but only as topics covered in the review.","evidence_snippet":"“we present a review” and “we explore the integration of generative models and LLMs”"},{"record_id":"rec_000702","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC4_no_foundation_model_evidence","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly states that the method implements a generative model. However, it is a domain-specific atlas-mapping model and does not show foundation-model-like broad pretraining or broad transferability.","evidence_snippet":"“implements a generative model describing the likelihood of data”"},{"record_id":"rec_000703","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"SciLinker is a text-mining pipeline built with pre-trained NER models and a fine-tuned PubMedBERT extractor. The abstract describes an applied extraction framework, not a new generative foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"“constructed an efficient and modular natural language processing pipeline” and “fine-tuned PubMedBERT model”"},{"record_id":"rec_000704","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a predictive skin-cancer detection system using genetic algorithms and deep neural networks. The abstract does not describe a generative architecture or foundation-model-style pretraining.","evidence_snippet":"“predictive skin cancer detection” and “deep neural networks”"}]}
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0110.stderr.log:2252:{"results":[{"record_id":"rec_002225","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract centers on single-cell RNA/transcriptomics with graph-aware attention and no substantive text/language component. It also does not describe a generative mechanism.","evidence_snippet":"\"a foundation model\" for \"single cell RNA data\" with \"gene token graph structure\" embedded in attention"},{"record_id":"rec_002228","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes jointly training language models on scRNA-seq-derived text datasets and biomedical literature, which supports a substantive text-bio bridge. It does not explicitly state a clearly generative architecture, so generative status remains unclear.","evidence_snippet":"\"jointly train language models on both information sources\" and \"common, literature-enriched representation\""},{"record_id":"rec_002230","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly states this is a systematic review of AutoML with interpretation in healthcare. It synthesizes prior work rather than presenting a primary generative model.","evidence_snippet":"\"We conducted a systematic review\" of AutoML with interpretation systems for healthcare"},{"record_id":"rec_002231","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract says this paper is a review of genome assembly, pan-genomes, and pre-trained genomic models. It summarizes the field rather than reporting a new model.","evidence_snippet":"\"Therefore, this review focuses on ... pre-trained genomic large language models\""},{"record_id":"rec_002237","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The study is a proteomics and multiplexed-immunofluorescence biomarker analysis, with a proprietary deep-learning cell-classification platform used as an analysis tool. No text component or generative model is described.","evidence_snippet":"\"unbiased proteomics and multiplexed immunofluorescence\" and \"proprietary, deep-learning based cell classification platform NeoLYTX\""},{"record_id":"rec_002240","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"ATLAS uses attention signals from pretrained genomic language models for downstream locus discovery rather than proposing a new generative model. The abstract frames it as a framework built on existing gLMs.","evidence_snippet":"\"leverages attention signals from pretrained genomic language models\"; \"without requiring explicit variant calls or supervised training\""}]}
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0172.stderr.log:599:./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0279.prompt.txt:47:[{"record_id":"rec_002225","title":"GREmLN: A Cellular Graph Structure Aware Transcriptomics Foundation Model","abstract":"A bstract The ever-increasing availability of large-scale single-cell profiles presents an opportunity to develop foundation models to capture cell properties and behavior. However, standard language models such as transformers benefits from sequentially structured data with well defined absolute or relative positional relationships, while single cell RNA data have orderless gene features. Molecular-interaction graphs, such as gene regulatory networks (GRN) or protein-protein interaction (PPI) networks, offer graph structure-based models that effectively encode both non-local gene token dependencies, as well as potential causal relationships. We introduce GREmLN ( G ene R egulatory Em bedding-based L arge N eural model), a foundation model that leverages graph signal processing to embed gene token graph structure directly within its attention mechanism, producing biologically informed single cell specific gene embeddings. Our model faithfully captures transcriptomics landscapes and achieves superior performance relative to state-of- the-art baselines on cell type annotation, graph structure understanding, and finetuned reverse perturbation prediction tasks. It offers a unified and interpretable framework for learning high-capacity foundational representations that capture complex, long-range regulatory dependencies from high-dimensional single-cell transcriptomic data. Moreover, the incorporation of graph-structured inductive biases enables more parameter-efficient architectures and accelerates training convergence.","doi":"10.1101/2025.07.03.663009","year":"2025","venue":"bioRxiv","sources":["biorxiv_medrxiv","semantic_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_002226","title":"M-STGCN: A Position-Aware Multimodal Graph Convolutional Framework for Joint Spatial Domain Identification and Gene Expression Denoising.","abstract":"Spatially transcriptomics (ST) has revolutionized our ability to profile gene expression within the architectural complexity of tissue microenvironments. However, decoding spatial heterogeneity requires robust multimodal data integration that unifies gene expression, spatial positions, and histopathological images to overcome modality-specific biases. Here, we propose M-STGCN, a multimodal unsupervised framework that constructs a spatial weight matrix from spatial coordinates to simultaneously refine both gene expression profiles and image features, by which we establish position-aware feature enhancement served as a core innovation before graph fusion. Verified on human brain and breast cancer datasets, M-STGCN significantly improves the accuracy of spatial domain identification. Ablation studies confirm the importance of its position-aware and image modality integration. For ST platforms lacking images and at diverse resolutions, M-STGCN maintains robust performance utilizing only gene expression and spatial coordinates. By effectively denoising raw spatial transcriptomic profiles, our approach identifies more significant spatial domain marker genes, as well as potential prognostic biomarkers for breast cancer. Moreover, M-STGCN reveals that image features and spatial information contribute equally to breast cancer analysis, underscoring the critical role of image features. As a versatile and scalable tool, M-STGCN enables unbiased integration of multimodal data, facilitating the deciphering of spatial heterogeneous in complex tissues.","doi":"10.1002/smtd.202501812","year":"2026","venue":"Small Methods","sources":["semantic_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_002227","title":"HMSP: Hierarchical Multimodal Semantic Fusion of Pathology-Text-Genomic Representations for Robust Survival Prediction","abstract":"Computational pathology has become an indispensable component of cancer survival analysis, yet state-of-the-art multimodal pipelines still depend heavily on paired whole-slide images (WSIs) and in situ genomic or immunohistochemistry (IHC) profiles. In practice, however, IHC and high-throughput sequencing are costly, tissue-consuming, and frequently unavailable, creating a critical performance gap for cases lacking molecular data. To bridge this gap, we introduce HMSP (Hierarchical Multimodal Survival Prediction), a framework that leverages richly structured pathology reports as a flexible linguistic surrogate for missing genomic information. Concretely, HMSP formulates WSIs, free-text pathology narratives, and (when present) genomic features as a three-level hierarchy of visual, lexical, and molecular semantics. We enforce hierarchical consistency with a Tri-Modal Ranking Contrastive Loss and a Cone-Based Hyperbolic (H-Cone) regulariser, enabling (i) fine-grained alignment between image regions and report phrases and (ii) high-level alignment between report embeddings and genomic biomarkers. Once trained, the report encoder alone can act as a plug-in genomic proxy, allowing robust survival prediction even when molecular assays are absent. Extensive experiments on five public cohorts demonstrate that HMSP not only surpasses previous multimodal baselines under full-data settings, but also maintains state-of-the-art performance when genomic channels are wholly ablated—highlighting the practicality of text-driven surrogates in real-world resource-constrained scenarios. Code and pretrained weights will be released upon publication.","doi":"10.1109/MedAI67139.2025.00013","year":"2025","venue":"2025 IEEE International conference on Medical Artificial Intelligence (MedAI)","sources":["semantic_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_002228","title":"Adding layers of information to scRNA-seq data using pre-trained language models","abstract":"Abstract\n                Pre-trained language models promise to enrich analyses of single-cell data with additional layers of information leveraging large text corpora. Yet, it is still unclear how to achieve optimal alignment with the primary quantitative single-cell data. To address this, we construct text-based training datasets from both scRNA-seq data and biomedical literature targeted to the experimental setting at hand. We then jointly train language models on both information sources to learn a common, literature-enriched representation. Our examples on functionality, disease associations, and temporal trajectories show the potential of knowledge-augmented embeddings as a generalizable and interpretable strategy for enriching single-cell analysis pipelines.","doi":"10.1101/2025.08.23.671699","year":"2025","venue":"bioRxiv","sources":["google_scholar","semantic_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_002229","title":"Predicting heart disease using machine learning: A review","abstract":"Heart disease remains the leading cause of global mortality, necessitating precise and nuanced detection strategies. Recent advancements in machine learning and deep learning have significantly enhanced predictive capabilities for heart disease, utilizing diverse patient data such as electrocardiograms, historical diagnoses, and genomic information. This review examines various computational modelsincluding decision trees, support vector machines, and neural networksassessing their applications, strengths, and limitations in heart disease prediction. Despite these advancements, challenges persist, including algorithmic safety, ethical considerations, and the need for prospective clinical validation. Future research should focus on integrating multimodal data, enhancing model interpretability, and addressing privacy concerns to further improve predictive accuracy and clinical utility.","doi":"10.54254/2755-2721/71/20241634","year":"2024","venue":"Applied and Computational Engineering","sources":["semantic_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_002230","title":"Automated machine learning with interpretation: A systematic review of methodologies and applications in healthcare","abstract":"Machine learning (ML) has achieved substantial success in performing healthcare tasks in which the configuration of every part of the ML pipeline relies heavily on technical knowledge. To help professionals with borderline expertise to better use ML techniques, Automated ML (AutoML) has emerged as a prospective solution. However, most models generated by AutoML are black boxes that are challenging to comprehend and deploy in healthcare settings. We conducted a systematic review to examine AutoML with interpretation systems for healthcare. We searched four databases (MEDLINE, EMBASE, Web of Science, and Scopus) complemented with seven prestigious ML conferences (AAAI, ACL, ICLR, ICML, IJCAI, KDD, and NeurIPS) that reported AutoML with interpretation for healthcare before September 1, 2023. We included 118 articles related to AutoML with interpretation in healthcare. First, we illustrated AutoML techniques used in the included publications, including automated data preparation, automated feature engineering, and automated model development, accompanied by a real‐world case study to demonstrate the advantages of AutoML over classic ML. Then, we summarized interpretation methods: feature interaction and importance, data dimensionality reduction, intrinsically interpretable models, and knowledge distillation and rule extraction. Finally, we detailed how AutoML with interpretation has been used for six major data types: image, free text, tabular data, signal, genomic sequences, and multi‐modality. To some extent, AutoML with interpretation provides effortless development and improves users' trust in ML in healthcare settings. In future studies, researchers should explore automated data preparation, seamless integration of automation and interpretation, compatibility with multi‐modality, and utilization of foundation models.","doi":"10.1002/med4.75","year":"2024","venue":"Medicine Advances","sources":["semantic_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_002231","title":"Advances in Human Genome Resolution: The Role of Pan-Genomic Strategies and Fine-Tuning Pre-trained Genomic Models","abstract":"The groundbreaking theory of DNA double helix structure has greatly promoted the development of molecular genetics, shaping and refining the genetic central dogma, thus enabling researchers to explore genotype-phenotype regulation at different levels. In particular, with the continued advancement of third-generation sequencing technology, an increasing number of highly accurate human genomes have been assembled, such as T2T-CHM13 and HG002. These high-quality genome sequences not only provide a more comprehensive human reference sequence, but also enable functional genomics studies within a unified coordinate system. To better explore and resolve the complex genetic information encompassed within human genome sequences, scientists have proposed novel research strategies, involving graphical pan-genome and pre-trained genomic models. The graphical pan-genomes provide population- level high-quality references, revealing the genomic diversity within populations and exploring the sequence complexity of specific regions, such as the KIR immune region. Concurrently, related studies of pre-trained models within the human genome offer new perspectives for interpreting sequence functions and delving into the hidden genetic codes, potentially leading to complete DNA decoding. Overall, graphical pan-genome and pre-trained genomic models represent two crucial strategies in genomics research, which will provide more new insights and make greater breakthroughs in the human genome. Together, these approaches have deepened our understanding of the human genome, fostered the development of bioinformatics ecosystems, and will contribute to the establishment and improvement of the entire field. Therefore, this review focuses on DNA sequencing, human genome assembly, high-quality pan-genome and pre-trained genomic large language models (LLMs), highlighting and summarizing the latest achievements and progress in human genome research, discussing existing challenges and providing future perspectives.","doi":"10.18103/mra.v12i7.5571","year":"2024","venue":"Medical Research Archives","sources":["semantic_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_002232","title":"Advancing immunotherapy for esophageal cancer: decoding the roles of PD-L1, TME, and tumor-intrinsic biomarkers","abstract":"Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma (ESCC) remains a global health challenge, with immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs) reshaping therapeutic strategies. However, heterogeneous responses underscore the urgent need for robust predictive biomarkers. While PD-L1 expression remains the most widely used marker, its limitations, including spatial heterogeneity and inducible expression, have prompted exploration of alternative and composite indicators. Recent advances highlight the predictive potential of tumor immune microenvironment (TME) features such as tumor-infiltrating lymphocytes (TILs), tertiary lymphoid structures (TLSs), stromal maturity, and T cell–inflamed gene expression profiles. Concurrently, tumor-intrinsic biomarkers, including microsatellite instability, tumor mutational burden, neoantigen load, and chromosomal alterations—have shown promise in stratifying immunotherapy responders. Multi-omics approaches, liquid biopsies, and integration of host factors such as gut microbiota are emerging to refine patient selection. This review comprehensively examines evolving biomarkers and therapeutic trials, emphasizing the need for integrative precision strategies to optimize immunotherapy efficacy in ESCC.","doi":"10.3389/fimmu.2025.1679365","year":"2025","venue":"Frontiers in Immunology","sources":["semantic_scholar"]}]
